Unit rules:

- **Kindergarten unit 1 assumes nothing.** Its readiness item checks only what a five-year-old arrives with
  (naming a classmate, taking a turn), and no later unit is assumed by it.
- **Cases stay in school, friend, family-chore, sport and online settings.** No invented case names self-harm, a
  diagnosis, dating, sex or a substance. The units that sit next to health, every grade 6 to 12 unit that teaches
  SM2 (coping with stress) or RS6 (saying no to pressure) first (among them "Stress and strong feelings", "Under
  pressure", "Stress and coping", "Saying no, setting limits", "Help, support and pressure" and "Steady under
  change"), name the health unit in the teacher notes and hand those topics to it.
- **Grade 10 unit 4, "Saying no, setting limits"** (retitled from "Healthy boundaries", Cory, 2026-11-21), so the
  title does not read as dating or sexual health.

Placement notes, by sub-skill, where the grade matters most:


- **SA6 (own snap judgements) and SO6 (norms that have been unfair)** start in K to 2 as fairness
  at play ("everyone can join"), and reach unfair rules of the past in grade 5 and later, taught from settled facts
  (see section 5). In grades 3 and 4 SO6 is a rule in a class, club or game that leaves someone out, on invented
  cases; SO8 is how a school's or a town's rules and signs shape what people do. Grade 7 ("Snap judgements") and grade 10 ("Culture and respect") are their main years.
- **SA8 (ability grows)** is taught as "practice and help make you better", never as a promise that belief alone
  raises grades: the evidence for short mindset lessons is mixed (lens, sources; from memory).
- **SO8 (systems shape behaviour)** starts as "why our class has rules" and grows to media and online norms in grade
  6 and to communities and systems in grade 12.
- **RS6 (saying no to pressure)** is taught as a skill on invented cases. The topics where pressure matters most in
  6 to 12 (substances, sex, dating violence) are health's; SEL rehearses the refusal skill and points to the health
  unit by name in the teacher notes.
- **Role play of refusal and standing up (RS6, RS9).** The teacher or a card plays the pressure or the unkind act; no
  student plays a person who pressures or is unkind.
- **SA1 and SA2 (groups you belong to, strengths from your culture)** use an invented character first. A child may share
  or make one up; a family or culture is never asked about by name in a check.
- **RS9 (speaking up for others)** stays inside school life: an upstander in bullying or exclusion, a student who
  gets help for a friend. It is not a cause the course picks (section 5).
- **Transition years** carry the theme: grade 5 "Ready for change", grade 6 "New school, new me", grade 9 "Finding
  my footing", grade 12 "Leading and leaving".

## 4. The deeper review of the design half (Cory, 2026-11-11: "mark this for a deeper review")

Read in full for this review: the criteria paper, pp 1 to 3 and 20 to 35 (design criteria, design updates, implementation
criteria, final designation). Already on file: the EEF primary SEL guidance (six recommendations, SAFE),
Durlak and others 2011 and Cipriano and others 2023 (abstracts only), Oregon's transformative SEL tool
(docs/reviews/bar-other-subjects-2026-11-11.md).

**What the paper's design half asks, against our bar (ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS part 9):**

| Paper (p) | What it asks, in our words | Our bar item | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| 23 | School-based, for every student, in school hours | 3 | Covered |
| 23 | All five competencies shown in the curriculum | 1 | Covered; section 3 goes further (all 40 sub-skills a year) |
| 23 | A sequence for each grade, or guidance for running it over several years | 2 | Covered by section 3 |
| 23 to 24 | One or more of four approaches: free-standing lessons, teaching practices, SEL inside academic subjects, school-wide structures | none | Ours is free-standing lessons; see gap A |
| 24 | Real practice: self-management and relationship skills by active rehearsal, the other three by reflection | 5 | Covered |
| 24 | SAFE: sequenced, active, focused, explicit | 4, 5, 6 | Covered |
| 24 | Lower rating for missing competencies, band-only lessons, too little practice | Fails if | Covered |
| 25 | Settings rated (classroom, school, family, community) as minimal, adequate or extensive | 7 (in part) | Gap B |
| 25 | Number of sessions a year reported; tools to monitor implementation and student behaviour | none | Gap C |
| 26 to 30 | Equity strategies: translation beyond English; students as agents of change; understanding context and working with bias (adult side) | none | Gap D |
| 27 to 29, 31 to 34 | Adult SEL and training: active, modeled, with feedback, building adults' own competencies | none | Gap E |
| 35 | Final rating is the lower of evidence and design | 8, "Unsupported" | Stated; the evidence half is unmet by design |

**Gaps and additions.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-sel-eld): all five are added to
ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS part 9 (items 9 to 13).

- **A. Approaches.** Free-standing lessons are the approach the registry most often rates; SEL inside academic
  courses is also counted. Addition: keep free-standing courses (decided), with one line in the bar: each SEL
  unit names one link to that grade's core courses (an ELA discussion routine, a social studies civics row) that
  the teacher may use. This is a lean, not a strand.
- **B. Settings.** Bar item 7 asks for a way to carry the skill beyond the lesson. Addition: make it concrete. Each
  unit has a "carry it out" line for the school day (a cue the teacher uses outside the session) and a family
  activity in the family note. Community is optional.
- **C. Sessions and monitoring.** Addition: the scope and sequence states sessions a year (it will, section 2). For
  monitoring, promise 2 rules out tracking students; a paper fidelity checklist for the teacher (did the session
  run as planned, which part was cut) and the class record sheet of skill checks meet the purpose without
  student data. Behaviour ratings of individual children are not collected; the class sheet marks a step seen on one named day, not the child.
- **D. Equity strategies.** Translation: family notes in English and Spanish; student pages English only for now
  (section 2). Students as agents of change: under the values rule, SEL
  gives student voice in class and school life (class norms, a school improvement the class proposes and carries
  out), and leaves the analysis of causes of inequity to social studies, where it is taught from sources and
  evidence. Working with bias: SA6 is taught as noticing and checking one's own snap judgements on invented cases,
  never as assigning guilt or innocence to a group. This is the "middle line" ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS sets for C12.
- **E. Adult SEL.** Professional learning is none for now (decided 2026-11-11). Addition: each unit's teacher page
  opens with a short "before you teach" note: the skill as an adult uses it, and one thing to model. Not training,
  and not claimed as such.

**Verdict of the review.** The design half is a sound bar for these courses: it is specific (SAFE, practice by
doing, grade sequence, all competencies) and checkable on paper. It is not enough alone in five places (A to E), and the
additions close them within the promises. The evidence half stays unmet and is said so on every course page (bar
item 8): no study has tried these courses. The registry rates a program by the lower of the two halves, so
Curio Class must never call its SEL courses "evidence-based" or use the registry's rating words.

## 5. Sensitive ground: FOUNDATION's promises and the values rule

SEL touches family, identity and mental health, so these rules hold in every SEL unit, read by the fresh
sensitivity agent on every unit plan and day and at the Finish check (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
"When these reads run"; FOUNDATION promise 7, BASELINE rules 5, 6 and 8).

**The values rule, in order** (Cory, DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12, as social studies applies it): (a) where a court has
ruled, side with the higher court; (b) facts and backed sources decide claims; (c) people are taught with dignity,
respect, kindness and openness, and we do not hide humanity (families of every kind appear where families appear,
LGBTQ families included, as in social studies); (d) grade level and life experience set how and when a subject is
taught. FOUNDATION's promises still bind.

Applied to SEL:

1. **Nothing personal is collected or scored.** Promise 2 (no tracking, no student analytics). A journal or
   reflection stays with the student; the teacher may read it only if the student offers it. Checks score a skill
   (naming the steps of a calming routine, choosing a fair next step in an invented case), never a feeling, a
   trait or a home life. No student surveys about feelings, family or mental health.
   Four sub-skills are about the inside of the child (SA3, SA4, SA7, SA9). Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21): each is checked as a skill on
   an invented case or a plan the student keeps. SA3: name a character's feeling and the clue. SA4: choose the true
   action in a case and say what it costs. SA7: name two steps a character could take and one place to ask for help.
   SA9: a private list the student keeps, checked only for "the steps were followed", never for its content. The course
   never records a student's own answer to SA7 or SA9.
2. **No disclosure is asked for.** Prompts use invented characters and cases, or "a time you choose to share, or
   make one up". No activity asks a child to rank, sort or reveal their identity, family situation or past harm
   (no privilege walks, no "step forward if" games).
3. **Role play stays safe.** Students rehearse a skill in an invented, low-stakes moment. No role play that puts a
   child in a victim's or perpetrator's place, or casts a real classmate (the same rule as social studies). Where a skill needs pressure to rehearse against (saying no,
   speaking up), the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays a person who pressures or is unkind.
4. **SEL is not therapy.** No diagnosis, no clinical words used of a child, no claim that a course treats anxiety or
   depression. Calming routines are breathing, movement and pause, taught as plain body skills with no religious or
   spiritual framing. Each unit's teacher notes carry health's steps for when a child discloses harm or risk
   (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something": the school's duty to report and the
   counselor, and the home route for a parent teaching one child). Suicide prevention, substances and sexual health belong to health.
5. **Hard facts plain, at the right age.** SO6 "norms that have been unfair" uses settled history the courts have
   ruled on (school segregation struck down in 1954 is the model case) and facts, not opinion; current political
   disputes are not SEL content. Grade sets the case: playground fairness in K to 2, unfair rules of the past from
   grade 5.
6. **Families.** Every family note says what the unit teaches, the words it uses and how to practice at home, in
   English and Spanish. Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21): a school may leave out an SEL unit (as with health's sensitive units),
   the school is told which sub-skills that year then does not teach, a later unit never assumes the left-out one (its readiness item rests on the previous year's row for that sub-skill, or, if that unit was left out too,
   the nearest earlier year that was taught, and checks only the one-step form), and the course page and the unit's
   family note say the course does not claim those sub-skills that year ("Acceptability is local",
   ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS), naming the ids, the same leave-out rule as arts (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-sel-1). Where a later unit uses a card the
   left-out unit made (section 7, "Shared steps"), its first Understanding session teaches the card's one-step form in
   its first ten minutes and claims none of the left-out sub-skills; each band frame lists those bridges.
7. **No product names, no rating words.** No program, app or registry name in public copy; no claim of being
   reviewed, approved, "evidence-based" or rated.

## 6. Left out, and why

- **The registry's school-wide and adult parts** (organizational strategies, staff SEL, coaching): Curio Class
  makes classroom courses and offers no professional learning for now (decided 2026-11-11). Section 4, E is the
  small stand-in.
- **Measuring children's SEL** with rating scales or screeners: promise 2, and such measures are for schools and
  counselors, not curriculum.
- **Mental health topics** (disorders, crisis, suicide prevention) and risk topics (substances, sexual health,
  dating violence): health (NHES 2024 and the HECAT topics), with SEL teaching the underlying skills.
- **Informed action and analysis of systems** past what a grade's sub-skills need: social studies.
- **Pre-kindergarten**: outside the course list.

## 7. Leans on other subjects

- **Health (NHES 2024, data/standards/nhes-2024.txt).** NHES S4 (interpersonal communication), S5 (decision making)
  and S6 (goal setting) overlap RS1, RS5, RS6, RD2 to RD4 and SM4. Rule: SEL teaches the skill on everyday cases;
  health applies it to health topics. When both courses run in a grade, the health frame names the SEL unit as
  "builds on" and does not reteach the steps. Where health teaches a shared card first in a year, the SEL unit opens
  with it as retrieval where the school runs health, and teaches it where it does not; each band frame names the
  units and weeks.
- **Shared steps (SEL, health and PE; 2026-11-25, from the K to 2 frame reads).** One set of words, so a child meets
  one card in every course. Calm-down card: "stop, breathe" (the balloon breath) first, then count to five (grade 1),
  then a stretch or asking for a break, chosen by the moment (grade 2); PE's "three slow breaths, count to five" uses
  the same picture. I-statement: "I feel ___ when ___. I need ___." in K to 2 (health kindergarten's words); grade 2
  adds asking "What do you need?" and a fair end; grades 3 to 5 add "because" (I feel, when, because, I need).
  Refusal second push: say it again, move away, tell (health's boundary steps). Goal steps card: my goal and one step;
  then a helper and a check; then a changed step; used by health's goal plans and PE's goal cycles. Talk turn card:
  its two steps open health's listening card. Changes on the health and PE side are the lead's.
- **ELA (data/progressions/ela.csv).** The discussion rows (ek-discussion to e5-discussion, e6-collab-discussion to
  e8-collab-discussion, e9-sl-discussion, e10-sl-seminar, e11-seminar and e12-seminar) carry turn-taking, listening and building on
  others' ideas. SEL's RS1 uses the same norms and words, so a class meets one set of talk rules.
- **Social studies (data/progressions/social-studies.csv).** ssk-rules-why, ssk-talk-together, ss1-group-rules and
  ss1-community-problems overlap SO8, RS1 and RD3 in K and 1. SEL takes the personal side (how I act in a group);
  social studies the civic side (why a community makes rules). The K and 1 SEL frames cite those rows as
  context links.
- **Library and research skills (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4).** Library places three AASL ids in SEL,
  taught in every band: V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard) with SM3, V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and
  help) with SA8, and III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job) with RS4. Library research units use these
  sub-skills and do not teach them first (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1).
- These leans are noted here for the frames; the core progressions are not edited.
# The Curio Class approach to teaching social and emotional learning

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-21 (Desk q-prework-open-1), with the cold read's fixes in (docs/reviews/prework-sel-read-2026-11-19.md). Written 2026-11-16.** Short by design. It fills the subject slots of the unit plan and the section plan
(docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) for SEL. The standard, the grade sequence and the sensitive-ground rules are in
[STANDARD.md](STANDARD.md); the lens is docs/agents/lenses/sel.md.

## What a good SEL session does

- **Teaches one skill by name, with steps.** "Stop, breathe, name it" is a skill; "be kind" is not. The skill has a
  name the class keeps all year, two to four steps (one or two in K to 2, STANDARD section 3), and a reason it helps (explicit: said plainly, not left to be guessed).
- **Shows it, then has every student do it.** The teacher models the skill on an invented case, thinking aloud. Then
  students rehearse it: with a partner, in a short scene with invented characters, or as a body routine. Practice
  by doing is the core of the session, not an extra (bar item 5).
- **Uses invented cases.** A character in a short story, a picture, a scene card. Students may bring a moment of their
  own only if they choose, and may make one up. Nothing personal is asked for or scored (STANDARD section 5).
- **Carries it out of the session.** Each unit names a cue the teacher uses during the school day ("try your stop
  and breathe") and a family activity in the family note.
- **Comes back.** Each later unit brings earlier skills back in short practice (spaced practice, C7).

## Kinds of day in SEL (the GLOSSARY kinds)

| Kind | What it is in SEL |
|---|---|
| Understanding | Name the skill, see it modeled on an invented case, say why it helps and when to use it. |
| Fluency | Rehearse the skill until the steps come easily: partner rehearsal, a body routine, sentence frames for an I-statement. |
| Application | Use the skill in a new invented case, a scene card or a planned class moment (a group task where the skill is needed). Student voice sits here: class norms, or a school improvement the class proposes and carries out. |
| Check | The end-of-unit assessment: a rehearsal against a look-for list and scenario items (what would a good next step be, and why). |
| Respond | Return to the skill where the checks showed gaps: reteach the steps, then rehearse again. |

A section is one group of related sub-skills, two or three a unit, three or four sessions on the full year and one
or two on the short path (STANDARD section 2, "How the sessions add up"; Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-sel-1): one
Understanding, then Fluency and Application, often in the same session in K to 2.

## Daily evidence

The day's evidence is **a rehearsal seen against a short look-for list** (did the student do the steps of the skill)
or **one scenario item** on an invented case (name the feeling, pick a fair next step, say why). It is recorded on a
paper class sheet as "seen / not yet", to plan the Respond day. The sheet holds one step seen on one named day, not a
rating of the child; it is the teacher's own working paper, is not shared outside the class and is thrown away at the end
of the unit. It is never a rating of a child's feelings, character or home life, never a survey, and never kept as
student data (promise 2). A private journal page may close a
session; it stays with the student. Once a unit, a self-check page (C8): the student marks which steps of the
unit's skill they can do alone and names the one they will practice next. It checks steps only, never a feeling or
a trait, stays with the student and is not collected.

## What SEL adds to the unit and section plan fields

Proposed, to be tried on the first SEL unit plans (after the core subjects). The shared part of every field stands
as UNIT-PLAN.md writes it; the lines below add only what SEL fills, under UNIT-PLAN.md's numbers.

- Header: sessions by kind; sessions a week and minutes, as the frame states them (pass rule, smaller courses).
  Pacing: as STANDARD section 2.
- Unit field 2: the sub-skill ids the unit teaches first (STANDARD section 3), in our own words; the skill names and
  steps the unit fixes for the year; what is carried (the class's skill names and cue words), the day the model is
  withdrawn and the day the student first does it alone.
- Unit field 3 (where it sits): builds on (the same sub-skill one year before, handoff strong or weak); leads to; comes
  back; and the one link to this grade's core courses a teacher may use (an ELA discussion routine, a social studies
  civics row), named by its row id.
- Unit field 4 (readiness): every unit's sub-skills were used the year before or earlier the same year, so the
  field is `Retrieval only:` (UNIT-PLAN field 4): one scenario item on the same sub-skill at its earlier depth,
  inside the session's five-minute opening (about four minutes in K to 2's 20-minute session), and on a miss the earlier skill modeled again. A `Given:` readiness
  check only where a left-out unit puts the last use two or more grades back (STANDARD section 5, item 6).
  Kindergarten unit 1: `Start-of-year check:` on what a child arrives with (STANDARD section 3).
- Unit field 5 (end-of-unit assessment): a rehearsal with look-fors and scenario items, each teacher-judged item with
  two sample answers. Nothing personal in any item.
- Unit field 6 (error register): the misreadings of the skill (for example "calming down means hiding the feeling",
  "an I-statement is a polite way to blame"), each with its source or "from memory".
- Unit field 7 (response allocation): the rehearsal is judged by the teacher against the look-fors, so nothing is
  marked by hands up. Groups for the Respond day come from the class sheet's "not yet" cells by step, at most four
  groups; the others rehearse in pairs from the step card. Marking time is stated as minutes.
- Unit field 8 (tools and supplies): scene cards, pictures, stories by title and record; no product or program names.
  A tool may show a step card, or invented cases to sort or choose a next step with feedback on the steps. No tool
  collects feelings, answers or names, none scores a child (C15), and none stands in for rehearsal with people
  (bar item 5). The teacher's fidelity checklist is a
  paper page: did the session run as planned, and which part was cut.
- Unit field 9 (family note): in English and Spanish: the skill's name and steps, the words the class uses, and one
  thing to try at home (the family activity). Where a school leaves a unit out, that unit's family note names
  the sub-skill ids the year then does not cover (STANDARD section 5, item 6; Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-sel-1).
- Unit field 10 (sources): a sources table with what was read and how much, what is from memory, no claim of effect,
  what is still to make, and a line pointing to the course-level logic model (C16).
- Section field 1 (what the teacher page must explain), so every unit's teacher pages open with it: before you
  teach: the skill as an adult uses it, and one thing to model (STANDARD section 4, E). The sensitive topics the
  section touches, named, and the steps if a child discloses harm (STANDARD section 5, item 4).
- Section field 2 (where it sits): the section just before and just after in the unit, and the unit's field 4
  retrieval items this section uses.
- Section field 3: the day's evidence as above, with its look-fors.
- Section field 4 (expected errors): each as a wrong example beside a right one (for example an I-statement that
  blames), with the day it is shown and the five to ten minute response if many made it.
- Section field 5 (what is carried): the skill's step card, plus the carry-out cue for the school day. The teacher holds
  the model on the first session; pairs hold the card on the second; the card is withdrawn on the first Application
  session, which the plan names, and the check is taken without it. K to 2 use their one or two steps as
  pictured words said aloud.
- Section field 6: kind of day; how it opens (a calm start routine or a retrieval question on an earlier skill, five
  minutes at most); the invented case used. In K to 5 a new skill is taught before it is tried.
- Section field 7: the talk routine (the same discussion norms as ELA that grade), the sentence frames, the rehearsal
  set-up, and the role-play rule (invented, low-stakes, no victim or perpetrator roles, no real classmates; the
  teacher or a card plays any pressure).
- Section field 8 (practice that returns): one earlier skill, named, rehearsed for three minutes with its card.
- Section field 9 (supports): English learners: six to eight words taught in depth per unit with a picture (feelings
  words and words with two meanings, such as cool down, stand up, pressure, mean), and a sentence frame for each
  step ("I feel ___ when ___. I need ___.", the shared I-statement, STANDARD section 7); the family note's languages are used where it has them. The key
  says: the right step shown in the home language, by pointing or acting, is a language gap; a wrong step is a content
  gap. Access: no student must speak aloud or be watched rehearsing; any rehearsal can be done in writing, with a card
  or with the teacher. Reading load: a scene card is at most five short sentences (K to 2: read aloud, pictured).
  Check accommodations: read aloud, oral answer, scribe, extra time. Extension on the same skill: a harder or less
  familiar invented case, or the skill returned in another setting, never the next grade's skill.
- Section field 10 (two rooms): a parent teaching one child at the table (the skill rehearsed with the adult playing
  the other part); one projector (the scene card shown and acted by volunteers).
- Section field 11 (not in this section): what is left to another section, course or subject, with health topics
  named and sent to the health unit.

## Where the evidence is thin

Studies of school SEL programs as a group find gains in skills, behavior and attainment (the meta-analyses in the
lens, read as abstracts only). They say nothing about these courses, which no study has tried. Short mindset lessons have mixed results (from memory).
Curio Class states this on every SEL course page and never calls these courses evidence-based.

## Evidence and sources

Read in full: the registry criteria paper, pp 1 to 3 and 20 to 35. Read: the primary SEL guidance from the education
endowment. Read as abstracts only: the meta-analyses. From memory: the mindset line above. No claim of effect is made for
these courses. The course-level logic model (C16) sits on each course page.
# Lens: social and emotional learning

Pre-work for SEL (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md, the SEL row), 2026-11-16. The expert reads this before any SEL
course frame, scope and sequence, unit plan or day. It is a way of seeing, not a bar: the bar is part 9 of
ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md. The standard and the grade sequence: docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md (CASEL 5, 40 sub-skills,
ids and our own words only). The approach: docs/courses/sel/APPROACH.md.

## What good teaching of SEL looks like

**Skills, taught like skills.** A good session names one skill, gives it steps and a reason, models it, and has
every student practise it. Programs that work in studies share four features: a connected sequence, active
practice, a set focus of time on the skills, and explicit skill goals (SAFE). A session of talk about values with no
rehearsal is the common failure.

**Practice by doing.** Self-management and relationship skills are rehearsed: a calming routine done with the body,
an I-statement said to a partner, a disagreement worked through on a scene card. Self-awareness, social awareness
and decision-making grow through reflection and talk about invented cases.

**A sequence by grade, with depth that grows.** Each grade year differs, and each sub-skill comes back at more
depth: a picture-book moment in kindergarten, a named strategy with reasons in grades 3 to 5, a choice under real
pressures in 6 to 8, a plan the student designs and judges in high school.

**The skill leaves the session.** The teacher uses the skill's name and cue in the rest of the day, and the family
note gives one thing to try at home. A skill used only in SEL time does not carry.

**A safe room, not a confessional.** Cases are invented. Sharing is a choice, and making one up is always allowed.
Checks score a skill, never a feeling, a trait or a home life. No surveys, no screening, no data kept (FOUNDATION
promise 2).

**SEL is not therapy.** Plain body and thinking skills, with no clinical words used of a child and no claim to
treat anything. A disclosure of harm or risk follows health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student
tells you something"): the school's reporting route and counselor, named in every unit's teacher notes. Mental health topics, substances and sexual health are health's.

**The values rule, in order** (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12, as social studies applies it). (a) Where a court has
ruled, side with the higher court. (b) Facts and backed sources decide claims. (c) Dignity, respect, kindness and
openness; families of every kind appear where families appear. (d) Grade level and life experience set how and
when. In SEL this means: fairness and bias taught as noticing and checking one's own snap judgements on invented
cases, never as guilt or innocence assigned to a group; unfair norms of the past taught from settled facts; student
voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course.

## Errors reviewers catch

**Course frame and grade sequence**
- A grade year that repeats another year of its band with the same cases and the same depth.
- A sub-skill missing from a grade, or met once with no return.
- Minutes a week not stated, or a short path that drops units rather than sessions.
- No "carry it out" cue or family activity for a unit.
- A unit a school could not leave out without breaking later units.

**Scope and sequence**
- Self-management or relationship skills met only by discussion, with no rehearsal.
- A skill's name or steps changed between units of one year.
- A topic that belongs to health (substances, sexual health, suicide) taught as SEL content rather than pointed to.
- A sub-skill taught above the grade's age (systems and unfair norms before grade 5 beyond playground fairness).

**Unit**
- No end-of-unit assessment of the skill, or one that scores a feeling, a trait or a disclosure.
- A sensitive topic (family, identity, loss, mental health) with no teacher note and no reporting-route line.
- Growth mindset taught as "believe and your grades rise" rather than practice and help.
- Calming routines with religious or spiritual framing.

**Day**
- A prompt that needs a personal disclosure to answer ("tell about a time your family...") with no invented option.
- A role play that casts a child as victim or perpetrator, or uses a real classmate.
- An activity that sorts or ranks students by identity or circumstance.
- Clinical labels for a child or a feeling ("that is anxiety", "you are depressed").
- A product, program, app or registry name, or a claim of being rated or evidence-based.
- The exit ticket asks how a student feels instead of what the skill's step is.

## What this lens does not cover

The scored bar (ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS part 9). Health content and its standards (the health lens). Whether a source
may be adapted (BASE-TEXTS.md). The final read of sensitive content (the fresh sensitivity agent, FOUNDATION
section 7). School-wide SEL, adult SEL and professional learning (out of scope, STANDARD section 6).

