### Handed on: no SEL course after

Grade 12 is the last SEL year; the grade 12 rows hand on to no row (their hands_on cells are empty). Each thread ends in its grade 12 owner unit, with the plan written for a setting past school (work, training, college, service or home), and the student keeps the band's plan pages: the one-year goal plan and support map (unit 24), the coping plan (unit 20) and the looking-ahead sketch (unit 19). The grade 12 year-end check is the band's last check.

## Models carried

| Model | Where it comes from | Introduced or first used here | Used again here | After the band |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| goal steps | SEL 6 to 8: four steps (goal, steps, block and plan, check; the shared goal card, STANDARD section 7). Grows for 9 to 12 (g9-sm-plan-and-tidy) into a plan the student designs: the goal, dated steps, the trade-off accepted, a check date and a verdict on how well it worked | Unit 1 | Units 2, 6, 8, 11, 12, 14, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 24 | Kept by the student: the grade 12 one-year goal plan (unit 24) |
| stop-think-choose | SEL 6 to 8: five steps (stop, list options, weigh each, choose, write the reason). Gains a sixth step for 9 to 12 (g9-soc-read-situation): look back, judge how the choice worked and what to change | Unit 1 | Units 2, 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 12, 13, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 22, 23 | Ends with grade 12 unit 23 (adult choices under pressure) |
| problem size meter | SEL 6 to 8: tiny, small, medium, big, each with who to go to. Grows for 9 to 12 (g9-rel-ask-offer-help) into a support map: each size beside the people and offices that fit it, in school and out | Unit 1 | Units 2, 8, 12, 17, 18, 23, 24 | Kept by the student: the grade 12 support map for the first year after school (unit 24) |
| feelings faces | SEL 6 to 8: the feelings scale (a word and how strong it is). Adds two parts for 9 to 12 (g9-sa-name-feelings): what set the feeling off and what it pushes toward | Unit 2 | Units 4, 7, 8, 11, 13, 14, 17, 19, 20, 24 | Ends with grade 12 units 20 and 24 |
| calm-down steps | SEL 6 to 8: five steps (stop, breathe, name it, pick a tool, go on) with a menu of four tools matched to settings. Becomes a coping plan for 9 to 12 (g9-sm-calm-down): the five steps, a tool for each of three settings, a back-up tool and a review date; plain body skills (breathing, stretching, pausing), no spiritual framing | Unit 2 | Units 8, 14, 20 | Kept by the student: the grade 12 coping plan for the first term away from school routines (unit 20) |
| talk turn card | SEL 6 to 8: five steps, the first "choose how to talk" (in person, a call or a message). Adds a last step for 9 to 12 (g9-rel-talk-listen): check what landed (the listener says back the main point) and plan the next talk | Unit 4 | Units 6, 7, 8, 11, 16, 18, 21, 24 | Ends with grade 12 units 21 and 24 |
| I-statement frame | SEL 6 to 8: four parts, "I feel ___ when ___ because ___. I need ___." (STANDARD section 7). Adds a request and a check-back for 9 to 12 (g9-rel-friendships): "when..., I feel..., I would like..., does that work for you?" | Unit 4 | Units 6, 8, 10, 16, 17, 18, 22, 23, 24 | Ends with grade 12 units 22, 23 and 24 |

Also carried from SEL 6 to 8 and used unchanged all band: the refusal options card (a plain no, a no with a reason, a way out of the place; grade 9 adds the plan made ahead, the ally and the second push, units 4, 10, 17, 23), the fairness questions card (units 5, 9, 18, 22), the who-is-affected map (the stakeholder map page, units 5, 12, 18, 22) and the upstander options card (speak up, support the person afterward, get an adult, report; units 6, 10, 18, 22).

