| 1 | 9 | A new start. *How do I plan my way into high school, and how will I know the plan works?* | 10 | g9-rel-ask-offer-help; g9-sa-i-can-try; g9-sa-what-interests-me; g9-sm-plan-and-tidy; g9-sm-stay-on-task; g9-soc-read-situation | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-discussion, the same discussion norms the class uses all year | Two groups. Group 1, plan the work: the goal steps card grown into the 9 to 12 plan page (the goal, dated steps, the trade-off accepted, a check date, a verdict); a planning system for an invented first month of high school (seven classes, a team, a long-term project) with what it gives up, followed through a case month and judged (SM5); a focus plan for an invented ninth grader with a project due in three weeks and no one checking daily (work blocks, what to do about the phone, a Friday self check-in), its trade-off, and one part rewritten after the first week (SM3; it also begins AASL.V.D.1, trying again when a task gets hard, placed in SEL by library); a plan for an invented student taking a course that feels too hard (two first steps, a place to ask, what counts as progress, the trade-off of less free time), judged from the first test result (SA7). Group 2, read the place and find help: the six-step stop-think-choose (the grade 8 five steps plus look back) used on three invented settings (a first lab with a new partner, a club fair, a hallway where older students gather), what each choice opens and closes, judged against how the case turned out (SO7); the problem size meter grown into a support map (each size of problem beside the people and offices that fit it, in school and out), built for an invented new student (a missed assignment, a schedule error, a worry that keeps coming back), one ask rehearsed aloud with a partner playing the counselor, and what to do if the first helper cannot help (RS8); privately, a plan to explore two interests the school offers (a course, a club, a job shadow day), its cost in time and what it might give, judged after a set date (SA9). Retrieval only: the grade 8 goal steps, stop-think-choose and problem size meter in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page (a support map and a focus plan for a new invented student, with the trade-off and a check date) and the help ask rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the Friday self check-in on the student's own planner, kept private. Family activity: the family's own support map of who to call for what. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the six-step stop-think-choose card, the support map. Curio Class writes the cases, the case month and the cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Management.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.5 (unit 20); CASEL.Social Awareness.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (unit 24). | First because every later plan in the band is written on the goal steps plan page and the support map, and a new high school is the first school-day case every student shares. The ELA lean is in the same weeks (Cross-subject). Six sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: a start-of-year readiness read of the grade 8 cards inside the first openings, for a class that did not have SEL 6 to 8 | SA9: the interests list stays with the student, checked only that the steps were followed. RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps (the school's reporting route and the counselor); health grade 9 unit 8 (help and concern, weeks 6 and 7) comes after and opens with the support map. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | 9 | Stress and coping. *What do I do when the pressure builds, and how do I know what helped?* | 11 | g9-rds-solve-problems; g9-sa-feel-think-link; g9-sa-name-feelings; g9-sa-skills-grow; g9-sm-big-feelings; g9-sm-calm-down | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-w-narrative, a narrative whose character meets pressure and changes | Three groups. Group 1, notice it early: the feelings scale with its two new parts (what set the feeling off, what it pushes toward), used to chart an invented student's school week (a test, a missed bus, good news), find a pattern and plan where an early sign could be caught (SA3); the think-feel-do loop of an invented student who reads a low quiz grade as "I am bad at this", with what the student cares about (doing well for a coach, being seen as capable) shown as part of the loop, and a plan for where to break it (a checked thought, a talk with the teacher) (SA5). Group 2, the coping plan: the calm-down steps grown into a coping plan (the five steps, a tool for each of three settings, a back-up tool, a review date), built for an invented student's heavy week (tests, a game, extra family chores), two tools rehearsed with a partner, judged after the case week (SM2); a response plan for an invented student who snaps at people after hard practices (the trigger, the early sign on the scale, a calm-down step, a planned response, a repair), its cost (leaving a moment early) and a judgment against a second scene (SM1). Group 3, practice and fix: a practice plan on the goal steps card for a skill an invented student finds hard (a language's verb forms, a drum pattern), with what to practice, how often, feedback, rest and a measure, judged from a made-up three-week log, never belief alone (SA8; it also begins AASL.V.D.2, skill grows with practice and help, placed in SEL by library); the class picks one school-day stressor it can act on (a crowded test week, a noisy study hall), sizes it on the support map, designs a fix with its trade-offs, pilots it where the school allows and judges it with the look-back step (RD3). Retrieval only: unit 1's goal plan page and the grade 8 calm-down menu in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen coping plan page for a new invented student and the response plan rehearsed from an unseen scene card. Carry it out: the calm start from the coping plan opens every session from here on. Family activity: a coping plan for one busy family week. Tools: the two-part feelings scale, the coping plan page, the goal steps plan page. Curio Class writes the cases, the week chart and the log. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the pilot's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.1 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.2 (unit 20); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 (unit 23). | Second because every later rehearsal (hard talks, refusals, setbacks) starts from the coping plan, and the first heavy test weeks of high school fall here. Health grade 9 unit 7 (influences and a plan, weeks 5 and 6) teaches its plan for the influences on health in the same weeks; where it comes first, SEL opens with its plan steps. Six sub-skills, three groups. Sessions 11 to 21 of the year, weeks 6 to 11. | Candidate: the class stressor pilot run as the unit's carry-out between sessions | No clinical words; SEL is not therapy and claims to treat nothing. Breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, no spiritual framing. Feelings are named for characters in checks. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health grade 9 unit 7; suicide and self-harm help is health unit 21, weeks 15 and 16). Disclosure: health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the stressor pilot inside school). |
| 3 | 9 | Identity and assets. *Who am I in a new school, and what do I bring?* | 8 | g9-rel-different-ways; g9-sa-my-strengths; g9-sa-snap-judgments; g9-sa-tell-truth; g9-sa-who-i-am | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-rl-world-stories, stories in translation whose characters stand between two worlds | Two groups. Group 1, who I am and what I bring: a short identity sketch for an invented ninth grader (or one the student makes up) naming the roles and groups that make up the person, which parts to keep steady in high school and which to try changing, with the trade-off of each, judged after a scene where two groups pull different ways (SA1); an invented character's assets (a skill, a habit, a language spoken at home, a talent from a hobby), where each could help in the new school year, a strength that may be undervalued there, and which match was strongest (SA2); a plan for an invented student acting on honesty where it is awkward (a group project where one member copied work): what to say and to whom, what it costs, judged with the look-back step (SA4). Group 2, check my guesses and work with difference: a plan for an invented ninth grader to test a snap judgment about a new lab partner (what to ask, what evidence would change it), run on the case and judged on whether it caught the error (SA6); working rules for an invented project team from different backgrounds and schools (how to disagree, how fast to reply) that respect each member's habits, what each member adapts, judged after a scene where they are tested (RS3). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 2 (values and integrity) rows and unit 1's stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen plan page (testing a snap judgment and an honest action for a new invented case) and the team-rules rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 1 and 2. Carry it out: one asset the student chooses to use in a class this month, kept private. Family activity: the family's story of one strength someone brought to a new place. Tools: the identity sketch page, the asset plan page, the six-step stop-think-choose card. Curio Class writes the cases and the scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 (unit 19); CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.4 (unit 23). | Third because a student who has a plan and a coping plan can now look at who they are in the new school; the honest-action and snap-judgment plans use unit 1's six steps. The ELA lean is taught just before (Cross-subject). Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 22 to 29 of the year, weeks 11 to 15, then the mid-year check (sessions 30 and 31). | Candidate: a second Fluency case where the honest path costs a friend, for the two-group unit's second rehearsal | SA1, SA2: family and culture are never asked about by name; home languages and culture are welcome when offered; a student may make up the sketch. RS3: no student is asked to explain their own culture; habits come from invented team members. SA6: invented cases only, never blame of a group. SA4: checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | 9 | Relationships that are good for me. *What makes a friendship healthy, and how do I talk when it is not?* | 11 | g9-rel-friendships; g9-rel-say-no; g9-rel-settle-disagreements; g9-rel-talk-listen; g9-soc-care-when-struggle; g9-soc-read-feelings; g9-soc-thankful | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-discussion, the discussion norms and evidence moves | Three groups. Group 1, talk so it lands: the talk turn card with its new last step (check what landed: the listener says back the main point, then plan the next talk), a hard school-day talk planned on an invented case (telling a lab partner the work split is unfair), rehearsed with a partner and judged from the check-back (RS1); the I-statement frame with its request and check-back ("when..., I feel..., I would like..., does that work for you?"), signs of healthy and unhealthy friendship sorted in an invented case (a friend who copies homework and gets angry when asked to stop), a talk with a limit planned and rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS2); an invented disagreement over a shared school task worked with a partner: each side's interests, two options, one agreed with the frame and a follow-up check, judged after the case's next week (RS5). Group 2, say no and stay steady: a refusal plan made before the pressure arrives (a friend pushing to share test answers in a group chat): the options, an exit, an ally, the value it rests on and the second push ("say it again, move away, tell"), rehearsed against the teacher or a card character (RS6); cues read in an invented school-day scene (a friend quiet at lunch, short replies after class), a check-in planned with two openings, judged once the case reveals more (SO4). Group 3, care and thanks: a support plan for an invented classmate going through a hard few weeks (a lost spot on the team): what to ask first, kinds of care, when to step back and when to bring in an adult, judged against how the friend responds (SO3); a small gratitude habit for an invented student's school week (who, how, when), its trade-off (time, feeling awkward), judged after the case week (SO5). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 5 (communication and conflict) rows, the coping plan and the feelings scale in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen talk plan with the I-statement frame for a new friendship case and the refusal plan rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the check-back step ("so what I hear is...") in every class discussion. Family activity: one family talk with the I-statement frame. Tools: the talk turn card, the I-statement frame, the support map. Curio Class writes the cases and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.1 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.6 (unit 23); CASEL.Relationship Skills.2 (unit 24); CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.3 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.5 (unit 24). | Fourth because hard talks need the coping plan (unit 2) and a sense of what the student values (unit 3). Health grade 9 units 10 (limits, consent and conflict, week 8) and 12 (alcohol and other drugs, weeks 9 and 10) apply these steps to health topics earlier in the fall; where the school runs health in the fall, SEL opens with health's boundary steps and the refusal as retrieval and adds the plan, the ally and the look-back. Seven sub-skills, three groups. Sessions 32 to 42 of the year, weeks 16 to 21. | Candidate: a third Application case set online (a group chat) beside the face-to-face ones | RS2: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health are health's (health units 19 and 20). RS6: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substances are health's (unit 12). RS5: the teacher plays any upset part. SO3: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | 9 | Norms, media and influence. *Who is shaping my choices, and how do I decide for myself?* | 11 | g9-rds-ask-questions; g9-rds-careful-thinking; g9-rds-check-facts; g9-rds-good-for-us; g9-rds-think-ahead; g9-rds-who-is-affected; g9-soc-fair-rules; g9-soc-why-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate, judging whether reasoning holds and evidence is relevant | Three groups. Group 1, rules and systems: the fairness questions applied to an invented school norm (seniors-only tables in the commons) and to a settled case the courts ruled on, Mendez v. Westminster (1947), where a federal court found the segregation of Mexican American children in California schools unlawful, taught as settled fact with dignity; the unwritten rules of two settings compared (the commons and a club meeting); then a plan to change the school norm through school channels, with its trade-offs (SO6); how an invented video app's feed and an invented school rule each push students' behavior, one change designed to either and judged by who it helps and what it costs (SO8); a week planned for an invented student pulled between late gaming streams, sleep and a team counting on them, judged for balance (RD6). Group 2, open questions and checked facts: the questions, sources and what would change one's mind planned before deciding on an invented school-wide issue (a new phone rule) (RD1); an invented claim spreading at school ("most students sleep six hours") checked against a class data set shown in two plots, a decision on what the data back and what they cannot show (RD2); the short- and long-term results of options in an invented case (posting a clip of a class mishap that would get views), the choice planned with its trade-off and judged as the story unfolds (RD4). Group 3, slow down and widen: the six-step stop-think-choose run on an invented choice pushed by a trending post, compared with the same choice made on a shortcut (RD5); everyone an invented school decision reaches mapped (a club's choice to post photos of an event), the rules it touches named (school photo rules, the people pictured), an option that weighs the far side, judged against a stakeholder the map first missed (RD7). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 1 (how I decide) and unit 4 rows, unit 1's six steps in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen decision page (a new invented claim checked against two plots, the people affected mapped, the choice with its trade-off) and the six-step rehearsal from an unseen card. Carry it out: one question asked before sharing any post this month, kept private. Family activity: the family weighs one household rule with the fairness questions. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the stakeholder map page, two printed plots. Curio Class writes the cases, mock feeds and data set. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.8 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 (unit 23). | Fifth because deciding for oneself under pressure needs the refusal and talk steps of unit 4; the data check leans on Algebra I unit 1 (course days 1 to 17), taught months before. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 43 to 53 of the year, weeks 22 to 27. | Candidate: the same phone-rule decision made twice, once with each group's step, as the Application case | SO6: settled facts the courts ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content; the history itself is social studies'. SO8: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity (the Mendez sources first). |
| 6 | 9 | Goals with others. *How do we set a goal together and get it done?* | 9 | g9-rel-lead-follow; g9-rel-stand-up; g9-rel-teamwork; g9-sm-have-a-say; g9-sm-set-goal; g9-sm-step-forward; g9-soc-notice-good; g9-soc-their-view | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e9-sl-presentation, presenting a plan clearly to an audience | Three groups. Group 1, a shared goal and a start: a term goal designed with a partner on the goal steps plan page (milestones, who does what, the trade-off, a midpoint check), judged and revised after an invented setback (SM4); a start-up plan for something a ninth-grade class lacks (a study group, a welcome table): who to ask, the first step, what could stop it and what it costs, judged after the first invented week (SM6); the class plans a proposal to improve one part of ninth-grade school life and each student designs their part (audience, route, evidence, trade-offs), carried out where the school allows and judged (SM7). Group 2, the team: the team designs its plan for a shared project (roles, norms, how to raise a problem), carries it out and holds a short look-back (RS4; it also begins AASL.III.D.2, the group's learning as one's own job, placed in SEL by library); a plan to lead a team session (an agenda, how the group decides, how to hear every voice), run, then following another member's lead, both judged from team feedback (RS7); credit across a project planned for a team leader (what to notice, when to say it), judged on which credit landed (SO2). Group 3, other views and speaking up: the other side's view in an invented team split written well enough that its holder would agree, a question to check it, judged when the case gives more (SO1); an upstander plan for an invented student left out of a team in a hurtful way (the safest option, the words with the I-statement frame, support afterward, when to report), rehearsed with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act (RS9). Retrieval only: grade 8 unit 6 (making a difference) rows and the I-statement frame in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the year-end course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen team plan page and the upstander rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 4 and 5. Carry it out: the shared term goal, checked at the unit's end. Family activity: a shared family goal on the plan page. Tools: the goal steps plan page, the team plan page, the I-statement frame. Curio Class writes the cases and the team task. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the proposal's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Relationship Skills.4 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.7 (unit 21); CASEL.Self-Management.6 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.2 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.9 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Management.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Management.4 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.1 (unit 24). | Last because a team goal pulls on every earlier unit: the plan page, the coping plan, the talk card and the fairness questions; it ends the year with something the class has built. Health grade 9 units 1 (a goal, week 1) and 9 (teams, week 7) teach the goal process and teamwork earlier; SEL opens with them as retrieval where the school runs health in the fall. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 54 to 62 of the year, weeks 27 to 31, then the year-end check (sessions 63 and 64, week 32). | Candidate: the proposal presented to an invited school adult as the Application audience | SM7: no cause is picked by the course; the class chooses inside school life. RS9: no student plays the unkind part or the one left out; school life only. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the proposal's task, inside school). |
| 7 | 10 | Listening to understand. *How do I know I have understood someone, and that they have understood me?* | 10 | g10-rel-talk-listen; g10-sa-feel-think-link; g10-soc-read-feelings; g10-soc-their-view; g10-soc-why-rules | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-sl-seminar, the same student-led discussion norms | Two groups. Group 1, two sides of one talk: a talk planned with a partner on an invented disagreement where each thinks the other is not listening, rehearsed with both people using the check-back step and judged from the partner's account of whether each felt understood (RS1); the strongest form of two invented classmates' opposed views on a school question written out, a conversation planned that finds common ground, judged on whether each person would accept how they were described (SO1); one person's feelings read in an invented scene, then the same moment through the second person's eyes, a response planned for each, judged on which reading the case bears out (SO4). Group 2, two loops and one system: the think-feel-do loops of two invented people in one misunderstanding mapped, with what each cares about shown in the loop, and where either could break it (SA5); how an invented school system (a points reward for attendance) looks to a student it helps and one it pushes out, a change designed and judged from both views (SO8). Retrieval only: grade 9 units 2, 4, 5 and 6 rows (the feelings scale, the talk turn card with its check-back) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen page (two views of a new invented school question written so each holder would agree, and a talk plan) and the check-back rehearsal from an unseen card. Carry it out: "so what I hear is..." before any reply in a seminar. Family activity: one family talk where each person says back the other's point first. Tools: the talk turn card, the feelings scale, the two-loop page. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 (unit 19); CASEL.Relationship Skills.1 (unit 21); CASEL.Social Awareness.8 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.1 (unit 24); CASEL.Social Awareness.4 (unit 24). | First because the grade 10 step (a second person's view) starts in listening; every later unit this year plans with a partner. The ELA and world history leans are in the same weeks (Cross-subject). Five sub-skills, two groups. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: a seminar from the ELA course used as the Application case, where the school runs both | SA5: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment. SO8: invented systems only; no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 8 | 10 | Hard conversations. *How do I hold a hard talk when both of us are upset?* | 10 | g10-rel-ask-offer-help; g10-rel-settle-disagreements; g10-sa-name-feelings; g10-sm-big-feelings; g10-sm-calm-down; g10-sm-have-a-say | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-rl-complex-characters, characters whose motives conflict and change | Two groups. Group 1, the talk itself: an invented hard conversation over a shared commitment planned and held with a partner, each writing the other's interests first, a fix agreed with the I-statement frame and checked later (RS5); a coping plan for two people in an invented tense talk (an agreed pause signal, a tool each could use, how to come back to the talk), rehearsed and judged on whether the pause helped both (SM2); an offer of help to an invented friend who may not want it, how the offer sounds from the friend's side, the support map used to plan when an adult must come in, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS8). Group 2, feelings on both sides and having a say: the feelings of both people in an invented hard conversation charted, where they set each other off, and where naming a feeling aloud could slow the talk (SA3); a plan for keeping a strong feeling from driving an invented hard talk, with how the other person's feeling might rise too, judged in a rehearsed scene (SM1); a plan to raise a class concern with a teacher or school leader (an invented crowded test schedule), how the adult may see it, the plan revised to answer that view, judged after a rehearsal (SM7). Retrieval only: grade 9 units 1, 2 and 4 rows (the coping plan, the support map, the I-statement frame) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page for a new invented hard talk (both sides' interests and feelings, the pause signal, the fix) and the talk rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the agreed pause signal in class discussions. Family activity: agree one pause signal at home. Tools: the coping plan page, the I-statement frame, the support map, the two-person feelings chart. Curio Class writes the cases and cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.1 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.2 (unit 20); CASEL.Self-Management.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 (unit 24); CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (unit 24). | Second because hard talks need the listening of unit 7, and they come before the limits and refusals of unit 10. Six sub-skills, two groups of three. Sessions 11 to 20 of the year, weeks 6 to 10. | Candidate: a rehearsal where the teacher plays an adult who says no, so the student plans the next step | RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps; the friend's trouble stays a school-day one (a grade slump, a quarrel), not a health topic. SM2, SA3: no clinical words; feelings named for characters in checks. RS5: the teacher plays any upset part. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 9 | 10 | Culture and respect. *How do people who see the world differently work and live well together?* | 9 | g10-rel-different-ways; g10-sa-my-strengths; g10-sa-snap-judgments; g10-sa-what-interests-me; g10-sa-who-i-am; g10-soc-fair-rules; g10-soc-notice-good | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-rl-world-literature, stories from under colonial rule, told from inside | Three groups. Group 1, unfair norms and difference: the fairness questions applied to a settled case, Brown v. Board of Education (1954), where the Supreme Court struck down school segregation, taught as settled fact with dignity; how the norm looked to the people it held down and to those it favored; the unwritten rules of two settings compared and a fair change planned (SO6); working norms planned with a partner from another background in an invented mixed team, each writing how the other sees a clash (eye contact, directness, time), judged after a scene (RS3); how an invented student could check a snap judgment about a classmate from another school, how the judged classmate would experience the guess, judged on whether the plan corrected it (SA6). Group 2, self and newcomer: how an invented character sees their own groups and how a newcomer sees them, a plan for making room while staying themselves (SA1); how an invented team could use a member's strength from a home language or culture (translating for a visitor, a family tradition of storytelling), with how the member might want it used or not (SA2). Group 3, credit and purpose: credit planned for an invented teammate whose strength looks different from the others', judged by how the teammate would hear it (SO2); privately, an interest weighed against what another person expects (a friend, a coach), that person's view written fairly, judged after a set time (SA9). Retrieval only: grade 9 unit 3 and 5 rows in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: this unit's Check and Respond are the mid-year course-level check (Course-level checks): an unseen page (team norms for a new invented mixed team, with each side's view) and the snap-judgment rehearsal from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 7 and 8. Carry it out: one question asked before a first judgment of a new classmate, kept private. Family activity: a family story of a custom another family showed them. Tools: the fairness questions page, the team norms page, the six-step stop-think-choose card. Curio Class writes the cases and scenes. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 (unit 19); CASEL.Social Awareness.2 (unit 21); CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 (unit 22); CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Social Awareness.6 (unit 22). | Third because the grade 10 view-taking of units 7 and 8 is now turned on difference and fairness; the world history course teaches apartheid in weeks 22 to 25, after this unit, so SEL uses Brown. Seven sub-skills, three groups (3, 2, 2). Sessions 21 to 29 of the year, weeks 11 to 15, then the mid-year check (sessions 30 and 31). | Candidate: a second settled case from the world history course's later unit, read back in the year-end check | SO6: settled facts the courts ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content; the history itself is social studies'. RS3, SA1, SA2: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from invented partners, each shown as one person's way, never a whole culture's. SA6: never blame of a group. SA9: the list stays with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity (the Brown sources first). |
