ck (Course-level checks): an unseen decision page (a new invented fairness question with a two-way table and two views) and the six-step rehearsal with a partner from an unseen card, with sampled items from units 10 and 11. Carry it out: the fairness pilot, run between sessions. Family activity: a chore split weighed from each person's side. Tools: the six-step stop-think-choose card, the stakeholder map page, a printed two-way table. Curio Class writes the cases and the data. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing; the pilot's supplies the class chooses, up to about $20. Begun here, owned in grade 12: CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (unit 19); CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 (unit 20); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 (unit 22); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 (unit 23); CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 (unit 23). | Last because a fair choice uses every grade 10 step: two views, the data, who is affected; the Geometry two-way table unit (unit 8, weeks 29 to 32) falls in the same weeks, and grade 8 math taught two-way tables, so the page reads one without needing the unit. Eight sub-skills, three groups (3, 3, 2). Sessions 54 to 62 of the year, weeks 27 to 31, then the year-end check (sessions 63 and 64, week 32). | Candidate: the fairness pilot's result read back as the year-end check's sampled case | RD6: the household case is invented; no student's own home is asked about. SA7: the course never records a student's own answer. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (the fairness pilot inside school). |
