strand,grade,skill_id,standard,assumes,hands_on,model_in,model_out,words,cross_subject,notes
rel,K,k-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,,g1-rel-talk-listen,,talk turn card,listen; take a turn; look; voice,ela:ek-discussion (same class talk rules and turn words),"Unit 1, Hello, our class. The child learns two pictured steps for class talk, ""eyes look, wait my turn"", and acts them in a circle game with a talking object; checked by doing the two steps once with the teacher watching. First year: school-day talk only, one listener and one speaker."
rel,K,k-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,,g1-rel-friendships,,,friend; share; play; join,,"Unit 1, Hello, our class. The child learns one way to start a friendship, ""ask to play"", and rehearses it with a puppet and then a partner at free play; checked by saying the ask-to-play words in a pictured case. Cases are classroom play only. First year: starting a friendship, not yet keeping one. Teacher note: no child is asked to name who their friends are, or who is not."
rel,K,k-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,,g1-rel-ask-offer-help,,,help; helper; ask,,"Unit 1, Hello, our class. The child learns the words for asking a grown-up for help (""Can you help me?"") and names the school helpers by picture (teacher, office, nurse); rehearsed with a puppet stuck on a zipper or a lost crayon. First year: asking only, for small school-day needs. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,K,k-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,,g1-soc-notice-good,,,good job; notice; kind,,"Unit 1, Hello, our class. The child notices one thing a classmate did well in a pictured story (""she shared the blocks"") and says it with a frame, ""I saw you ___""; practiced as a closing circle. Praise names an action, never a look or a trait. The band starts with one noticed action, said aloud."
sa,K,k-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,,g1-sa-who-i-am,,,me; like; class; family,,"Unit 1, Hello, our class. The child makes an ""all about me"" picture of things they like to do and one group they belong to (our class), using an invented character's page as the model first. First year: one group, the class, and things the child likes. Teacher note: a child may draw a made-up page; families and culture are never asked about by name, and any family shown is the child's own choice; families of every kind appear in the model pages."
sa,K,k-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,,g1-sa-name-feelings,,feelings faces,happy; sad; mad; scared; feeling,health:k-meh-feeling-words (health uses the same feeling words for health topics),"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child names four feelings from faces in a picture book and points to the face that matches a story character, then plays a feelings-faces matching game; checked by naming a character's feeling from a picture and one clue (the face). The class keeps one feelings-faces chart all year. Where K to 2 begins: four feelings read from faces. Teacher note: feelings are named for characters; a child is never asked how they feel in a check."
sa,K,k-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,,g1-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,feel; think; because,,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child links one feeling to one cause in a story (""the bear is sad because the rain stopped the picnic"") with the frame ""___ feels ___ because ___"", said aloud. First year: one cause, one feeling, a story moment only."
sm,K,k-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,,g1-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,big feeling; slow down; choose,,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child acts out, with a puppet, a character with a big mad feeling who stops before grabbing, using the pictured step ""stop"" from the calm-down steps; checked by showing the stop step on a scene card. The point is that a feeling is fine and the action is chosen, not that a feeling is hidden. Where the band begins: one step, stop, between the feeling and the action."
sm,K,k-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,,g1-sm-calm-down,,calm-down steps,calm; breathe; stop; body,health:k-meh-calm-body (health applies the same calm body routine),"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child learns two pictured calm-down steps, ""stop, breathe"" (a slow balloon breath), and rehearses them as a body routine at the start of each session; checked by doing both steps when the teacher shows the card. First year: two body steps, done with the teacher. Teacher note: a plain body skill with no religious or spiritual framing; no clinical words. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,K,k-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,,g1-sm-have-a-say,,,choose; job; our class,,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child picks one class job or choice-time activity from pictures and says ""I choose ___"", then tries it; the class votes with pictures on one small class choice (which song to start the day). First year: one small choice that shapes the child's own day."
sm,K,k-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,,g1-sm-step-forward,,,try; start; ready,,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child practices starting a small task without being asked twice (getting out a book at the start signal), rehearsed as a ""ready, go"" game; checked by doing the first step after one signal. First year: starting a school-day task on one signal."
sm,K,k-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,,g1-sm-plan-and-tidy,,,"clean up; put away; first, next",,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child follows a two-picture ""first, next"" routine for tidying a center and putting materials away, practiced at the end of each session; checked by doing both steps from the picture card. The band opens with a two-picture routine for one place."
sm,K,k-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,,g1-sm-stay-on-task,,,keep going; finish; focus,,"Unit 2, Naming my feelings. The child keeps working on one short task (a puzzle, a drawing) to the end of a sand timer with the cue ""eyes on my work""; the timer is a pace cue for a short task, not a race. Checked by finishing one small task. First year: one short task finished, with one cue."
sa,K,k-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,,g1-sa-my-strengths,,,good at; strong; help,,"Unit 3, Being me. The child names one thing they are good at or can do (tie a shoe, sing, help a friend), first for an invented character, then for themselves if they choose, drawn on a strengths page. First year: one strength, named for a character before oneself. Teacher note: strengths from home languages and culture are welcome when a child offers them; the course never asks about a child's culture by name."
sa,K,k-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,,g1-sa-tell-truth,,,true; tell the truth; brave,,"Unit 3, Being me. The child hears a story where a character breaks a cup and chooses whether to tell; the child picks the true action from two pictures and says what it costs (""he might be in trouble, but the grown-up can help""). Checked on the invented case only, never on a child's own conduct. The band starts with a choice between two pictures."
sa,K,k-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,,g1-sa-i-can-try,,,I can; try; hard,,"Unit 3, Being me. The child learns the words ""I can try"" for a hard thing and acts out a character who tries a new climb on the playground; checked by naming one step the character can take and one person to ask for help. The course never records a child's own answer. First year: three words and one step for a hard thing."
sa,K,k-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,,g1-sa-skills-grow,,,practice; grow; better,,"Unit 3, Being me. The child sees a character who could not hop on one foot, practiced, got help, and then could; the class tries a short new skill (a clapping pattern) three times and sees it get easier. Taught as practice and help making you better, never as a promise that believing raises results. Where K to 2 starts: a skill seen getting easier over three tries."
sa,K,k-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,,g1-sa-what-interests-me,,,like; interest; fun,,"Unit 3, Being me. The child picks pictures of things they like to learn about and keeps them on a private ""I like"" page; the step checked is that the child chose and drew, never what is on the page. First year: interests picked from pictures. Teacher note: the page stays with the child."
soc,K,k-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,,g1-soc-their-view,,,they; see; same; different,,"Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child says what a story character sees or wants that is different from another character (the one who wants the swing too), with the frame ""___ wants ___"". First year: one other person's want, in a story moment."
soc,K,k-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,,g1-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; hurt; help; kind,,"Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child notices a character who is hurt or sad (fell down, lost a toy) and acts one kind action with a puppet: ask ""are you OK?"", get a grown-up. Checked by naming one caring action for a pictured case. The band begins with one caring action for a hurt that can be seen."
soc,K,k-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,,g1-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,face; body; clue,,"Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child reads a feeling from face and body clues in photos of invented children and the feelings-faces chart, and names the clue; played as ""what is the clue?"" Checked by one face, one feeling, one clue. First year: clues from the face and body only."
soc,K,k-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,,g1-soc-thankful,,,thank you; grateful; glad,,"Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child says thank you for one thing a helper did with the frame ""Thank you for ___"", and the class makes a thank-you picture for a school helper. Thanks name an action, never a gift or what a family can afford. First year: thanks for one action, said with a frame."
sa,K,k-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,,g1-sa-snap-judgments,,,fair; guess; everyone,,"Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child hears a story where a character guesses a new child ""can't play tag"" and is wrong, and learns the check ""ask, don't guess""; checked by choosing the asking action on a picture card. First year: one check on one guess, in fairness at play. Teacher note: taught as checking one's own guess on invented cases, never as blame of a group."
soc,K,k-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,,g1-soc-why-rules,,,rule; class; why; safe,"social-studies:ssk-rules-why (social studies teaches why groups make rules, SEL how I act with them)","Unit 4, Kind hearts. The child connects one class rule to what it helps people do (""walking feet so nobody falls"") by acting the rule and its reason; the class rule poster is the case. First year: why our class has rules, nothing wider."
rel,K,k-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,,g1-rel-different-ways,,,different; same; play together,,"Unit 5, Playing together. The child learns a game from another place, taught by the teacher or a story, and plays it with a partner, saying one way people play differently and one way they are the same. First year: one way different and one the same, met through a game. Teacher note: no child is asked to teach or explain their own culture; offering is the child's choice."
rel,K,k-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,,g1-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,team; together; take turns,,"Unit 5, Playing together. The child builds a block tower with a partner using one step, ""take turns"", from the talk turn card; checked by taking turns for one build. First year: one step, taking turns, in school-day play."
rel,K,k-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,,g1-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; calm-down steps,talk turn card; calm-down steps,disagree; calm; share; swap,,"Unit 5, Playing together. Two puppets both want the same ball; the child shows two steps, ""stop, breathe"" then ""say what you want"", and the class picks a fair end (take turns, swap, share). Checked by doing both steps on a scene card. The teacher plays the puppet who is upset. The band starts with two steps and a fair end the class picks."
rel,K,k-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,,g1-rel-say-no,,,no; stop; walk away,health:k-s-trusted-helpers (health names the trusted grown-ups the child walks to),"Unit 5, Playing together. The child learns to say ""No, I don't want to"" and walk to a grown-up when a puppet (played by the teacher) asks them to do something unsafe on the playground (climb the fence). First year: one refusal sentence and a walk to a grown-up. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no child plays the one who pressures. Health owns body safety."
rel,K,k-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,,g1-rel-lead-follow,,,leader; follow; line,,"Unit 5, Playing together. The child takes a turn as line leader and as follower in a ""follow the leader"" movement game, naming one thing a good leader does (goes slow, looks back) and one a good follower does (watches). The band begins with leading and following in a movement game."
rel,K,k-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,,g1-rel-stand-up,,,unkind; tell; stand with,,"Unit 5, Playing together. A puppet (played by the teacher) is left out of a game; the child learns two steps, ""stand with them, tell a grown-up"", and acts them with a card; checked on a pictured case. First year: two steps, with the grown-up doing the rest. Teacher note: no student plays the one who is unkind or the one left out; the teacher or a card does."
soc,K,k-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,,g1-soc-fair-rules,,,join; fair; everyone can play,,"Unit 5, Playing together. The child hears a story where a game rule (""only kids with red shirts"") leaves someone out, says why it is not fair, and the class makes an ""everyone can join"" rule for one center. First year: playground fairness only. Teacher note: no history or current dispute at this grade."
soc,K,k-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,,g1-soc-read-situation,,,inside voice; outside voice; place,,"Unit 5, Playing together. The child sorts pictures of places (library, playground, lunch) by what they ask of us (quiet voice, running feet, waiting), and acts the right voice for a place the teacher names. First year: three school places and what each asks."
rds,K,k-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,,g1-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,wonder; ask; question; idea,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child asks one ""I wonder"" question about a story problem and hears a classmate's different idea, saying ""that is a new idea"". Practiced in a wonder circle; checked by asking one question about a pictured case. Where K to 2 begins: one wonder question about a story."
rds,K,k-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,,g1-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,look; count; check,math:mk-sort-count (sorting and counting a class tally),"Unit 6, Good choices. The class picks a class game by counting picture votes, and the child says ""we checked, more chose ___""; checked by reading which group is bigger on a sorted tally. First year: counting small groups only."
rds,K,k-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,,g1-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,problem; fix; idea,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child names one fix for a small class problem in a story (spilled paint, not enough glue) and acts it; one problem, one fix, at school. First year: a fix the child can act alone."
rds,K,k-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,,g1-rds-think-ahead,,stop-think-choose,stop; think; choose; then,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child learns the three pictured words ""stop, think, choose"" and says what might happen next in a picture case (""if he runs with scissors, then ___""); checked by naming one next thing for one choice. The class keeps the stop-think-choose card all year. Where the band begins: one step ahead, what happens next."
rds,K,k-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,,g1-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,think; careful; plan,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child sees two characters, one who rushes a puzzle and one who stops to think, and says which went better and why, at play and at a classroom task. First year: two characters compared, at play and at a task."
rds,K,k-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,,g1-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,good for me; good for you; healthy,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child sorts picture choices (wash hands, share snack, push in line) into ""good for me and you"" or ""not"", and says one reason. Health owns the health topics; SEL uses everyday class choices. The band starts with a sort into two piles and one reason."
rds,K,k-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,,g1-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,who; affect; others,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child names who a choice touches in a story (the character, a friend, the class) by placing pictures on a ""who does it touch?"" circle. First year: who a story choice touches, placed by picture."
sm,K,k-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,,g1-sm-set-goal,,goal steps,goal; try; step,,"Unit 6, Good choices. The child picks one small class goal from pictures (clean up the reading corner by the song's end) and one step to reach it, shown on a two-rung goal steps card, then the class tries it. Checked by naming the goal and the step. First year: one class goal and one step."
rel,1,g1-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,k-rel-talk-listen,g2-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,listen; reply; same topic,ela:e1-discussion (same class talk rules),"Unit 1, Our class team. The child adds a step to class talk: listen, then reply on the same topic (""I agree with ___ because""), rehearsed in pairs with the talk turn card. Step up from K: a second person speaks back, so the child listens for what the other said."
rel,1,g1-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,k-rel-teamwork,g2-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,team; job; help each other,,"Unit 1, Our class team. Small groups build a paper bridge with a job each (holder, taper, checker), using the talk turn card to share ideas; the child names their job and does it. Step up: each teammate has a different view of the task."
soc,1,g1-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,k-soc-notice-good,g2-soc-notice-good,,,notice; good at; tell,,"Unit 1, Our class team. The child notices what a teammate did well during the group task and tells them with ""You were good at ___ when ___"". Step up: noticing a real classmate's action in a planned task, not only a story."
sa,1,g1-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,k-sa-my-strengths,g2-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; good at; bring,,"Unit 1, Our class team. The child names one strength they bring to the team (counting, drawing, being calm), first for an invented teammate in a story, and places it on a team strengths poster if they choose. Step up: a strength seen through what a teammate needs. Teacher note: home languages and culture welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,1,g1-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,k-sa-name-feelings,g2-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,frustrated; excited; worried; calm,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The child adds four feeling words to the feelings faces chart and names a character's feeling with two clues (face and what happened). Step up: two characters in one scene can feel different things. Teacher note: feelings are named for characters in checks."
sm,1,g1-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,k-sm-big-feelings,g2-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,big feeling; pause; choose,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The child acts a character with a big frustrated feeling who uses ""stop, breathe"" and then picks what to do, and hears how the other character in the scene felt. Checked on a scene card. Step up: the choice now also looks at the other person."
sm,1,g1-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,k-sm-calm-down,g2-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,calm; breathe; count; choose,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The child adds count to five to the calm-down steps (the shared calm-down card, docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md section 7) and chooses breathe or count; rehearsed every session. Step up: a partner reminds with the cue word and the child chooses the tool. Teacher note: body skill only, no spiritual framing, no clinical words. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,1,g1-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,k-sm-stay-on-task,g2-sm-stay-on-task,,,keep going; distracted; back to work,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The child practices getting back to work when a partner is chatting, with the cue ""back to my work"" said politely; checked by doing it once in a planned work time. Step up: a second person is the distraction."
sm,1,g1-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,k-sm-have-a-say,g2-sm-have-a-say,,,choose; class; vote; say,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The class chooses one calm corner tool for the room by a picture vote, and the child says why it helps others too. Step up: the choice shapes the group, not only the child."
sm,1,g1-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,k-sm-plan-and-tidy,g2-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,first; next; last; ready,,"Unit 2, Calm body, calm mind. The child follows two pictured steps to get ready for a task (desk clear, tools out), then starts, and checks each step on the goal steps card. Step up: the plan gets ready for a task, not only tidies after one, and is shared with a partner."
rds,1,g1-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,k-rds-solve-problems,g2-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,small problem; big problem; fix,"social-studies:ss1-community-problems (social studies looks at community problems, SEL at the child's own)","Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child places invented problems on a three-step problem size meter (small: fix it myself; medium: ask a friend; big: get a grown-up) and names one fix for a small one. Step up from K: the fix depends on who else is in the problem."
rds,1,g1-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,k-rds-think-ahead,g2-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,if; then; next,,"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child uses stop-think-choose on a scene card and names what happens next for the character and for one other person. Step up: two people's results."
rel,1,g1-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,k-rel-settle-disagreements,g2-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; problem size meter,talk turn card; problem size meter; I-statement frame,I feel; when; I need; disagree,,"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child learns the frame ""I feel ___ when ___. I need ___."" (the same three parts as health kindergarten's I-statement, docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md section 7) and uses it with a partner on an invented disagreement (both want the same seat), after checking the problem size; the class names a fair end. Step up: the other person answers. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,1,g1-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,k-rel-say-no,g2-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,no; pressure; move away; tell,health:g1-s-choose-and-ask (health applies choosing and asking to safety topics),"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child rehearses ""No, I don't want to, let's ___ instead"", then moving away and telling (the words of health grade 1's ""say stop, move away, tell""), when a card character urges a rule break (taking another child's snack). Step up: offering a second idea. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; health owns body safety and medicine topics."
rel,1,g1-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,k-rel-ask-offer-help,g2-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,help; ask; offer; grown-up,health:g1-s-ask-for-help (health applies asking for help to safety),"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child matches the problem size meter to who to ask (myself, a friend, a grown-up) and also practices offering help (""Do you want help?"") to a puppet. Step up: help goes both ways. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,1,g1-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,k-sm-step-forward,g2-sm-step-forward,,,start; notice; do it,,"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. The child notices a small class problem nobody is fixing (crayons on the floor) and starts the fix without being asked, in a planned clean-up moment. Step up: noticing what others need."
rel,1,g1-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,k-rel-lead-follow,g2-rel-lead-follow,,,leader; follower; take turns leading,,"Unit 3, Small problems, big problems. In a small group puzzle, the child takes a turn as leader who asks ""what do you think?"" and as follower who shares one idea. Step up: the leader listens to a second person's idea."
soc,1,g1-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,k-soc-their-view,g2-soc-their-view,,,point of view; think; want,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child tells a short story from a second character's side (the wolf's or the friend's), with the frame ""If I were ___, I would think ___"". Step up: a full second point of view, not only what they want."
soc,1,g1-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,k-soc-care-when-struggle,g2-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; comfort; ask,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child picks a caring action that fits what a struggling character wants (some want a friend, some want space) and acts it with a puppet. Step up: ask what the other person wants."
soc,1,g1-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,k-soc-read-feelings,g2-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,clue; voice; body; face,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child reads a feeling from voice and body as well as face in short teacher-acted scenes and names two clues. Step up: two people in the scene, two feelings."
soc,1,g1-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,k-soc-thankful,g2-soc-thankful,,,thankful; notice; say thanks,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child writes or draws a thank-you note to a classmate naming the act and how it helped. Step up: thanks say how the act helped the other person."
sa,1,g1-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,k-sa-feel-think-link,g2-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,feel; think; do,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child traces how a character's feeling changed what they thought and did (""he felt left out, thought nobody liked him, so he went home""), on a three-box strip. Step up from K: the chain reaches an action."
sa,1,g1-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,k-sa-snap-judgments,g2-sa-snap-judgments,,,guess; check; ask,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child spots a character's wrong guess about another child (from how they look or talk) and uses ""ask, don't guess"" to fix it on a scene card. Step up: the other child's side of the story is told. Teacher note: invented cases only, never blame of a group."
rel,1,g1-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,k-rel-stand-up,g2-rel-stand-up,,,stand up; tell; kind words,,"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child rehearses two steps when a card character is unkind to another: stand with them and say ""That's not kind"", then tell a grown-up. Step up: the child now speaks to the unkind act, not only stands with the one left out. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the unkind part; no student plays victim or the one who is unkind."
soc,1,g1-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,k-soc-why-rules,g2-soc-why-rules,,,rule; school; sign; why,social-studies:ss1-group-rules (social studies on why groups have rules),"Unit 4, How do they feel? The child finds signs and rules on a school walk (stop sign at the crossing, quiet sign at the library) and says how each shapes what people do. Step up: rules beyond the classroom, in the whole school."
sa,1,g1-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,k-sa-who-i-am,g2-sa-who-i-am,,,family; friends; groups; me,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child makes a ""my circles"" page of groups they belong to (class, team, club, family) from an invented character's model first. Step up: the same person belongs to many groups. Teacher note: a child may make up their page; families of every kind appear in the models; a family or culture is never asked about by name in a check."
sa,1,g1-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,k-sa-tell-truth,g2-sa-tell-truth,,,truth; promise; values,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child chooses the true action in a story where telling the truth upsets a friend, and says what it costs. Step up: the truth involves a second person's feelings. Checked on the invented case only."
rel,1,g1-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,k-rel-friendships,g2-rel-friendships,,,friend; kind; fix; sorry,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child learns two steps to keep a friendship after a small hurt, ""say sorry, make it right"", and rehearses them with a partner on a scene card. Step up: keeping, not only starting, a friendship. Teacher note: no child names their friends or who is not."
rel,1,g1-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,k-rel-different-ways,g2-rel-different-ways,,,different; same; learn from,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child hears a story of two friends from different places sharing foods or games, and says one thing each learned from the other, then plays a game from the story. Step up: learning from the other person, not only noticing a difference. Teacher note: no child is asked to explain their own culture."
soc,1,g1-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,k-soc-fair-rules,g2-soc-fair-rules,,,fair; leave out; change the rule,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child hears a story where a club rule leaves a newcomer out, says who it leaves out and why that is unfair, and suggests a fairer rule. Step up: the newcomer's side is told. Fairness at play, no history or current dispute."
soc,1,g1-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,k-soc-read-situation,g2-soc-read-situation,,,place; expect; home; school,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child compares what two invented places ask (a visit to the library, the school bus) and acts the fitting way to greet and behave in each. Step up: places outside the classroom."
rds,1,g1-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,k-rds-ask-questions,g2-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; idea; open,,"Unit 5, Families, friends and me. The child asks a partner how they would build the class tower differently and says one new idea it gave them. Step up: questions to another person, not only about a story."
sa,1,g1-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,k-sa-i-can-try,g2-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,I can try; steps; help,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child plans two steps on the goal steps card for a character facing a hard task and one place to ask for help; the class then tries a hard challenge (a tricky tower) with those steps. The course never records a child's own answer. Step up: help comes from another person."
sa,1,g1-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,k-sa-skills-grow,g2-sa-skills-grow,,,practice; mistake; learn,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child keeps a practice chart of one skill tried over a week (a jump-rope count, writing a letter shape) and sees it grow with practice and a classmate's tip. Practice and help, never belief alone. Step up: help from a peer."
sa,1,g1-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,k-sa-what-interests-me,g2-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; wonder; why,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child adds to a private ""I like"" page one thing they want to learn and why; checked only that the steps were followed. Teacher note: the page stays with the child. Step up: a reason, sometimes ""so I can help someone""."
sm,1,g1-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,k-sm-set-goal,g2-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; step; check,health:g1-phw-name-a-goal (health applies goals to health habits),"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child sets a two-step class goal with a partner on the goal steps card and checks it after a week. Step up: a shared goal."
rds,1,g1-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,k-rds-check-facts,g2-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,count; check; choose,math:m1-data-three-categories (a three-category class tally),"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The class picks how to help the school (pick up litter, read to K) from a three-choice tally and the child says what the counts show. Step up: three choices and what others prefer."
rds,1,g1-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,k-rds-careful-thinking,g2-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,think first; careful; plan,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child uses stop-think-choose before a class helping task and says how thinking first helped the group, at school and at home. Step up: the reason is said for a group task, and home joins school."
rds,1,g1-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,k-rds-good-for-us,g2-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,good for me; good for others,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child picks a helping choice that is good for themselves and for someone else and says both reasons. Step up: two people's well-being."
rds,1,g1-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,k-rds-who-is-affected,g2-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,who; affect; class; school,,"Unit 6, I can try, I can help. The child names who a class helping choice touches (us, the K class, the custodian) on the ""who does it touch?"" circle. Step up: people outside the class."
soc,2,g2-soc-fair-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g1-soc-fair-rules,g3-soc-fair-rules,,,fair; unfair; rule; change,,"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child compares a fair and an unfair game rule in two invented clubs, says who each leaves out and what it costs them, and the class rewrites the unfair one. Owner year for K to 2: fairness of rules at play, with a cost named. Teacher note: playground and club fairness only; unfair rules of the past wait for grade 5 by the grade table; no current dispute."
soc,2,g2-soc-read-situation,CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g1-soc-read-situation,g3-soc-read-situation,,,expect; place; different; fit,,"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child reads what an invented situation asks (a quiet test, a noisy assembly, a new classmate's first day) and the chance it offers (a new classmate's first day is a chance to be the first to say hello), acts the fitting response and names the cost of getting it wrong. Owner year for K to 2: both halves of the code, what a situation asks and the chance it offers."
soc,2,g2-soc-why-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g1-soc-why-rules,g3-soc-why-rules,,,rule; sign; town; why,,"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child sorts rules of the class, school and town (crossing guard, library card) and says how each shapes what people do and what goes wrong without it. Owner year for K to 2: rules at school and in town, with a cost."
rel,2,g2-rel-talk-listen,CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g1-rel-talk-listen,g3-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,listen; ask; build on,ela:e2-discussion (same class talk rules),"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child asks a question, then adds to a partner's idea in a planned talk about class rules, with the talk turn card; checked by doing the two steps in one talk. Listening stays the K card's step, said by the teacher as a prompt. Owner year: talk when the other person disagrees."
sa,2,g2-sa-tell-truth,CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g1-sa-tell-truth,g3-sa-tell-truth,,,honest; values; hard choice,,"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child chooses the true action in a case where a rule broken by a friend could be hidden, and says what telling costs and why it matters. Owner year for K to 2: truth with a cost. Checked on the invented case only; never on a child's own conduct."
soc,2,g2-soc-thankful,CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g1-soc-thankful,g3-soc-thankful,,,grateful; thank; show it,,"Unit 1, Rules here and there. The child makes a thank-you card for a school or town helper named in the rules unit, saying what they did and how it helps many people. Owner year: thanks to people outside the class."
sa,2,g2-sa-name-feelings,CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g1-sa-name-feelings,g3-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,disappointed; jealous; proud; nervous,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child names harder feelings on the feelings faces chart and tells which clue shows a character's feeling when the face hides it. Owner year for K to 2: mixed or hidden feelings. Teacher note: feelings are named for characters in checks; no clinical words."
sa,2,g2-sa-feel-think-link,CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g1-sa-feel-think-link,g3-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,feel; think; do; change,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child shows on a three-box strip how changing a thought changes a character's feeling and action (""maybe she didn't see me"" turns mad into okay), with what the character cares about (being included) shown as part of the chain; an unseen item links that value to the thought and the feeling. Owner year: the chain can be changed, and values are part of it."
sm,2,g2-sm-big-feelings,CASEL.Self-Management.1,g1-sm-big-feelings,g3-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,strong feeling; steady; choose,"ela:e2-character-response (how story characters respond to a hard event, the cases SEL acts out)","Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child acts a character with a strong feeling under a pressure (losing a game in front of friends), uses the calm-down steps and chooses an action, then says what the other choice would have cost. Owner year for K to 2."
sm,2,g2-sm-calm-down,CASEL.Self-Management.2,g1-sm-calm-down,g3-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,calm; cope; tool; plan,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child chooses which calm-down tool on the shared card fits which moment (the balloon breath before a test, count to five after a bump, a stretch or asking for a break when the feeling stays big) and rehearses a two-step plan. Owner year: choosing the tool. Teacher note: body skill only, no spiritual framing; health owns mental health topics. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,2,g2-sm-stay-on-task,CASEL.Self-Management.3,g1-sm-stay-on-task,g3-sm-stay-on-task,,,focus; keep going; hard part,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child keeps working through the hard part of a task with a self-talk cue (""one more try""), in a planned work time, and says what quitting would cost. Owner year for K to 2. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sa,2,g2-sa-who-i-am,CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g1-sa-who-i-am,g3-sa-who-i-am,,,groups; belong; part of me,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The child shows how an invented character feels different in different groups (proud at the team, shy at a new club) and makes their own circles page if they choose. Owner year for K to 2. Teacher note: a child may make up the page; family and culture are never asked about by name."
sm,2,g2-sm-have-a-say,CASEL.Self-Management.7,g1-sm-have-a-say,g3-sm-have-a-say,,,say; choose; change; class,,"Unit 2, Strong feelings, steady me. The class proposes one change that would make the room calmer for everyone (a quiet signal) and tries it for a week; the child gives one reason and one cost. Owner year: student voice in class life."
rel,2,g2-rel-friendships,CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g1-rel-friendships,g3-rel-friendships,,,friend; healthy; kind; fair,,"Unit 3, Being a good friend. The child sorts invented friend actions into ""good for a friendship"" and ""hurts it"", and rehearses fixing one hurt. Owner year for K to 2: keeping a friendship when it costs something (sharing a turn). Teacher note: no naming of real friends."
rel,2,g2-rel-settle-disagreements,CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g1-rel-settle-disagreements,g3-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,I feel; when; I need; fair,,"Unit 3, Being a good friend. Both partners use the full I-statement, ""I feel ___ when ___. I need ___."", on an invented disagreement, each asks the other ""What do you need?"", and they agree a fair end; checked as a rehearsal against the look-fors. Owner year: the ask part (asking what the other needs), and an end both can live with. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,2,g2-rel-ask-offer-help,CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g1-rel-ask-offer-help,g3-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,help; ask; offer; trust,,"Unit 3, Being a good friend. The child decides when a friend's problem is too big for kids (problem size meter) and rehearses getting a grown-up, and offers help on a small one. Owner year for K to 2. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,2,g2-soc-their-view,CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g1-soc-their-view,g3-soc-their-view,,,point of view; both sides,"ela:e2-points-of-view (characters who see one event differently, the same move as telling a quarrel from both sides)","Unit 3, Being a good friend. The child tells a friend quarrel from both sides and says what each wants and what it costs them to give in. Owner year for K to 2."
soc,2,g2-soc-read-feelings,CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g1-soc-read-feelings,g3-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,clue; care; check in,,"Unit 3, Being a good friend. The child reads clues that a friend is upset when they say ""I'm fine"" and practices a check-in question. Owner year: hidden feelings. Teacher note: if a child tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rel,2,g2-rel-lead-follow,CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g1-rel-lead-follow,g3-rel-lead-follow,,,leader; fair; follow,,"Unit 3, Being a good friend. In a group game, the child leads by giving everyone a turn and follows another leader's plan even when it was not their idea. Owner year for K to 2: following has a cost."
rel,2,g2-rel-say-no,CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g1-rel-say-no,g3-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,no; pressure; reason; instead,health:g2-v-my-limits-say-stop (health applies refusal to body safety),"Unit 4, Standing up, speaking up. The child rehearses two refusal steps, ""say no with a reason, then offer something else"", when a card character urges a dare or rule break; under the second push the child says it again, moves away and tells, the same words as health grade 2's boundary steps (docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md section 7). Owner year: holding the no under a second push. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; health owns substance and body-safety topics."
rel,2,g2-rel-stand-up,CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g1-rel-stand-up,g3-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; speak up; get help,,"Unit 4, Standing up, speaking up. The child rehearses speaking up for a card character left out or teased (""Stop, that's not fair""), standing with them and getting help, and says the cost of staying quiet. Owner year for K to 2. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, no cause chosen by the course."
soc,2,g2-soc-care-when-struggle,CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g1-soc-care-when-struggle,g3-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; comfort; act,,"Unit 4, Standing up, speaking up. The child chooses and acts a caring follow-up for a character who was teased (sit with them, invite them in), knowing it may cost them a turn with other friends. Owner year for K to 2."
sa,2,g2-sa-snap-judgments,CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g1-sa-snap-judgments,g3-sa-snap-judgments,,,guess; check; fair; first idea,,"Unit 4, Standing up, speaking up. The child catches a character's snap judgment about a newcomer, checks it by asking, and names what the wrong guess cost the newcomer. Owner year for K to 2. Teacher note: invented cases; noticing one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rds,2,g2-rds-think-ahead,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g1-rds-think-ahead,g3-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stop; think; choose; result,,"Unit 4, Standing up, speaking up. The child uses stop-think-choose on a speak-up case, names the likely result of two choices for each person, and judges which was better afterward. Owner year: judging the result."
sa,2,g2-sa-i-can-try,CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g1-sa-i-can-try,g3-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,I can; steps; help; try again,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child plans steps and a helper for a character's hard goal and a ""try again"" step for a setback. Owner year for K to 2. The course never records a child's own answer."
sa,2,g2-sa-skills-grow,CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g1-sa-skills-grow,g3-sa-skills-grow,,,practice; grow; help; mistake,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child tracks one skill over two weeks with practice and help, and explains a setback as ""not yet with practice""; never belief alone as the cause. Owner year for K to 2. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sa,2,g2-sa-what-interests-me,CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g1-sa-what-interests-me,g3-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; purpose; why,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child picks one interest from their private page and names a reason to work at it. Checked only that the steps were followed. Teacher note: the page stays with the child."
sm,2,g2-sm-set-goal,CASEL.Self-Management.4,g1-sm-set-goal,g3-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; steps; check; adjust,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child sets a two-step goal on the goal steps card, checks progress at the end of each week and changes a step that did not work. Owner year: adjusting the plan."
sm,2,g2-sm-plan-and-tidy,CASEL.Self-Management.5,g1-sm-plan-and-tidy,g3-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,plan; time; materials; list,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child makes a picture-and-word plan with times for a two-day class project and keeps materials in a labeled folder. Owner year for K to 2: time is part of the plan."
sm,2,g2-sm-step-forward,CASEL.Self-Management.6,g1-sm-step-forward,g3-sm-step-forward,,,start; lead; idea,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child proposes and starts a small helping idea for the class (a book swap shelf) and says what starting it costs (time at recess). Owner year for K to 2."
sa,2,g2-sa-my-strengths,CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g1-sa-my-strengths,g3-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; bring; grow,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child names a strength they can use toward a goal and one a teammate brings, modeled first on an invented teammate whose strength comes from speaking two languages (she helps a new classmate with a word). Owner year for K to 2: the culture and language part of the code is taught on the character. Teacher note: home languages and culture welcome when offered, never asked about."
soc,2,g2-soc-notice-good,CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g1-soc-notice-good,g3-soc-notice-good,,,notice; strength; specific,,"Unit 5, Goals and growing. The child gives a classmate specific feedback on what they did well in reaching a goal, with an example. Owner year for K to 2."
rel,2,g2-rel-different-ways,CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g1-rel-different-ways,g3-rel-different-ways,,,different; respect; learn; together,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child works in a mixed group on a class helping project with an invented story partner from another place, who brings one way of sharing the work from a game or chore at home (from a named source), tries it and says what it added. Owner year for K to 2: people whose cultures differ, at playground and class scale. Teacher note: no child is asked to explain their own culture; the partner's way is one child's, never a whole culture's."
rel,2,g2-rel-teamwork,CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g1-rel-teamwork,g3-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,team; roles; plan; share,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The group plans and carries out a class helping project with roles, and the child solves one team problem with the talk turn card. Owner year for K to 2: teamwork when time is short. Also carries AASL.III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
rds,2,g2-rds-ask-questions,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g1-rds-ask-questions,g3-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; open; new idea,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child asks questions of other classes or helpers to find what the school needs and changes their idea after hearing a new one. Owner year for K to 2."
rds,2,g2-rds-check-facts,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g1-rds-check-facts,g3-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,facts; count; graph; choose,math:m2-picture-bar-graphs (a picture or bar graph of class votes),"Unit 6, Our class helps. The class graphs a vote on which helping project to do and the child chooses from what the graph shows, not from who talks loudest. Owner year for K to 2."
rds,2,g2-rds-solve-problems,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g1-rds-solve-problems,g3-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,problem; solution; school,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child names a school problem the class can fix (a messy hallway), sizes it on the problem size meter and picks a solution with its cost. Owner year: problems in the wider school."
rds,2,g2-rds-careful-thinking,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g1-rds-careful-thinking,g3-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,careful; think; plan; check,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child says how careful thinking helped the project and where rushing would have cost the class, in school and at home. Owner year for K to 2."
rds,2,g2-rds-good-for-us,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g1-rds-good-for-us,g3-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; good for us; others,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child weighs a helping choice for what it does for themselves, their class and the people helped. Owner year for K to 2."
rds,2,g2-rds-who-is-affected,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g1-rds-who-is-affected,g3-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,who; affect; near; far,,"Unit 6, Our class helps. The child maps who the class project touches near (class, school) and farther (families, neighbors) on the ""who does it touch?"" circle, and one rule it must follow. Owner year for K to 2."
sa,3,g3-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g2-sa-name-feelings,g4-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,frustrated; embarrassed; strength of a feeling; body clue,health:g3-meh-feelings-say (health has students say feelings in health topics with the same words),"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. The feelings faces chart gains a 1 to 5 strength scale, and the student uses a three-step routine, ""spot the body clue, name it, rate it"", on characters in school-day scene cards (a lost recess game, a hard spelling quiz), and says why naming a feeling first makes it easier to handle. Across the band line: grade 2 named hidden and mixed feelings; grade 3 adds how strong and a named routine. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,3,g3-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g2-sa-feel-think-link,g4-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought; feeling; action; chain; kinder thought,,"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. The student fills a think-feel-do chain for a scene-card character (a classmate did not wave back) and then uses ""catch the thought, test it, try another"": writes a second thought that fits the facts and shows how the feeling and action change. Grade 2 saw that the chain can change; grade 3 has the student change it, with a reason (thoughts are guesses, and guesses can be checked). Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sm,3,g3-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g2-sm-big-feelings,g4-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,react; respond; pause; trigger,,"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. The calm-down steps grow to four for grades 3 to 5: stop, breathe, name it, choose. The student rehearses them as a routine before acting on a big feeling in a school-day case (a teammate grabs the ball), then explains in a sentence the difference between reacting and responding. From grade 2's steps used once under a push to a routine the class names and gives a reason for."
sm,3,g3-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g2-sm-calm-down,g4-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,breathe; stretch; count; cope; plan card,,"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. The student builds a plan card with the four calm-down steps and a menu of three body tools (slow breaths, a stretch, counting back from ten) for two school moments (before a test, after a recess argument), rehearses it daily for a week (kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue) and says why each step helps the body. Bigger than grade 2's two-step plan: four steps, each with its reason. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rds,3,g3-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g2-rds-careful-thinking,g4-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,rush; careful; check; result,,"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. Stop-think-choose grows to four steps for grades 3 to 5: stop, list two or three choices, think what each would cause, choose and check. The student compares two invented classmates doing the same school task, one who rushed and one who used the steps, and writes one place at home where the steps would help too. The K to 2 owner year said how thinking helped one project; now two cases sit side by side."
rds,3,g3-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g2-rds-good-for-us,g4-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; healthy; option; others,,"Unit 1, Think, feel, do. The student uses stop-think-choose on a school-day choice (finish a game indoors, or go out and play with a classmate who is alone) and lists what each option does for their own well-being and for the people around them, then picks with a reason. A step past grade 2: two options weighed, not one choice explained."
sa,3,g3-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g2-sa-who-i-am,g4-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; group; role; belong,,"Unit 2, Who I am and what I bring. The student builds an identity web for an invented character (a team, a helper role at home, a club, a language) in three steps: list the groups, say what each adds, say where each part shows. They may make their own private web. Grade 2 showed one character in two groups; grade 3 builds a whole web and says why knowing it helps. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,3,g3-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g2-sa-my-strengths,g4-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; skill; talent; evidence,,"Unit 2, Who I am and what I bring. A strengths hunt in three steps: recall a task that went well, name the skill it used, find another place it helps. Done first for a story character, then for the student if they choose, and a language spoken counts as a strength. The new demand over grade 2: each strength is backed by an example. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,3,g3-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g2-sa-tell-truth,g4-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,honest; integrity; value; tempted,,"Unit 2, Who I am and what I bring. On a school-day case (a classmate's answer sheet left in view), the student uses stop-think-choose to pick the honest action, names the value behind it, and says what honesty cost the character and what it kept. Grade 2 chose truth with a cost; grade 3 ties the choice to a named value. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,3,g3-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g2-sa-what-interests-me,g4-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; curious; purpose; explore,,"Unit 2, Who I am and what I bring. The student keeps a private interest log for two weeks (kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue) (what they chose to do in free time, and why), then picks one entry and names a purpose for it (to help someone, to make something). The step from grade 2: the interest comes from what they actually did, not a single pick. Teacher note: the log stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
soc,3,g3-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g2-soc-notice-good,g4-soc-notice-good,,,specific; shout-out; feedback; effort,,"Unit 2, Who I am and what I bring. After a planned partner task the student gives a three-part shout-out (what they did, how they did it, why it mattered) and rewrites a vague ""good job"" into a specific one. Grade 2 named what and gave an example; grade 3 adds the how and the why."
soc,3,g3-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g2-soc-their-view,g4-soc-their-view,,,perspective; point of view; reason; side,"ela:e3-point-of-view (telling one's own view apart from a character's, the same move in reading)","Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. The student uses a ""shoes switch"" on a school-day scene card: name what each person saw, what each wants and why each acted, then retell the scene from the other side. Grade 2 told both sides of a quarrel; grade 3 asks for the reason behind each side."
soc,3,g3-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g2-soc-care-when-struggle,g4-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,empathy; comfort; ask; act,,"Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. Three care steps, ""notice, ask what would help, do it"", acted with a partner on a scene card where a character struggles (a lost library book, a hard reading turn); the student explains why asking first beats guessing. Up from grade 2: asking what the other person wants is now its own step, read from the feelings faces. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,3,g3-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g2-soc-read-feelings,g4-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,body language; tone of voice; clue; check in,,"Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. The student reads three kinds of clue (face, body, voice) in short teacher-acted school scenes, rates the strength on the feelings faces scale, and checks the guess with a question. Building on grade 2's hidden feelings, the guess now has a strength and a check."
soc,3,g3-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g2-soc-thankful,g4-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; notice; express,,"Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. The class keeps a gratitude wall for two weeks (kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue); each note names the act, the person and the difference it made, and the student says why noticing good things changes a class. Grade 2 thanked a helper once; grade 3 turns thanks into a habit kept over weeks."
sa,3,g3-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g2-sa-snap-judgments,g4-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assume; judgment; evidence; first thought,,"Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. A three-step check on a snap judgment in a school-day case (a quiet new student called stuck-up): catch the first thought, ask what I actually know, find out before deciding, using stop-think-choose. Grade 2 caught and checked a guess; grade 3 names the steps and says why first guesses mislead. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,3,g3-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g2-rel-different-ways,g4-rel-different-ways,,,culture; custom; respect; curious,,"Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. With a story partner from another place on a shared task, the student uses three steps: notice a different way, ask about it with respect, try it or combine it. The new piece after grade 2 is the respectful question. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
soc,3,g3-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g2-soc-why-rules,g4-soc-why-rules,,,rule; system; school; community,"social-studies:ss3-rules-rights (social studies traces a rule back to the problem it solves, SEL how the rule shapes what people do)","Unit 3, Walking in their shoes. The student follows one school system (the lunch line, the bus line-up) in three steps: name the rule, say what it makes people do, say what would happen without it. Grade 2 sorted rules of class, school and town; grade 3 follows one system all the way through."
rel,3,g3-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g2-rel-talk-listen,g4-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,say back; build on; clarify; respond,ela:e3-discussion (the same class talk norms and words),"Unit 4, When friends disagree. The talk turn card grows to four steps for grades 3 to 5: listen, say back what you heard, ask a question, add your idea. The student uses it in a planned partner talk on a friend-disagreement case and gives the reason for saying back first (the other person knows they were heard). Grade 2 asked and added; saying back and listening as a named step are new."
rel,3,g3-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g2-rel-friendships,g4-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,trust; respect; repair; healthy,,"Unit 4, When friends disagree. The student sorts friendship actions into ""builds trust"" and ""breaks trust"", then repairs a broken-trust moment from a scene card in three steps: own it, say sorry with the I-statement frame, make a plan to fix it. The student then rehearses the repair talk with a partner playing the friend who was hurt, saying the sorry with the I-statement frame. Grade 2 fixed one hurt; grade 3 adds a plan. Teacher note: no real friends are named."
rel,3,g3-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g2-rel-settle-disagreements,g4-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,compromise; calm; fair; solution,,"Unit 4, When friends disagree. The I-statement frame grows to four parts for grades 3 to 5: I feel, when, because, I need. With a partner, the student works an invented recess disagreement through ""cool, talk, choose"": calm first, both say an I-statement, then pick a fair solution from at least two. Grade 2 agreed one end; grade 3 compares two. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,3,g3-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g2-rel-ask-offer-help,g4-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,trusted adult; help; offer; problem size,health:g3-s-which-helper (health names which helper fits which safety problem),"Unit 4, When friends disagree. The problem size meter gains a fourth level for grades 3 to 5 (tiny, small, medium, big), each with who to go to. The student sizes school-day problems from a card deck, rehearses asking a trusted adult about a big one in three steps (go to them, say what happened, say what you need) with a partner playing the adult, and rehearses offering help on a small one aloud, the partner now playing the classmate. Grade 2 decided when a problem is too big; grade 3 rehearses the asking itself. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,3,g3-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g2-soc-read-situation,g4-soc-read-situation,,,situation; expect; fit; chance,,"Unit 4, When friends disagree. The student reads a school situation with three questions (where am I, what does this place expect, what chance does it give me) on cases like the start of a group project or a fire drill, and acts the fitting response. Grade 2 named one chance on an invented case; grade 3 asks for it with a named question on school cases."
rel,3,g3-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g2-rel-lead-follow,g4-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,leader; follower; sum up; choose,,"Unit 4, When friends disagree. In a small-group disagreement case the student leads with three steps (hear every idea with the talk turn card, sum up, help the group choose), then follows another leader's choice. Grade 2 gave everyone a turn; grade 3 leads the group to a choice."
rel,3,g3-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g2-rel-say-no,g4-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refuse; pressure; reason; walk away,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. A four-step refusal, ""stop and think, say no clearly, give a reason, offer something else or walk away"", rehearsed when a card character urges a school-day rule break (copying homework, sneaking into the closed gym); the student says why a reason makes a no stronger. Grade 2 held a no under a second push; grade 3 adds thinking before answering. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substance and body-safety topics are health's."
rel,3,g3-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g2-rel-stand-up,g4-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; speak up; include; report,"health:g3-v-bullying (health teaches what bullying is, SEL rehearses the upstander steps)","Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. The student learns an upstander plan with four options (speak up with an I-statement, stand with the one left out, get help, check in after) and picks which fits each of two school cases of teasing or exclusion, with a reason. The student then rehearses the chosen words with a partner, using the I-statement frame, while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act. Up from grade 2: choosing among options. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
rds,3,g3-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g2-rds-ask-questions,g4-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; curious; open-minded; what if,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. Before a class choice the student asks three kinds of question (what, why, what if) of a scene-card case and changes their first idea when a new fact comes in, saying what changed their mind. Grade 2 asked and changed an idea; grade 3 names the kinds of question."
rds,3,g3-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g2-rds-check-facts,g4-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,fact; opinion; data; scaled graph,math:m3-scaled-graphs (reading a scaled bar graph of class data),"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. The class makes a scaled bar graph of a survey (which game for indoor recess) and the student chooses from it, saying which fact decided and which opinion did not. Grade 2 read a picture graph; grade 3 reads a scaled one and pulls fact apart from opinion."
rds,3,g3-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g2-rds-solve-problems,g4-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,problem; brainstorm; solution; test,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. The student solves a classroom problem (crowded coat hooks) in four steps: size it, list ideas, choose one, test it for a week and check (the test kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue). The grade 2 owner row picked a solution; grade 3 tests it."
rds,3,g3-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g2-rds-think-ahead,g4-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,predict; result; later; judge,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. On a school-day choice card the student uses stop-think-choose to predict what each of two choices causes next, later that day and next week, and judges the result after. Grade 2 judged one result; grade 3 looks along time."
soc,3,g3-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g2-soc-fair-rules,g4-soc-fair-rules,,,fair; unfair; include; exclude,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. The student checks an invented game or club rule (only kids who own a ball may play) with three fairness questions (who can join, who is left out, how could it change) and rewrites it. Grade 2 rewrote one unfair rule; grade 3 has a named check to use on any rule. Teacher note: rules in a class, club or game on invented cases; unfair rules of the past wait for grade 5 by the grade table; no current dispute."
rds,3,g3-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g2-rds-who-is-affected,g4-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,affect; ring; school; rule,,"Unit 5, Saying no, choosing well. The student places who a class choice affects on a three-ring ""who does it touch?"" circle (me, my class, the school), with a reason for each, and names one school rule the choice must follow. Grade 2 named near and far; grade 3 places each person with a reason."
sa,3,g3-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g2-sa-i-can-try,g4-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,stretch goal; block; helper; if-then,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The student plans a stretch goal for an invented character on the goal steps card (goal, steps, a likely block, a helper) and writes an ""if this happens, then I will"" plan for the block. Grade 2 planned a try-again step after a setback; grade 3 names the block before it happens. The course never records a student's own answer."
sa,3,g3-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g2-sa-skills-grow,g4-sa-skills-grow,,,practice; feedback; improve; strategy,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The student keeps a three-week practice record (kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue) of one school skill (multiplication facts, a cartwheel), notes which practice or help made the biggest change, and explains growth as practice, strategy and help, never belief alone. Over grade 2: comparing which kind of practice helped most."
sm,3,g3-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g2-sm-stay-on-task,g4-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; distraction; self-talk; finish,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. A three-step focus plan (spot the distraction, say the cue, move seats or ask) used in a planned work time and checked off on the goal steps card, with a try-again step for when the work itself gets hard (try another way, then ask). Grade 2 used one self-talk cue; grade 3 plans for the distraction ahead of time."
sm,3,g3-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g2-sm-set-goal,g4-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; steps; block; check,health:g3-phw-set-goal (health applies goal setting to a health habit),"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The goal steps card grows to four steps for grades 3 to 5: goal, steps, block and plan, check. The student sets a three-week school goal (finish a chapter book), checks weekly and changes one step; the goal is kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue. The new demand over grade 2: a block is planned for before it comes."
sm,3,g3-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g2-sm-plan-and-tidy,g4-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,schedule; deadline; organize; checklist,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The student plans a one-week class project on a planner with days and times, keeps a materials checklist, and reviews at the end what went to plan. Grade 2 planned two days; grade 3 a week."
sm,3,g3-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g2-sm-step-forward,g4-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,notice; start; first step; need,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The student notices a class need, plans the first step on the goal steps card and starts it without being asked (a welcome kit for new students). Grade 2 started a small idea; grade 3 plans before starting."
sm,3,g3-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g2-sm-have-a-say,g4-sm-have-a-say,,,propose; vote; reason; evidence,"social-studies:ss3-taking-part (social studies on how people take part in a democracy, SEL on the student's own voice in class)","Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. The class proposes and votes on one change to a class routine; the student gives a reason with evidence and helps carry it out for two weeks, kept between sessions as the unit's carry-out cue. Grade 2 gave a reason; grade 3 backs it with evidence."
rel,3,g3-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g2-rel-teamwork,g4-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,team; role; recorder; timekeeper,,"Unit 6, Goals that stretch me. Groups rotate four named roles (leader, recorder, checker, timekeeper) on a stretch challenge (a paper tower that holds a book), use the talk turn card, and the student solves one team problem and says what the group did well. Grade 2 had roles; grade 3 rotates four."
sa,4,g4-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g3-sa-tell-truth,g5-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,honest; respect; mistake; values,,"Unit 1, Team rules we make. On a team case where a teammate wants to hide a mistake from the teacher, the student uses stop-think-choose to pick the honest action, says how the teammate sees it, and finds a way to be honest that respects them. Grade 4 adds the teammate's view of the honest choice. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
rel,4,g4-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g3-rel-talk-listen,g5-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,say back; view; agree; norm,ela:e4-discussion (the same class talk norms and words),"Unit 1, Team rules we make. Before the class agrees a team norm, the student uses the talk turn card to say back a partner's view and then their own, and checks that the partner feels heard. This year the listener reports the other person's view, not only their words."
soc,4,g4-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g3-soc-read-situation,g5-soc-read-situation,,,situation; role; expect; teammate,,"Unit 1, Team rules we make. The student reads what a team situation asks of each member (a group presentation, a substitute teacher day) and compares what it asks of them with what it asks of a teammate in another role. New in grade 4: the same moment read for a second person in another role."
soc,4,g4-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g3-soc-fair-rules,g5-soc-fair-rules,,,fair; newcomer; member; accept,,"Unit 1, Team rules we make. Teams review invented club rules from two sides, a newcomer's and a longtime member's, with the three fairness questions, and agree a version both could accept. Grade 4 adds the second view. Teacher note: invented class, club or game rules only; unfair rules of the past wait for grade 5; no current dispute."
rel,4,g4-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g3-rel-teamwork,g5-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,agreement; decide; share; both ideas,physical-education:g4-team-roles (team roles in games),"Unit 1, Team rules we make. Teams write their own working agreement (how we decide, how we share jobs) and use it on a group build; the student names how a teammate saw a problem differently and how the team used both ideas. The grade 4 turn: a teammate's angle shapes the plan."
rds,4,g4-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g3-rds-careful-thinking,g5-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,review; mistake; careful; check,,"Unit 2, Plans and steps. The student reviews a team plan with stop-think-choose, asks a teammate where careful thinking caught a mistake, and writes one place outside school where it would help. Grade 4 adds a partner who points out what the thinking caught."
sa,4,g4-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g3-sa-i-can-try,g5-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,path; goal; helper; confidence,,"Unit 2, Plans and steps. For two invented characters with the same hard goal, the student plans two different paths on the goal steps card, each with a helper, and says why one path fits each character better. This year two characters, two paths: what fits one person may not fit another. The course never records a student's own answer."
sm,4,g4-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g3-sm-stay-on-task,g5-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; partner check; distraction; finish,,"Unit 2, Plans and steps. Across a two-week team project the student uses the focus plan and its try-again step (try another way, then ask) and checks in with a partner who uses theirs; they compare what pulls each of them off task or stalls them on a hard step, and swap one tip. Grade 4 adds the partner: two plans compared and a tip traded."
sm,4,g4-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g3-sm-set-goal,g5-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,shared goal; steps; block; adjust,health:g4-phw-goal-plan (health plans steps toward a health goal),"Unit 2, Plans and steps. With a partner the student sets a shared goal on the goal steps card, each with their own steps, and changes a step after hearing what blocks the partner. A second person's block now shapes the plan."
sm,4,g4-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g3-sm-plan-and-tidy,g5-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,timeline; deadline; who does what; materials,,"Unit 2, Plans and steps. The team splits a project into a timeline with deadlines and who does what; the student keeps their own materials list, and at review compares how a teammate planned the same job. Grade 4 adds the team: the plan holds other people's parts and deadlines."
sm,4,g4-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g3-sm-step-forward,g5-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,propose; first step; ask; start,,"Unit 2, Plans and steps. The student proposes a first step for a team project, asks a teammate's view before starting, and starts it, saying how the other view changed the start. New in grade 4: a teammate's view shapes the first step."
sa,4,g4-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g3-sa-who-i-am,g5-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; share; differ; web,,"Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student compares two invented characters' identity webs, finds what they share and what differs, and may add to their own private web. Grade 4 sets one web beside another. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,4,g4-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g3-sa-my-strengths,g5-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; language; culture; seen by others,,"Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student finds a strength a story character brings from their culture or language, and a strength a teammate saw in them, and compares the two views of a strength. Grade 4 adds how another person sees a strength. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
soc,4,g4-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g3-soc-notice-good,g5-soc-notice-good,,,specific; strength; unseen; feedback,,"Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student gives specific feedback that names a strength the classmate may not see in themselves, and asks how they see it. The grade 4 turn: the classmate's own view of the strength is asked for."
soc,4,g4-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g3-soc-their-view,g5-soc-their-view,,,point of view; misunderstand; both; shoes switch,ela:e4-narration-pov (first and third person narration compared),"Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student retells a school scene from two characters' points of view with the shoes switch, and writes what each misunderstood about the other. The two sides now meet in one write-up."
sa,4,g4-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g3-sa-snap-judgments,g5-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assume; judge; check; differ,,"Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student checks snap judgments in a case where two characters judge a third differently, finds what each assumed, and uses stop-think-choose to decide what to find out. Grade 4 adds a second judge: two characters, two guesses. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,4,g4-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g3-rel-different-ways,g5-rel-different-ways,,,custom; blend; respect; ask,social-studies:ss4-regional-cultures (regions with different ways of life),"Unit 3, Many ways to be. In a project with a story partner whose customs differ, the student plans how to blend two ways of doing a task and asks how the partner sees the blend. This year the partner's view of the blend counts. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
sa,4,g4-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g3-sa-name-feelings,g5-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,mixed; strength; same event; different,,"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student names and rates feelings for two characters in one team scene on the feelings faces scale and explains how the same event made them feel differently. Grade 4 adds a second character: one event, two feelings. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,4,g4-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g3-sa-feel-think-link,g5-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought; feeling; action; two chains,health:g4-meh-same-thing-different-people (health shows one thing affects people differently),"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student fills two think-feel-do chains for two characters after the same event (a team loses) and shows how different thoughts led to different feelings and actions. Grade 4 runs the chain for two people at once. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sm,4,g4-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g3-sm-big-feelings,g5-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,respond; collide; teammate; steady,,"Unit 4, Feelings at work. In a team scene where two characters' strong feelings collide, the student uses the calm-down steps and says how their response lands on the teammate. New in grade 4: the response is judged by how it lands on someone else."
sm,4,g4-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g3-sm-calm-down,g5-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,calm-down plan; coach; cue word; cope,health:g4-meh-calm-stress (health applies calming to stress),"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student builds a calm-down plan for a team moment (a project falling behind) and coaches a partner through theirs with the cue word, noticing which tool works for whom. Grade 4 adds a partner: the student coaches someone else's plan. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,4,g4-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g3-soc-care-when-struggle,g5-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; adapt; ask; struggle,,"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student uses the care steps for a struggling teammate and adapts when the teammate wants something different from what the student would want. Grade 4 adds the other person's wish, which may differ from the student's own. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,4,g4-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g3-soc-read-feelings,g5-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,clue; check in; fit; respect,,"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student reads the clues of two people in a scene on the feelings faces chart and checks in with one in a way that fits that person (quietly, later, with a question). Grade 4 adds the fit: the check-in suits the other person, not the student."
soc,4,g4-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g3-soc-thankful,g5-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; cost; helper; view,,"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The team writes thanks to a person who helped the project, from that person's side: what helping cost them and what it made possible. New this year: thanks written from the helper's side."
soc,4,g4-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g3-soc-why-rules,g5-soc-why-rules,,,system; rule; role; shape,social-studies:ss3-levels-of-government (what local and state governments do),"Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student traces how one school or town system (recess rules, a bike lane) shapes what two different people do, such as a student and a crossing guard, and why each might see it differently. Grade 4 adds a second person: one system seen from two places in it."
rel,4,g4-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g3-rel-friendships,g5-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,repair; friend; side; plan,,"Unit 5, Leading and following. In a team case where two friends land in different groups, the student repairs the hurt with own it, sorry and a plan, and hears the friend's side first. The student rehearses the repair talk aloud with a partner playing the friend, using the I-statement frame. Grade 4 adds the friend's side: it is heard before the sorry. Teacher note: no real friends are named."
rel,4,g4-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g3-rel-settle-disagreements,g5-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,both reasons; compromise; fair; I feel,,"Unit 5, Leading and following. With a partner, the student works a team disagreement where both sides have good reasons: each says the other's view first, then they use the I-statement frame and find a solution that meets both. Grade 4 adds the other side's view, said before one's own. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,4,g4-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g3-rel-ask-offer-help,g5-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,offer; take over; trusted adult; size,health:g4-meh-help-with-feelings (health on getting help with feelings),"Unit 5, Leading and following. The student notices when an invented teammate needs help and offers it without taking over, and sizes a team problem on the problem size meter to know when to bring in a trusted adult. The student rehearses the offer aloud with a partner playing the teammate, then the ask with the partner playing the adult. Grade 4 adds the teammate: help offered so the other person keeps the job. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rel,4,g4-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g3-rel-lead-follow,g5-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,leader; follower; decision; view,,"Unit 5, Leading and following. The student leads a team task with the three leading steps, then follows, and reflects on how leader and follower saw the same decision. This year the leader's and the follower's views of one decision are compared."
rel,4,g4-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g3-rel-say-no,g5-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refuse; group; pressure; keep friends,health:g4-t-saying-no (health applies refusal to tobacco),"Unit 5, Leading and following. The student rehearses the refusal steps when a card character urges the whole group to break a rule (skip the hard part, blame another team), and holds the no while keeping the friendship. Grade 4 adds the group: the no is held while the friendship is kept. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substance and body-safety topics are health's."
rel,4,g4-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g3-rel-stand-up,g5-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; leader; follower; include,health:g4-v-speak-up-notice (health on noticing and speaking up),"Unit 5, Leading and following. The student plans how a leader and how a follower could each speak up when a teammate is left out, choosing from the upstander options for each role. The student then rehearses the leader's words and the follower's words with a partner, using the I-statement frame, while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act. Grade 4 adds the role: what a leader can say differs from what a follower can. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
sm,4,g4-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g3-sm-have-a-say,g5-sm-have-a-say,,,proposal; elect; weigh; carry out,,"Unit 5, Leading and following. The class elects a team to carry out a class improvement, and the student weighs a classmate's different proposal before voting. Grade 4 adds a classmate's different proposal, weighed before the vote."
rds,4,g4-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g3-rds-good-for-us,g5-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; friend; pull apart; weigh,health:g4-s-decide-with-help (health on deciding with help),"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student weighs a choice where their own well-being and a friend's pull apart (stay up for the friend's call, or sleep before the game) and picks with a reason. New in grade 4: a friend's well-being and one's own pull apart."
sa,4,g4-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g3-sa-what-interests-me,g5-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; purpose; others; share,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student adds to the private interest log a purpose that connects to other people and names who might share the interest. Teacher note: the log stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
rds,4,g4-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g3-rds-ask-questions,g5-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,interview; open question; view; change,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student interviews a partner with open questions about a choice the class faces and writes how the partner's view changed their own idea. Grade 4 adds the interview: a partner's answers can change the student's idea."
rds,4,g4-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g3-rds-check-facts,g5-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,claim; source; fact; check,health:g4-fn-food-claims-source-check (health checks a claim against its source),"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student checks a claim a classmate makes (this snack gives you energy) against facts from a second source, and chooses on what the facts show. This year a second source is checked against a classmate's claim."
rds,4,g4-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g3-rds-solve-problems,g5-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,problem; views; gather; solution,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The team solves a school problem by gathering two groups' views (younger students, lunch staff) before choosing and testing a solution. Grade 4 adds other groups' views before the choice."
rds,4,g4-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g3-rds-think-ahead,g5-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,predict; result; second person; compare,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student predicts the results of a choice for themselves and for a second person, then compares the prediction with what happened in the story. Grade 4 adds the second person: results predicted for someone else too."
rds,4,g4-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g3-rds-who-is-affected,g5-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,affect; group; view; rule,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student maps who a school choice touches and how each group would see it, then names the rule or group the choice must answer to. New in grade 4: how each group would see the choice."
sa,4,g4-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g3-sa-skills-grow,g5-sa-skills-grow,,,feedback; practice; record; compare,,"Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student compares their practice record with a story character's and explains how feedback from another person changed the practice. Grade 4 adds another person's feedback as a cause of growth. Growth comes from practice, strategy and help, never belief alone."
sa,5,g5-sa-name-feelings,CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g4-sa-name-feelings,g6-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,mixed feelings; nervous; excited; both,,"Unit 1, Changes ahead. The student names and rates mixed feelings about a coming change for a story character (moving to middle school), including two feelings that pull opposite ways, and says why naming both helps. Owner year for 3 to 5: feelings named under the pressure of change. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,5,g5-sa-feel-think-link,CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g4-sa-feel-think-link,g6-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,balanced thought; worry; fact; chain,,"Unit 1, Changes ahead. The student fills a think-feel-do chain for a character whose friends say middle school is scary, and writes a balanced thought that keeps to the facts. Owner year for 3 to 5: the chain is changed under others' pressure. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sm,5,g5-sm-big-feelings,CASEL.Self-Management.1,g4-sm-big-feelings,g6-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,pressure; react; respond; cost,,"Unit 1, Changes ahead. On an invented case (a schedule change on a test day), the student lists two responses and picks one, then rehearses the calm-down steps and the chosen response in a short scene with a partner, and says what reacting would have cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: the steps hold when a change adds pressure."
sm,5,g5-sm-calm-down,CASEL.Self-Management.2,g4-sm-calm-down,g6-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,cope; test; tool; change,health:g5-meh-mind-habits (health on habits that help the mind),"Unit 1, Changes ahead. The student builds a calm-down plan for a time of change, tests two tools in a timed practice, and keeps the one that worked with a reason. Owner year for 3 to 5: choosing a tool under time pressure. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,5,g5-soc-read-situation,CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g4-soc-read-situation,g6-soc-read-situation,,,chance; new place; expect; miss,,"Unit 1, Changes ahead. The student reads what a new situation will ask (a middle school visit, a new team) and names the chances it offers and what missing them would cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: a new situation read ahead, with the cost of a missed chance."
sa,5,g5-sa-what-interests-me,CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g4-sa-what-interests-me,g6-sa-what-interests-me,,,purpose; worth it; time; interest,,"Unit 2, What drives me. The student finds a purpose for an interest that costs something (practice time) and decides privately whether it is worth it. Owner year for 3 to 5: an interest weighed against what it costs. Teacher note: the log stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,5,g5-sa-i-can-try,CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g4-sa-i-can-try,g6-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,setback; backup plan; helper; cost,,"Unit 2, What drives me. The student plans a stretch goal for a character facing a setback that costs something (a free afternoon), with two steps, a helper and a backup plan on the goal steps card. Owner year for 3 to 5: the plan carries a backup for a setback that costs something. The course never records a student's own answer."
sa,5,g5-sa-skills-grow,CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g4-sa-skills-grow,g6-sa-skills-grow,,,strategy; setback; help; not yet,,"Unit 2, What drives me. When a friend in a story says ""you're just not good at it"", the student uses a three-week practice record to explain the setback as a sign to change strategy or get help. Never belief alone. Owner year for 3 to 5: growth explained against someone else's put-down. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sm,5,g5-sm-stay-on-task,CASEL.Self-Management.3,g4-sm-stay-on-task,g6-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; temptation; long project; cost,,"Unit 2, What drives me. The student keeps on task through a long project when friends offer something more fun and when the next step gets hard, using the focus plan and a try-again step (try another way, then ask), and says what giving in would cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: focus held against friends' pull and through a hard step. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sm,5,g5-sm-set-goal,CASEL.Self-Management.4,g4-sm-set-goal,g6-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; track; compete; drop,health:g5-phw-goal-track (health tracks progress toward a goal),"Unit 2, What drives me. The student sets a four-week goal on the goal steps card (it runs inside the unit and is judged at its end), tracks it weekly, and decides what to drop when two goals compete for time. Owner year for 3 to 5: a goal adjusted when two goals compete."
sm,5,g5-sm-plan-and-tidy,CASEL.Self-Management.5,g4-sm-plan-and-tidy,g6-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,backward plan; deadline; slip; recover,,"Unit 2, What drives me. The student plans a multi-week project backward from a deadline and recovers when a step slips, saying what the slip cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: a plan built backward that survives a slip."
rds,5,g5-rds-careful-thinking,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g4-rds-careful-thinking,g6-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,careful; hurry; group; hold,,"Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student shows on an invented case how careful thinking held up when a group pushed for a fast choice, and what hurrying would have cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: careful thinking held against a group's hurry."
sa,5,g5-sa-snap-judgments,CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g4-sa-snap-judgments,g6-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,snap judgment; group; agree; check,,"Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student notices how a group can push a snap judgment (friends agree about a new student) and checks it anyway with the three steps and stop-think-choose. Owner year for 3 to 5: the check held when friends share the guess. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
soc,5,g5-soc-why-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g4-soc-why-rules,g6-soc-why-rules,,,system; rule; follow; pressure,social-studies:ss5-constitution (how a government's rules and rights work),"Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student traces how a school system and a town system (school rules, park signs and fines) shape what people do, and names a pressure that makes people follow or break them. Owner year for 3 to 5; media and online norms begin in grade 6."
rds,5,g5-rds-check-facts,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g4-rds-check-facts,g6-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,claim; evidence; everyone; numbers,ela:e5-reasons-to-points (reasons and evidence behind a point),"Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student checks a claim like ""everyone is doing it"" against facts and numbers and chooses on the evidence even when friends lean the other way. Owner year for 3 to 5: the facts chosen over the crowd."
soc,5,g5-soc-fair-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g4-soc-fair-rules,g6-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; unfair; change; Supreme Court,social-studies:ss5-civil-rights-movements (the history of Brown v. Board of Education and the movement),"Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student uses the three fairness questions on a norm from settled history that was unfair and was changed: school segregation, struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954, is the model case. They name what changing it cost the people who did. Owner year for 3 to 5: the first year past playground fairness. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; the history is social studies' unit; current political disputes are not SEL content."
soc,5,g5-soc-their-view,CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g4-soc-their-view,g6-soc-their-view,,,point of view; change; place; imagine,ela:e5-narrator-influence (how a narrator's view shapes a story),"Unit 4, Friends through change. On an invented case (a story friend whose family is moving across town mid-year), the student takes the friend's view with the shoes switch and says how their own view would change in that place. Owner year for 3 to 5: a view taken through a change the student has not lived."
soc,5,g5-soc-care-when-struggle,CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g4-soc-care-when-struggle,g6-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; cost; friend; follow up,health:g5-meh-care-and-kindness (health on care and kindness),"Unit 4, Friends through change. The student acts the care steps with a partner for an invented friend whose best friend has moved away, even when it costs time with others, and plans a follow-up. Owner year for 3 to 5: care given when it costs, and kept up after. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,5,g5-soc-read-feelings,CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g4-soc-read-feelings,g6-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,mixed clues; pull away; check in; respect,,"Unit 4, Friends through change. The student reads mixed clues from an invented friend who says they are fine but is pulling away, checks in, respects the answer, and names a trusted adult to tell if the worry stays. Owner year for 3 to 5: clues that contradict the words. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rel,5,g5-rel-talk-listen,CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g4-rel-talk-listen,g6-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,hard conversation; patience; say back; build,ela:e5-discussion (the same class talk norms and words),"Unit 4, Friends through change. The student uses the full talk turn card in an invented hard conversation about a friendship changing (a friend joins a new group), where saying back costs patience. Owner year for 3 to 5: the full card held when saying back costs patience."
rel,5,g5-rel-friendships,CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g4-rel-friendships,g6-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,healthy; unhealthy; change; trusted adult,,"Unit 4, Friends through change. On two invented cases, a friend who will be in a different class next year and an invented friend who will only play if the student leaves another child out, the student plans how to keep the first friendship across the change and how to step back from the second with a trusted adult's help, using the I-statement frame. The student rehearses the opening of each talk with a partner playing the friend. Owner year for 3 to 5: a friendship kept through change, and stepped back from when it asks something unkind. Teacher note: no real friends are named; relationship health topics are health's. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rel,5,g5-rel-settle-disagreements,CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g4-rel-settle-disagreements,g6-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,compromise; give up; fair; both,health:g5-v-conflict-steps (health applies conflict steps to health topics),"Unit 4, Friends through change. With a partner, the student works a disagreement where any fair solution costs each side something, using the I-statement frame, and chooses a compromise both can keep. Owner year for 3 to 5: a compromise where each side gives something up. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,5,g5-rel-ask-offer-help,CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g4-rel-ask-offer-help,g6-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,ask; embarrassed; new; offer,,"Unit 4, Friends through change. On an invented case the student sizes two problems on the problem size meter, then rehearses with a partner asking a teacher for help when asking feels costly (in front of friends), the partner playing the teacher, and offering help to an invented new classmate, the partner playing the newcomer. Owner year for 3 to 5: asking holds even when friends are watching. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
rel,5,g5-rel-say-no,CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g4-rel-say-no,g6-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refuse; left out; hold; another friend,health:g5-aod-refuse-alcohol-drugs (health applies refusal to alcohol and drug topics),"Unit 4, Friends through change. The student rehearses the refusal steps against a card character who adds a social cost (""then you're not in our group""), holds the no and names another friend to turn to. Owner year for 3 to 5: the no held against a social cost. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substance and body-safety topics are health's."
soc,5,g5-soc-notice-good,CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g4-soc-notice-good,g6-soc-notice-good,,,specific; overlooked; credit; feedback,,"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The student gives specific feedback to a teammate whose work was overlooked, even when the group praises someone else. Owner year for 3 to 5: credit given against the group's lean."
soc,5,g5-soc-thankful,CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g4-soc-thankful,g6-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; cost; overlooked; thanks,,"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The student thanks someone who helped the group at a cost to themselves, naming what it cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: thanks for help that had a price, said in specific words."
rel,5,g5-rel-different-ways,CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g4-rel-different-ways,g6-rel-different-ways,,,custom; clash; respect; together,,"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The student works in a mixed team on the school improvement and resolves a clash of habits with respect, using the three steps. Owner year for 3 to 5: a clash of habits resolved on a real class project. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
rel,5,g5-rel-lead-follow,CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g4-rel-lead-follow,g6-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,split group; lead; follow; lose,,"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The student leads a group decision when the group is split, and follows well when their own idea loses. Owner year for 3 to 5: leading a split group, and following when one's own idea loses."
rel,5,g5-rel-stand-up,CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g4-rel-stand-up,g6-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; risk; safe option; friends,health:g5-s-speak-up-for-safety (health on speaking up for safety),"Unit 5, My voice, our group. On an invented case (the student's own friend group laughs at a schoolmate's mistake at the class concert), the student chooses the safest upstander option, rehearses the chosen words with a partner using the I-statement frame while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, and says why that option fits. Owner year for 3 to 5: speaking up when it may cost standing with friends. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
sm,5,g5-sm-step-forward,CASEL.Self-Management.6,g4-sm-step-forward,g6-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,start; recruit; first step; time,,"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The student plans the first step on the goal steps card for an invented case first (a lost-and-found box that overflows), then starts a class improvement the class chooses, though it takes recess time, and recruits others. Owner year for 3 to 5: starting when starting costs something."
sm,5,g5-sm-have-a-say,CASEL.Self-Management.7,g4-sm-have-a-say,g6-sm-have-a-say,,,proposal; evidence; principal; carry out,social-studies:ss5-informed-action (social studies on choosing and carrying out an informed action),"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The class proposes a school improvement to the principal or student council with reasons and evidence; the student weighs its time cost and carries out a part. Owner year for 3 to 5: student voice in school life."
rel,5,g5-rel-teamwork,CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g4-rel-teamwork,g6-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,deadline; fall behind; share; solve,computer-science:g5-team-project (team project roles),"Unit 5, My voice, our group. The team works on the school improvement under a deadline and solves a team problem when one member falls behind, without blame. Owner year for 3 to 5: teamwork held under a deadline when one member falls behind. Also carries AASL.III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
rds,5,g5-rds-good-for-us,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g4-rds-good-for-us,g6-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; pressure; others; choose,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. On an invented case (a friend asks the student to skip the class job to play), the student weighs both people's well-being with stop-think-choose and picks with a reason. Owner year for 3 to 5: the choice holds when friends push the other way."
sa,5,g5-sa-who-i-am,CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g4-sa-who-i-am,g6-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; change; stay; new school,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. The student shows how an invented character's identity stays and changes across a move to a new school. Owner year for 3 to 5: identity followed through a change of school. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,5,g5-sa-my-strengths,CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g4-sa-my-strengths,g6-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; new setting; language; use,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. The student plans how to use their strengths in a new setting (middle school, a new team), including a strength from a language or culture if they choose. Owner year for 3 to 5: strengths planned for a setting not yet met. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,5,g5-sa-tell-truth,CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g4-sa-tell-truth,g6-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,honest; cost; value; prize,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. The student chooses the honest action in a case where honesty costs a friendship or a prize, and names the value. Owner year for 3 to 5: honesty that costs a friendship or a prize. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
rds,5,g5-rds-ask-questions,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g4-rds-ask-questions,g6-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,different view; open; unpopular; question,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. Before a big choice in an invented case (where a class trip should go, with the character's friends all set on one place), the student uses the three kinds of question to seek out a view different from the friends' and keeps an open mind when it is unpopular. Owner year for 3 to 5: questions asked when the answer may be unpopular."
rds,5,g5-rds-solve-problems,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g4-rds-solve-problems,g6-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,community; solution; cost; act,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. The student solves a community problem the class can act on (litter at the park), weighing what each solution costs. Owner year for 3 to 5: problems beyond the school."
rds,5,g5-rds-think-ahead,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g4-rds-think-ahead,g6-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,short term; long term; many people; judge,health:g5-meh-decision-look-back (health on looking back at a decision),"Unit 6, Choices with reach. On an invented case (a class votes to turn its reading corner into a game corner), the student uses stop-think-choose to predict short- and long-term results for many people, and judges a past choice in a story. Owner year for 3 to 5: results traced for many people and over time."
rds,5,g5-rds-who-is-affected,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g4-rds-who-is-affected,g6-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,near; far; community; rule,,"Unit 6, Choices with reach. The student maps who a class choice affects near and far, including people outside the school, and which rules it touches. Owner year for 3 to 5: the map reaches people outside the school."
sm,6,g6-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g5-sm-plan-and-tidy,g7-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,planner; locker; priority; system,,"Unit 1, Finding my way. The student sets up a weekly planner for an invented first week of middle school (six classes, a locker, two clubs), compares two ways to keep materials (one binder or a folder per class), picks one and writes why it fits the week. Step up from grade 5: from one backward plan for one project to a system chosen among options across many teachers' deadlines."
sm,6,g6-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g5-sm-stay-on-task,g7-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,distraction; notification; option; trade-off,,"Unit 1, Finding my way. In a homework scene where a phone keeps buzzing with an invented group chat, the student lists three ways to stay on task (phone in another room, a timer, a study partner), weighs each against what friends expect, picks one and writes the reason, then picks a try-again option for a homework problem that will not come out (reread the worked example, try another way, ask the teacher next day). Step up from grade 5: the focus plan becomes a choice among options against an online pull."
soc,6,g6-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g5-soc-read-situation,g7-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,expectation; setting; clue; choice,,"Unit 1, Finding my way. The student reads three new-school settings (a crowded hallway, a club meeting, a lunch table) for what each asks and the chances it offers, chooses how to act in one from options on stop-think-choose, and writes the clue that decided it. Step up from grade 5: from naming what one new place will ask to choosing an action in places that ask different things."
rel,6,g6-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g5-rel-ask-offer-help,g7-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,counselor; reach out; private; fit,health:g6-s-reach-reliable-help (health on reaching reliable help and what gets in the way),"Unit 1, Finding my way. The student sizes the problems an invented sixth grader meets (a lost schedule, a stuck locker, a worry that does not go away) on the problem size meter, chooses who to ask among options (a friend, a teacher, the counselor, a message to a family adult), rehearses the ask aloud with a partner playing the chosen helper, and writes why that helper fits. Step up from grade 5: from asking one helper despite embarrassment to choosing the helper that fits the size of the problem. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sa,6,g6-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g5-sa-i-can-try,g7-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,confidence; challenge; first step; reason,,"Unit 1, Finding my way. The student plans how a character new to middle school could take on a hard first task (a band tryout, a first lab report), chooses between two first steps on the goal steps card, names one place to ask for help and writes why that step comes first. Step up from grade 5: a backup plan becomes a choice between first steps with a reason. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,6,g6-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g5-sa-who-i-am,g7-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; role; group; show,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. The student maps the roles and groups of an invented sixth grader (a team, family chores, an online game club, a language spoken at home), chooses which parts the character shows in two settings and writes why. Step up from grade 5: from how identity stays and changes across a move to choosing what to show in different groups. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,6,g6-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g5-sa-my-strengths,g7-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; asset; task; match,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. The student matches an invented character's strengths, one of them from a home language, to three new-school tasks, picks where each helps most and writes the reason. Step up from grade 5: from using strengths in one new setting to choosing which strength fits which task. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,6,g6-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g5-sa-tell-truth,g7-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,integrity; excuse; option; value,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. In a case where a character forgot a group task and friends offer an easy excuse, the student lays out the options on stop-think-choose, picks the honest one, names the value behind it and writes what it may cost with friends. Step up from grade 5: honesty chosen among tempting options, with the reason on paper. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,6,g6-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g5-sa-what-interests-me,g7-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; elective; try out; purpose,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. The student lists new interests a middle school offers (clubs, electives, a sport), weighs two against time and what friends pick, and privately chooses one to try with a written reason. Step up from grade 5: from judging one interest's worth to choosing among new ones. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,6,g6-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g5-sa-name-feelings,g7-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,intensity; scale; trigger; exact word,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. The grades 3 to 5 1 to 5 strength scale gains the exact word among close ones. The student names and rates an invented character's feelings in a first week of middle school, chooses the most exact word among close ones (nervous, uneasy, dreading) and writes the clue that fits it. Step up from grade 5: from naming mixed feelings to choosing the exact word and its strength. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,6,g6-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g5-sa-feel-think-link,g7-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought; reread; other reading; evidence,,"Unit 2, Who am I now?. The student fills the think-feel-do chain for a character who reads a friend's short reply in an invented chat as anger, writes two other possible thoughts, picks the one the facts best support and writes why. Step up from grade 5: one balanced thought becomes a choice among thoughts tested against the facts. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sm,6,g6-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g5-sm-big-feelings,g7-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,impulse; respond; option; regret,,"Unit 3, Stress and strong feelings. When an invented character is laughed at in the hallway, the student lists three responses beside the calm-down steps, weighs each against what the friends watching might think, picks one, rehearses the calm-down steps and the chosen response in a short scene, speaking as the character while the teacher reads the hallway moment from the card (no student laughs at another), and writes why it lets the feeling guide rather than drive. Step up from grade 5: the steps used to choose among responses, with onlookers as the pressure."
sm,6,g6-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g5-sm-calm-down,g7-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,stressor; coping tool; menu; fit,health:g6-meh-stress-coping (health teaches stress and coping as a health topic),"Unit 3, Stress and strong feelings. The grades 3 to 5 calm-down steps gain a fifth step, go on: stop, breathe, name it, pick a tool, go on. The student builds a menu of four coping tools, tries them in three school moments with different limits (in class, at a game, before bed with a phone nearby), and writes which tool fits which moment and why. Step up from grade 5: one tool kept for one change becomes a menu matched to settings. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress as a health topic and mental health topics are health's (health row g6-meh-stress-coping), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sa,6,g6-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g5-sa-skills-grow,g7-sa-skills-grow,,,strategy; feedback; plateau; spaced practice,,"Unit 3, Stress and strong feelings. The student reads an invented practice log of a character stuck on a skill (free throws, a hard piece of music), lists three changes (a new strategy, feedback from a coach, spaced practice), picks one and writes why the log points to it. Never belief alone. Step up from grade 5: from explaining a setback to choosing the fix among options."
rds,6,g6-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g5-rds-careful-thinking,g7-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,options; weigh; reason; rushed,,"Unit 3, Stress and strong feelings. The stop-think-choose card grows to five steps for 6 to 8: stop, list options, weigh each, choose, write the reason. The student uses it on a crowded night in a story (three assignments due, a friend asking for help the same evening) and shows in writing where careful thinking changed the choice. Step up from grade 5: careful thinking done on options laid side by side."
rds,6,g6-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g5-rds-good-for-us,g7-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; sleep; balance; count on,,"Unit 3, Stress and strong feelings. The student weighs an invented character's choice between a late online game with friends and sleep before a test, for the character's own well-being and for teammates counting on them, and writes the reason for the pick. Step up from grade 5: well-being weighed across several options, with online time as the pull."
rel,6,g6-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g5-rel-talk-listen,g7-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,paraphrase; tone; message; face to face,ela:e6-collab-discussion (the same discussion norms and paraphrase moves),"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The talk turn card adds a first step for 6 to 8: choose how to talk (in person, a call or a message). With a partner the student rehearses one hard friend talk two ways, as a typed message and face to face, and writes which fits and why tone gets lost in text. Step up from grade 5: the card used to choose the channel, not only to take turns."
rel,6,g6-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g5-rel-friendships,g7-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,friend group; drift; loyal; healthy,,"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The student weighs paths for an invented character whose friend group drifts apart in middle school (keep one old friend, join a new group, do both), with what each costs, picks one, rehearses the opening of the talk it needs with a partner playing the old friend, using the I-statement frame, and then writes the pick and the reason. Step up from grade 5: from keeping one friendship across a change to choosing among friendship paths. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's."
rel,6,g6-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g5-rel-settle-disagreements,g7-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,conflict; escalate; cool off; option,,"Unit 4, Friendships that change. With a partner the student works a disagreement in an invented group chat that is heating up, lists three ways forward (reply now, move to a face-to-face talk, wait and cool off), picks one and says the I-statement. Step up from grade 5: the steps used with a choice of how and when to settle. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,6,g6-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g5-rel-say-no,g7-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal; reason; way out; delay,health:g6-t-when-careful-decision (health applies careful choosing to nicotine pressure),"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The refusal steps grow into three options: a plain no, a no with a reason, and a way out of the place. The student rehearses choosing among them against a card character who pushes them to skip class with friends, and writes which option fits and why. Step up from grade 5: from holding one no to choosing the refusal that fits. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, body-safety and dating topics are health's (health row g6-t-when-careful-decision takes the nicotine cases)."
soc,6,g6-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g5-soc-their-view,g7-soc-their-view,,,perspective; assume; ask; side,ela:e6-narrator-pov (how a narrator's point of view shapes what a reader knows),"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The student takes the view of an invented friend who was left out of weekend plans, lists what that friend might be thinking, and chooses one question to ask before taking a side, with the reason. Step up from grade 5: one friend's view held while choosing how to check it."
soc,6,g6-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g5-soc-read-feelings,g7-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,body language; message; read; check in,,"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The student reads a friend's feelings from clues in person and in an invented message thread (short replies, no emoji), lists possible readings, chooses how to check in and writes why. Step up from grade 5: clues read across channels, with the check-in chosen among options."
soc,6,g6-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g5-soc-care-when-struggle,g7-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,empathy; support; space; kind of care,,"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The student chooses among ways to show care to a story friend having a hard week (sit with them, send a message, give space, tell an adult) and writes why that way fits this friend. Step up from grade 5: from acting the care steps to choosing the kind of care. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,6,g6-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g5-soc-thankful,g7-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; specific; public; private,,"Unit 4, Friendships that change. The student chooses how to thank an invented friend who stood by them through a change (in person, a note, in front of others) and writes why that way suits the friend. Step up from grade 5: thanks given through a chosen channel."
soc,6,g6-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g5-soc-fair-rules,g7-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; exclude; online; fair,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student uses the three fairness questions on invented norms of a group online and offline (an invite-only group chat, a saved-seats rule at lunch), chooses which norm to change and how, and writes the reason. They set it beside the settled history met in grade 5 to compare how an unfair norm was changed. Step up from grade 5: the questions turned on norms students this age meet, with a chosen action. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content."
soc,6,g6-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g5-soc-why-rules,g7-soc-why-rules,,,media; norm; likes; streak,health:g6-phw-influences-interact (health on how media and peers shape health choices),"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. Media and online norms begin here. The student traces how features of an invented video app (likes, streaks, shares) shape what people post, compares two rules the app could have, and writes which would push people toward kinder posts and why. Step up from grade 5: from school and town systems to the media and online systems the age group meets. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rds,6,g6-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g5-rds-ask-questions,g7-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; source; open; viral,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. Before sharing a viral claim in an invented feed, the student writes three questions to ask, looks for a view that disagrees, and chooses whether to share, wait or check, with the reason. Step up from grade 5: questions chosen to test a claim before acting online."
rds,6,g6-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g5-rds-check-facts,g7-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,claim; data; sample; summary,math:m6-summarize-data (summarizing a data set before drawing a conclusion),"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student checks a claim like ""everyone in sixth grade has a phone"" against an invented class data set, chooses which evidence counts, and writes a decision with the reason. Step up from grade 5: numbers summarized and chosen among, not only read."
rds,6,g6-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g5-rds-think-ahead,g7-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,consequence; screenshot; lasting; future,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student predicts short- and long-term results of three options for an invented post (a joke photo of a friend), including that a screenshot can last, and chooses with the reason written. Step up from grade 5: results weighed across options online, where a post can travel."
sa,6,g6-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g5-sa-snap-judgments,g7-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assumption; profile; first impression; check,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student notices a snap judgment made from an invented online profile, lists ways to check it (meet the person, ask, look for more facts), chooses one and writes why. Step up from grade 5: the check chosen among options for first impressions online and off. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
soc,6,g6-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g5-soc-notice-good,g7-soc-notice-good,,,credit; comment; specific; public,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student writes specific credit for a classmate's work on an invented class project page and chooses between public and private praise, with a reason. Step up from grade 5: credit given in a chosen setting, online included."
rds,6,g6-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g5-rds-who-is-affected,g7-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,audience; share; reach; rule,,"Unit 5, Online and offline norms. The student maps who an invented post could reach (friends, strangers, the person in it, the school) and which school rules it touches, then picks among options with the reason. Step up from grade 5: the map of who is affected reaches an online audience."
sm,6,g6-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g5-sm-set-goal,g7-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; alone; with others; term,health:g6-phw-goal-alone-or-with (health on goals pursued alone or with support),"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The student sets a goal for the term on the goal steps card, chooses between working at it alone or with a partner, weighs it against time for clubs and friends, and writes the reason. Step up from grade 5: choosing how to pursue a goal, not only tracking it."
sm,6,g6-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g5-sm-step-forward,g7-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; volunteer; role; first step,,"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The student chooses among three ways an invented sixth grader could step forward in a team project (offer a plan, take a role nobody wants, draw in a quiet member) and writes why, then plans the first step on the goal steps card. Step up from grade 5: starting by choosing among roles."
sm,6,g6-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g5-sm-have-a-say,g7-sm-have-a-say,,,proposal; route; audience; carry out,,"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The class drafts a proposal to improve a sixth-grade routine (the lunch line, locker time); the student weighs two ways to present it (a letter, a short talk to the student council) and carries out a part. Step up from grade 5: student voice in school life with a chosen route."
rel,6,g6-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g5-rel-different-ways,g7-rel-different-ways,,,custom; habit; adjust; include,physical-education:g6-welcome-differences (respecting differences among teammates),"Unit 6, Goals and our team. In an invented mixed team from different elementary schools and backgrounds, the student chooses among ways to settle a clash of habits (how to split work, when to talk) and writes the reason. Step up from grade 5: respect shown by choosing how to adjust. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
rel,6,g6-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g5-rel-teamwork,g7-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,role; deadline; fair share; split,computer-science:g6-inclusive-collab (pair roles and giving credit in a team),"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The team chooses how to divide a project among options (by strength, by rotation, by choice) and solves a time problem when a member has a game the night before the deadline, writing why the split was fair. Step up from grade 5: the split itself chosen and defended."
rel,6,g6-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g5-rel-lead-follow,g7-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,leader; consensus; vote; follow,,"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The student leads a team choice by picking a way to decide (a vote, consensus, the leader decides) that fits the decision, writes why, then follows a decision they argued against. Step up from grade 5: leading by choosing how the group decides."
rel,6,g6-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g5-rel-stand-up,g7-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; options; report; group chat,"health:g6-v-bullying-harassment-help (health on bullying, cyberbullying and getting help)","Unit 6, Goals and our team. The grades 3 to 5 upstander plan gains ""report online"", for four options (speak up, support the person afterward, get an adult, report online). The student chooses the safest for an invented case of a student mocked in a team chat, rehearses the chosen words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the one posting the mockery (the I-statement frame for a speak-up, the plain facts for a report), and writes why. Step up from grade 5: from one planned speak-up to a choice among upstander options. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
rds,6,g6-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g5-rds-solve-problems,g7-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,solution; criteria; trade-off; pilot,,"Unit 6, Goals and our team. The class picks a school problem (a crowded hallway between classes); the student lists solutions on stop-think-choose, sizes the problem on the meter, weighs solutions against criteria (cost, time, who it helps) and writes the pick and reason. Step up from grade 5: solutions weighed against stated criteria."
sa,7,g7-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g6-sa-who-i-am,g8-sa-who-i-am,,,belong; insider; outsider; group,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. The student compares how two invented seventh graders see the same group (one belongs to the team, one is new to it), and chooses how each could show who they are there, with the reason. Grade 7 adds the other person's view of belonging. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,7,g7-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g6-sa-my-strengths,g8-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; notice; contribute; feedback,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. The student sets a partner's list of an invented teammate's strengths beside the teammate's own list, and chooses which strength the group should lean on, writing why. New this year: how others see a strength. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,7,g7-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g6-sa-what-interests-me,g8-sa-what-interests-me,,,shared interest; pull; own choice; purpose,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. Privately the student weighs an interest a friend group shares against one only they care about, and writes which to give time to and why. What grade 7 adds: another person's pull on the choice. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
rel,7,g7-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g6-rel-friendships,g8-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,trust; balance; new group; both sides,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. When one invented friend joins a new group, the student writes how the friendship looks from each friend's side, chooses what to say with the I-statement frame, rehearses it with a partner playing the friend who joined the new group, and writes why. This year the friend's side is weighed with one's own. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's."
rel,7,g7-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g6-rel-different-ways,g8-rel-different-ways,,,perspective; custom; direct; adapt,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. In an invented mixed team, the student takes the other partner's view of a clash over how blunt feedback should be, chooses how both could adapt, and writes the reason. Grade 7 asks students to see their own habits as a partner might. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
soc,7,g7-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g6-soc-notice-good,g8-soc-notice-good,,,overlooked; quiet; credit; land,,"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. The student notices strengths in an invented group member others overlook, imagines how that member would hear praise, and chooses words that will land. The other person's view now shapes the credit."
rel,7,g7-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g6-rel-teamwork,g8-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,role; feedback; listen; adjust,physical-education:g7-game-plans-feedback (specific feedback on a team plan),"Unit 1, Belonging and our groups. The team gives and takes feedback on a shared plan; the student hears a teammate's view of how the work was split, changes one role and writes why. New in grade 7: a teammate's sense of fairness reshapes the plan."
rel,7,g7-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g6-rel-say-no,g8-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,pressure; peer; alternative; keep the friend,health:g7-aod-turn-down-requests (health applies refusal to alcohol and drug requests),"Unit 2, Under pressure. The student rehearses the refusal options against a card character in an invented group chat who presses them to forward a mean meme that mocks a student's drawing, then thinks through how the pushing friend sees it and offers an alternative that keeps the friendship. What changes: the other side's view helps pick the refusal. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, body-safety and dating topics are health's (health row g7-aod-turn-down-requests takes the substance cases)."
soc,7,g7-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g6-soc-read-situation,g8-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,cue; stakes; expectation; other eyes,,"Unit 2, Under pressure. The student reads an invented situation (an invitation from older students to sit with them at a game, a group dare at the park) from their own side and from a parent's or friend's side, chooses how to act on stop-think-choose, and writes what each side would want. Grade 7 reads the moment through someone else's eyes."
soc,7,g7-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g6-soc-why-rules,g8-soc-why-rules,,,trend; spread; norm; target,,"Unit 2, Under pressure. The student traces how an invented trend spreads on a video app and how it looks to the person the trend makes fun of, then writes which app rule would change what people do. This year adds the view of the person a norm hurts. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rds,7,g7-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g6-rds-think-ahead,g8-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,consequence; ripple; prank; other end,,"Unit 2, Under pressure. The student predicts how a choice under pressure (joining an invented prank on a substitute teacher) plays out for themselves and for the person on the other end, and chooses with the reason written. Grade 7 puts the other person's results into the weighing."
sm,7,g7-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g6-sm-stay-on-task,g8-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; pull; set break; respect,,"Unit 2, Under pressure. During an invented study session with a friend who wants to chat, the student chooses a focus option that also respects the friend (a set break, a shared timer) and a try-again option for the hardest part of the study task (try another way, explain it to the friend), and writes why on the goal steps card. The pull is now a person whose view counts."
sa,7,g7-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g6-sa-snap-judgments,g8-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,bias; stereotype; assumption; evidence,,"Unit 3, Snap judgments. Main year for snap judgments in 6 to 8 (STANDARD section 3). The student notices a snap judgment in an invented case (a character decides a new student will not fit the team because of how they dress), writes how the judged student would experience the guess, and checks it against evidence before choosing what to do. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
soc,7,g7-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g6-soc-their-view,g8-soc-their-view,,,motive; interpret; misread; both views,ela:e7-contrast-pov (contrasting the points of view of characters),"Unit 3, Snap judgments. The student retells an invented clash from the view of each person in it, names the motive each might have, and chooses what to ask before judging. Grade 7 holds two full points of view at once, where grade 6 held one."
soc,7,g7-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g6-soc-fair-rules,g8-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; exclusion; history; fair change,,"Unit 3, Snap judgments. Main year for unfair norms in 6 to 8. The student uses the three fairness questions on an invented club rule and on a settled case the courts have ruled on (school segregation, struck down in 1954), takes the view of the people each norm left out, and chooses a fair change with the reason. Here the second person is the one the norm left out. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content."
rds,7,g7-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g6-rds-ask-questions,g8-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,open mind; other side; best case; curious,,"Unit 3, Snap judgments. Before a class vote the student talks with a partner who holds the other view, writes the best version of that view, and decides whether it changes their own, with the reason. New this year: the other side's case written out in full."
rds,7,g7-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g6-rds-check-facts,g8-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,sample; representative; claim; believe,math:m7-random-samples (a random sample standing for a population),"Unit 3, Snap judgments. The student checks two invented claims about what most seventh graders think against a small random sample, decides which claim the data back, and writes why each person might have believed theirs. Grade 7 adds why the other person believes what they do."
sa,7,g7-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g6-sa-name-feelings,g8-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,hidden feeling; show; inside; outside,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student names an invented character's inside feeling and the feeling the character shows, as another character would see it, rates both on the scale, and chooses words to ask about the gap. Grade 7 asks what one person's feeling looks like to another. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,7,g7-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g6-sa-feel-think-link,g8-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,interpretation; assumption; reframe; each side,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student fills two think-feel-do chains for the same invented moment, one for each person in it, and writes how each person's thought changed their feeling and action. This year the chain runs for both people. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sm,7,g7-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g6-sm-big-feelings,g8-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,trigger; effect; respond; land,physical-education:g7-manage-feelings (managing frustration and stress in games),"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. In an invented scene where a teammate's mistake loses a game, the student uses the calm-down steps, chooses a response, and writes how the teammate would hear each option. What grade 7 adds: how a response lands on the other person."
sm,7,g7-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g6-sm-calm-down,g8-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,support; signal; tool; partner,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student coaches a partner through choosing a tool from the menu for an invented stressful moment, notices the partner may need a different tool than their own, and writes how two friends could agree on a calm signal. New in grade 7: helping another person choose. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress, sleep and mental health topics are health's (health row g6-meh-stress-coping), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sa,7,g7-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g6-sa-tell-truth,g8-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,honesty; loyalty; cover; trust,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student chooses the true action in an invented case where a friend asks them to cover for a broken rule, writes how the friend and the teacher each see it, and names what honesty costs. Grade 7 sets honesty against loyalty to another person. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,7,g7-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g6-sa-i-can-try,g8-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,doubt; encourage; steps; ask,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. When an invented friend doubts they can pass a hard test, the student names two steps the friend could take and one place to ask for help on the goal steps card, and writes what they would say to encourage. Grade 7 turns the skill outward, to another person's doubt. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,7,g7-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g6-sa-skills-grow,g8-sa-skills-grow,,,feedback; coach; practice plan; improve,,"Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student reads feedback a coach gave an invented player, takes the coach's view of what to change, and chooses the practice plan with the reason. Never belief alone. This year the fix starts from someone else's feedback."
rel,7,g7-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g6-rel-talk-listen,g8-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,active listening; summarize; clarify; heard,ela:e7-collab-discussion (acknowledging others' ideas and modifying one's own in discussion),"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student listens to a partner's account of an invented misunderstanding and sums it up until the partner agrees, then gives their own side with the talk turn card. Grade 7 makes the other person the judge of whether they were heard."
rel,7,g7-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g6-rel-settle-disagreements,g8-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,repair; apology; interests; both sides,health:g7-v-conflict-prevent-end (health on preventing and ending conflict),"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. With a partner the student works an invented conflict by naming each side's interests, choosing a repair (an apology, a fix, a new plan), and saying the I-statement. New this year: the other side's interests shape the repair. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,7,g7-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g6-rel-ask-offer-help,g8-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,notice; offer; accept; bring in,health:g7-meh-seek-and-give-support (health on seeking and giving support),"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student notices an invented friend who is struggling but has not asked, sizes the problem on the meter, chooses how to offer help, rehearses the offer with a partner playing the friend (who first turns it down), knows when to bring in an adult even if the friend says no, and writes why the chosen offer fits. Grade 7 is about offering from the other person's side. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Self-harm and crisis topics are health's (health row g7-meh-self-harm-help)."
soc,7,g7-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g6-soc-care-when-struggle,g8-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,empathy; ask first; space; welcome,,"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. Before acting, the student asks what an invented struggling friend wants (company, help, space), and writes how the friend would feel about each kind of care. The friend's own wish now decides the care. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,7,g7-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g6-soc-read-feelings,g8-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,signal; mixed message; both people; respect,,"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student reads the feelings of both people in an invented argument from their clues, checks in with each, and chooses words that respect each side. Grade 7 reads two people at once."
soc,7,g7-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g6-soc-thankful,g8-soc-thankful,,,thanks; acknowledge; sincere; repair,,"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. After an invented conflict is settled, the student thanks the other person for something they did in the repair, choosing words that person will take as sincere. New in grade 7: thanks as part of repair, aimed at how the other person hears it."
rel,7,g7-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g6-rel-stand-up,g8-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; target; support; follow up,health:g7-s-spot-speak-up (health on spotting chances to speak up),"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student plans how to support the person treated unfairly in an invented case, asks what that person wants before acting, and chooses the upstander option they would welcome. The student rehearses the check-in question and the chosen words with the teacher, who plays the person treated unfairly and then the unkind act, while a partner watches for the steps; then the student writes why that option fits. Grade 7 adds the target's own view. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
rel,7,g7-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g6-rel-lead-follow,g8-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,facilitate; neutral; disagreement; follow,,"Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student leads a group through an invented disagreement as a neutral guide with the talk turn card, making sure each side is heard, then follows the group's choice. This year the leader holds everyone's view, not their own."
sm,7,g7-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g6-sm-set-goal,g8-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,shared goal; check-in; adjust; time,health:g7-phw-write-goal-link (health writes measurable goals),"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student sets a shared goal with a partner on the goal steps card, weighs both partners' time against it, and changes the plan after a check-in where the partner's needs differ. Grade 7: a partner's needs reshape the choice."
sm,7,g7-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g6-sm-plan-and-tidy,g8-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,timeline; depend; buffer; delegate,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student plans an invented team event timeline where others' tasks depend on theirs, builds in a buffer, and writes how a late teammate changes the plan. What grade 7 adds: depending on other people."
sm,7,g7-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g6-sm-step-forward,g8-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; invite; include; ownership,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student starts a class project and invites a classmate who would not join on their own, writing how the project looks from that classmate's side. New this year: bringing another person in."
sm,7,g7-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g6-sm-have-a-say,g8-sm-have-a-say,,,stakeholder; proposal; feedback; revise,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The class drafts a proposal for a school change, and the student asks two groups it affects (younger students, staff) what they think and changes the proposal with the reason. Grade 7: the proposal answers other people's views."
rds,7,g7-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g6-rds-solve-problems,g8-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,root cause; view; solution; test,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student solves a school problem with an invented group that disagrees on its cause, records each person's view of the cause, and chooses a solution to test, writing the reason. Other people's views of the cause enter this year."
rds,7,g7-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g6-rds-careful-thinking,g8-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,pause; partner; reasons; hold,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student runs the five-step stop-think-choose with a partner who prefers a different option, and writes how hearing the partner's reasons changed or held the choice. Careful thinking now takes in another person's reasons."
rds,7,g7-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g6-rds-good-for-us,g8-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; family; team; both sides,,"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student weighs an invented choice between a weekend team practice and a family chore day, for their own well-being and for the people on each side, writing how each would see it. Grade 7 gives the other people's well-being its own column."
rds,7,g7-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g6-rds-who-is-affected,g8-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakeholder; impact; intended; unintended,computer-science:g7-impact-history (intended and unintended effects on people),"Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student maps who a class decision affects, then asks a partner playing one affected group for an effect they missed, and chooses with the reason. Grade 7 adds a voice from the affected side."
rds,8,g8-rds-ask-questions,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g7-rds-ask-questions,g9-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,inquiry; source; open-minded; side,ela:e8-conflicting-viewpoints (answering conflicting viewpoints),"Unit 1, How I decide. The student questions two invented sources that disagree on a school issue, stays open while friends have already picked a side, and writes a choice and reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: openness kept when it costs standing with friends."
rds,8,g8-rds-check-facts,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g7-rds-check-facts,g9-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,misinformation; verify; viral; evidence,health:g8-phw-handle-false-messages (health on handling false health messages),"Unit 1, How I decide. The student checks a fast-spreading invented rumor against facts and numbers before a deadline to respond, and chooses on the evidence when the popular view leans the other way, writing the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: evidence weighed under time and social pressure."
rds,8,g8-rds-think-ahead,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g7-rds-think-ahead,g9-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,trade-off; long term; risk; judge,health:g8-pa-decision-look-back (health on judging a decision afterward),"Unit 1, How I decide. The student weighs short- and long-term costs of options in an invented case (quitting a team for more free time), chooses, and later judges the choice against how the story turns out. Owner year for 6 to 8: a choice where every option costs something."
rds,8,g8-rds-careful-thinking,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g7-rds-careful-thinking,g9-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,time limit; impulse; process; reflect,,"Unit 1, How I decide. The student runs stop-think-choose with a two-minute limit on an invented case, then reflects in writing on where the clock bent the thinking and what a longer pause would have changed. Owner year for 6 to 8: careful thinking against a clock."
sa,8,g8-sa-feel-think-link,CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g7-sa-feel-think-link,g9-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought trap; pressure; slow down; choose,,"Unit 1, How I decide. The student fills the think-feel-do chain for a character pushed to decide fast (an invented group chat demanding an answer now), writes a thought that slows the chain, and chooses. Owner year for 6 to 8: the chain changed while pressure pushes. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
rds,8,g8-rds-who-is-affected,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g7-rds-who-is-affected,g9-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,community; ripple; rule; popular,,"Unit 1, How I decide. The student maps who a choice affects near and far when it is popular with friends but costs another group, and writes the pick with the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: weighing everyone affected when it costs popularity."
sa,8,g8-sa-tell-truth,CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g7-sa-tell-truth,g9-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,integrity; consequence; stand; value,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student chooses the true action in an invented case where honesty costs a team its win (a missed call in its favor), and names the value behind it and the price. Owner year for 6 to 8: honesty with a real loss attached. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,8,g8-sa-who-i-am,CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g7-sa-who-i-am,g9-sa-who-i-am,,,values; identity; consistent; group push,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student shows how an invented eighth grader keeps their values across groups that want different things, and chooses what to hold to when one group pushes. Owner year for 6 to 8: identity held under group pressure. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,8,g8-sa-my-strengths,CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g7-sa-my-strengths,g9-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; risk; show; setting,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student plans how an invented character could use a strength where showing it costs something (being called a try-hard), and writes the choice and reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: a strength used despite a social cost. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,8,g8-sa-snap-judgments,CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g7-sa-snap-judgments,g9-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assumption; group view; check; fair,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student checks a snap judgment an invented friend group already shares about a new student, chooses a fair action though disagreeing costs standing, and writes the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: a fair action chosen against a group's shared guess. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,8,g8-rel-say-no,CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g7-rel-say-no,g9-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal; exit plan; values; cost,health:g8-aod-refusal-and-driving (health applies refusal to unsafe rides),"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student rehearses the refusal options against a card character who adds a price (being left out of a weekend plan, an invented post mocking them), and writes an exit plan tied to their values. Owner year for 6 to 8: a no held when it costs. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, body-safety and dating topics are health's (health row g8-aod-refusal-and-driving takes the unsafe-ride case)."
soc,8,g8-soc-read-situation,CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g7-soc-read-situation,g9-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakes; options; stay; leave,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student reads an invented situation whose stakes rise (a game at the park turns into a dare), chooses on stop-think-choose whether to stay, change it or leave, and writes what each costs. Owner year for 6 to 8: reading a moment as the pressure climbs."
sa,8,g8-sa-what-interests-me,CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g7-sa-what-interests-me,g9-sa-what-interests-me,,,purpose; commitment; give up; worth,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. Privately the student weighs whether an interest is worth what it takes (dropping another activity, time with friends) and writes a purpose for keeping or dropping it. Owner year for 6 to 8: keep or drop weighed against real costs, in writing. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,8,g8-sa-i-can-try,CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g7-sa-i-can-try,g9-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,risk; public; plan; support,,"Unit 2, Values and integrity. The student plans how an invented character could take on a challenge where failing would be public (a solo at a concert), with two steps, a helper and what to do if it goes wrong, on the goal steps card. Owner year for 6 to 8: belief acted on when failure has a cost. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,8,g8-sa-name-feelings,CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g7-sa-name-feelings,g9-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,stress signal; overwhelmed; rising; name,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. The student names and rates the feelings of an invented student through a heavy week (tests, a game, a family event), choosing exact words as the strength rises, and writes the clue for each. Owner year for 6 to 8: feelings named as pressure builds. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sa,8,g8-sa-skills-grow,CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g7-sa-skills-grow,g9-sa-skills-grow,,,practice; rest; plateau; help,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. The student plans practice for an invented skill when time is short, choosing among more practice, better practice and asking for help, and writes why rest belongs in the plan. Never belief alone. Owner year for 6 to 8: growth planned under a time cost. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sm,8,g8-sm-big-feelings,CASEL.Self-Management.1,g7-sm-big-feelings,g9-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,high stakes; respond; recover; cost,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. In a high-stakes invented scene (a tryout, a public mistake), the student uses the calm-down steps, picks a response from options, rehearses the steps and the response in a short scene, and writes what reacting would cost and how to recover. Owner year for 6 to 8: a response chosen when the stakes are high, with a plan to recover."
sm,8,g8-sm-calm-down,CASEL.Self-Management.2,g7-sm-calm-down,g9-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,routine; sleep; screen time; coping plan,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. The student builds a weekly coping plan for an invented busy student that weighs sleep, screen time and practice, chooses tools from the menu, and writes what each choice costs. Owner year for 6 to 8: coping chosen when every option takes time. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress, sleep and mental health topics are health's (health rows g6-meh-stress-coping and g7-phw-sleep-habits-community), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,8,g8-sm-stay-on-task,CASEL.Self-Management.3,g7-sm-stay-on-task,g9-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; deadline; put off; self-check,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. The student keeps an invented long project on track through a week with a game and a group chat that will not quiet down, chooses what to put off and writes the cost, and makes a try-again plan for the hardest part of the project, chosen from options (try another way, break it smaller, ask for help) with the reason written. Owner year for 6 to 8: focus held against both time and friends, and through the hard part. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sm,8,g8-sm-plan-and-tidy,CASEL.Self-Management.5,g7-sm-plan-and-tidy,g9-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,schedule; overload; priority; cut,,"Unit 3, Stress, focus and rest. The student plans an invented week where commitments clash (homework, a team, a family chore), decides what to cut or move, and writes the cost of each choice. Owner year for 6 to 8: planning when not everything fits."
soc,8,g8-soc-their-view,CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g7-soc-their-view,g9-soc-their-view,,,perspective; values; disagree; respect,health:g8-meh-different-views (health on respecting different views),"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student states the view of an invented person whose values differ on a school issue well enough that the person would agree with the summary, even when friends mock that view. Owner year for 6 to 8: taking a view that costs something to take."
soc,8,g8-soc-care-when-struggle,CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g7-soc-care-when-struggle,g9-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,concern; ongoing; support; adult,health:g8-meh-express-concern (health on expressing concern for a friend),"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student plans care for an invented friend whose hard time goes on, including when caring costs time or the friend's annoyance, and when to bring in an adult. Owner year for 6 to 8: care kept up over time, with the point where an adult comes in. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g8-meh-suicide-help)."
soc,8,g8-soc-read-feelings,CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g7-soc-read-feelings,g9-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,subtle cue; joke; context; respect,,"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student reads feelings in an invented group conversation where cues hide behind jokes and short replies, chooses among responses one that may cost a laugh but respects the person, and writes the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: cues read under the group's laughter."
soc,8,g8-soc-fair-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g7-soc-fair-rules,g9-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; civil rights; price; change,social-studies:ss8-civil-rights-laws (the civil rights laws that changed unfair norms),"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student uses the fairness questions on a settled case the courts have ruled on, names what changing the norm cost the people who did it, and sets beside it an invented school norm that someone would pay a price to change. Owner year for 6 to 8: the price of changing a norm, in settled history and in school. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (ss8-civil-rights-laws)."
rel,8,g8-rel-different-ways,CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g7-rel-different-ways,g9-rel-different-ways,,,difference; common ground; give up; deadline,,"Unit 4, Understanding others. In an invented team whose partners' habits clash with a deadline close, the student chooses how to find common ground and writes what each side gives up. Owner year for 6 to 8: respect kept when time is short. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
soc,8,g8-soc-notice-good,CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g7-soc-notice-good,g9-soc-notice-good,,,credit; rival; generous; specific,,"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student gives specific credit to an invented rival team or classmate even when it costs a little pride, and writes why it matters. Owner year for 6 to 8: credit given to a rival, at a cost to pride."
soc,8,g8-soc-why-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g7-soc-why-rules,g9-soc-why-rules,,,system; incentive; media; influence,social-studies:ss8-news-literacy (how news media shape what people know),"Unit 4, Understanding others. The student traces how an invented school system and an invented app each reward some behavior, names the pressure each puts on students, and writes one change and what it would cost. Owner year for 6 to 8: a school system and an online one weighed side by side, with the cost of a change. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rel,8,g8-rel-talk-listen,CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g7-rel-talk-listen,g9-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,assertive; respectful; channel; plain,ela:e8-collab-discussion (qualifying and justifying views in discussion),"Unit 5, Communication and conflict. The student uses the talk turn card in an invented hard talk where speaking plainly may cost a friendship, choosing the channel and the words, and writes the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: the channel and the words chosen when plain talk may cost a friendship."
rel,8,g8-rel-friendships,CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g7-rel-friendships,g9-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,healthy; unhealthy; limit; step back,,"Unit 5, Communication and conflict. The student weighs an invented friendship where staying close costs other friends or values, chooses whether to set a limit or step back, rehearses the I-statement with a partner playing the friend, and writes the choice and its reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: a friendship choice with a price on each side. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's."
rel,8,g8-rel-settle-disagreements,CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g7-rel-settle-disagreements,g9-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,negotiate; compromise; interest; deal,health:g8-v-negotiate-compromise (health on negotiating a compromise),"Unit 5, Communication and conflict. With a partner the student negotiates an invented disagreement where any deal costs both sides, chooses a compromise and writes why it is fair. Owner year for 6 to 8: a negotiated deal where both sides pay. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,8,g8-rel-ask-offer-help,CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g7-rel-ask-offer-help,g9-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,reluctant; trusted adult; worry; follow through,,"Unit 5, Communication and conflict. The student plans asking for help when it costs (admitting a mistake, a friend's secret that worries them) and offering help that takes real time, sizing each on the meter and choosing whom to go to from options, rehearses each ask aloud with a partner playing the adult or the friend, and writes the reason for the choice. Owner year for 6 to 8: help sought and given when it has a price. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. A worry about a friend's safety goes to an adult, even when the friend asked for a secret."
soc,8,g8-soc-thankful,CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g7-soc-thankful,g9-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; sincere; timing; sacrifice,,"Unit 5, Communication and conflict. The student thanks someone in an invented case whose help cost them (a teammate who gave up a spot), choosing words and timing. Owner year for 6 to 8: thanks for help that had a price."
sm,8,g8-sm-set-goal,CASEL.Self-Management.4,g7-sm-set-goal,g9-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,long-term goal; monitor; setback; give up,health:g8-phw-goal-monitor (health on monitoring and adjusting a goal),"Unit 6, Making a difference. The student sets a term goal, monitors it on the goal steps card, and adjusts when it competes with friends and time, writing what was given up; the goal is checked at the unit's end and carried on as the unit's carry-out cue. Owner year for 6 to 8: a goal kept against friends and time, with the trade written down."
sm,8,g8-sm-step-forward,CASEL.Self-Management.6,g7-sm-step-forward,g9-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; risk; recruit; persist,,"Unit 6, Making a difference. For a school improvement that may cost time and draw teasing, the student chooses between two first steps (ask the student council, or start a sign-up sheet), writes why the chosen one fits the cost, starts it on the goal steps card and recruits others. Owner year for 6 to 8: starting when starting is costly."
sm,8,g8-sm-have-a-say,CASEL.Self-Management.7,g7-sm-have-a-say,g9-sm-have-a-say,,,advocacy; objection; stakeholder; follow through,social-studies:ss8-civic-action (deliberating and carrying out an informed action),"Unit 6, Making a difference. The class proposes and carries out a school improvement; the student weighs its costs for different groups, answers objections and does a part. Owner year for 6 to 8: student voice in school life, with no cause picked by the course."
rel,8,g8-rel-teamwork,CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g7-rel-teamwork,g9-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,accountability; absence; deadline; no blame,health:g8-meh-teamwork (health on team roles and cooperation),"Unit 6, Making a difference. The team carries the project under a real deadline; when a member's absence costs everyone, the student chooses among three ways to cover the absent member's part (share it out, cut its scope, ask for more time) and writes the reason, without blame. Owner year for 6 to 8: a gap in the team covered by a weighed choice. Also carries AASL.III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
rel,8,g8-rel-lead-follow,CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g7-rel-lead-follow,g9-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,leadership; unpopular; trust; follow,physical-education:g8-leading (leading a group in activity),"Unit 6, Making a difference. The student leads a decision that will be unpopular with part of the team and follows a leader they disagree with, writing what each cost. Owner year for 6 to 8: leading and following at a cost to the team's goodwill."
rel,8,g8-rel-stand-up,CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g7-rel-stand-up,g9-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; risk; safe option; report,,"Unit 6, Making a difference. On an invented case where speaking up costs standing with friends (the group keeps replaying a classmate's stumble from a school video), the student chooses the safest of the upstander options, rehearses the words with a partner, using the I-statement frame, while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, and writes why that option fits the cost. Owner year for 6 to 8: speaking up chosen and weighed when it costs standing with friends. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
rds,8,g8-rds-solve-problems,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g7-rds-solve-problems,g9-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,community; criteria; trade-off; review,,"Unit 6, Making a difference. The class solves a community problem it can act on, weighs solutions against cost and time on stop-think-choose, chooses one and judges it afterward. Owner year for 6 to 8: problems where the fix has a cost."
rds,8,g8-rds-good-for-us,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g7-rds-good-for-us,g9-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; community; want; balance,,"Unit 6, Making a difference. The student weighs a choice for their own and others' well-being when it costs something they want (a trip with friends against a promise to help a neighbor), and writes the reason. Owner year for 6 to 8: well-being weighed when it costs something wanted."
sm,9,g9-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g8-sm-plan-and-tidy,g10-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,system; calendar; trade-off; review,,"Unit 1, A new start. The goal steps card grows for 9 to 12 into a plan the student designs: the goal, dated steps, the trade-off accepted, a check date and a verdict on how well it worked. The student designs a planning system for an invented first month of high school (seven classes, a team, a long-term project), names what the system gives up (a paper planner is slower, a phone calendar distracts), follows it through a two-week case calendar and judges what held and what to change. Step up from grade 8: from cutting and moving clashing commitments to building and testing a whole system."
sm,9,g9-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g8-sm-stay-on-task,g10-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus plan; long-term; check-in; adjust,,"Unit 1, A new start. The student designs a focus plan for an invented ninth grader with a project due in three weeks and no one checking daily: chosen work blocks, what to do about the phone, a self check-in each Friday. The student writes the trade-off of the plan and, after reading how the character's first week went, judges which part worked and rewrites one. Step up from grade 8: the student builds the focus plan rather than choosing what to put off in one week."
soc,9,g9-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g8-soc-read-situation,g10-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,setting; expectation; opportunity; look back,,"Unit 1, A new start. The stop-think-choose card gains a sixth step for 9 to 12: look back, judge how the choice worked and what to change. The student reads three invented high-school settings (a first lab with a new partner, a club fair, a hallway where older students gather), plans how to act in each with what each choice opens and closes, then judges the plan against how the case turned out. Step up from grade 8: from reading rising stakes in one moment to planning across settings and judging the plan."
rel,9,g9-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g8-rel-ask-offer-help,g10-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,support map; resource; counselor; follow up,health:g9-meh-help-and-concern (health applies asking for and giving help to health concerns),"Unit 1, A new start. The problem size meter grows into a support map for 9 to 12: each size of problem beside the people and offices that fit it, in school and out. The student builds a support map for an invented new high-school student (a missed assignment, a schedule error, a worry that keeps coming back), plans how to ask in each case and what to do if the first helper cannot help, rehearses one ask aloud with a partner playing the adult or office, then judges whether the map covers the cases. Step up from grade 8: from planning one costly ask to designing a map of where help is. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
sa,9,g9-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g8-sa-i-can-try,g10-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,challenge; plan; setback; evidence,,"Unit 1, A new start. The student designs a plan for an invented ninth grader taking on a course that feels too hard: two first steps, a place to ask for help, what the character will count as progress, and the trade-off (less free time). After reading the character's first test result, the student judges the plan and names one change. Step up from grade 8: belief turned into a designed plan with its own test. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,9,g9-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g8-sa-what-interests-me,g10-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; purpose; explore; trade-off,computer-science:g9-career-interests (listing a goal and the skills it needs),"Unit 1, A new start. Privately the student plans how to explore two interests a high school offers (a course, a club, a job shadow day), with what each costs in time and what it might give, then after a set date judges whether the plan helped them find a purpose. Step up from grade 8: from weighing one interest's worth to designing a small test of interests. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,9,g9-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g8-sa-name-feelings,g10-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,pattern; trigger; strength; early sign,,"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The feelings scale adds two parts for 9 to 12: what set the feeling off and what it pushes toward, so a plan can meet it early. The student charts an invented student's feelings across a school week (a test, a missed bus, good news), finds a pattern and plans where an early sign could be caught, then judges whether the chart showed enough to plan from. Step up from grade 8: from naming feelings as pressure builds to reading a pattern and planning for it. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sm,9,g9-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g8-sm-big-feelings,g10-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,response plan; trigger; recover; review,,"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The student designs a response plan for an invented student who snaps at people after hard practices: the trigger, the early sign on the feelings scale, a calm-down step, a planned response and a repair if it goes wrong. The student names what the plan costs (leaving a moment early) and judges it against a second scene. Step up from grade 8: from choosing one response in a high-stakes moment to a plan made ahead."
sm,9,g9-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g8-sm-calm-down,g10-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,coping plan; setting; back-up; habit,health:g9-meh-influences-judge-plan (health designs a plan for the influences on health),"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The calm-down steps become a coping plan the student designs for 9 to 12: the five steps, a tool for each of three settings, a back-up tool and a review date. The student builds the plan for an invented student's heavy week (tests, a game, extra family chores), rehearses two tools with a partner, names what each costs and judges after the case week which tool fit. Step up from grade 8: from a weekly plan chosen from the menu to a plan built and judged. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sa,9,g9-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g8-sa-feel-think-link,g10-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought; feeling; action; loop,,"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The student maps the think-feel-do loop of an invented student who reads a low quiz grade as ""I am bad at this"", plans where to break the loop (a checked thought, a talk with the teacher), and judges the plan by how the next scene goes. Step up from grade 8: from slowing one chain under pressure to planning where to break a loop that repeats. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sa,9,g9-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g8-sa-skills-grow,g10-sa-skills-grow,goal steps,goal steps,practice plan; feedback; measure; rest,physical-education:g9-skill-practice-plan (setting a skill goal and planning practice),"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The student designs a practice plan on the goal steps card for a skill an invented student finds hard (a language's verb forms, a drum pattern): what to practice, how often, who gives feedback, when to rest and how to measure progress, then judges the plan from a made-up log of three weeks. Never belief alone. Step up from grade 8: from choosing among ways to practice to designing and testing the whole plan."
rds,9,g9-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g8-rds-solve-problems,g10-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,problem statement; criteria; pilot; evaluate,,"Unit 2, Stress and coping. The class picks a school-day stressor it can act on (a crowded test week, a noisy study hall); the student writes the problem plainly, sizes it on the meter, designs a fix with its trade-offs, runs a small pilot where the school allows, and judges with the look-back step whether it helped. Step up from grade 8: from weighing solutions to a community problem to piloting a designed one and judging it."
sa,9,g9-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g8-sa-who-i-am,g10-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; role; steady; change over time,,"Unit 3, Identity and assets. The student writes a short identity sketch for an invented ninth grader (or one they make up): the roles and groups that make up the person, which parts the character plans to keep steady in high school and which to try changing, with the trade-off of each, then judges the sketch after a scene where two groups pull different ways. Step up from grade 8: from holding values under group push to planning how identity grows. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,9,g9-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g8-sa-my-strengths,g10-sa-my-strengths,,,asset; strength; plan; fit,,"Unit 3, Identity and assets. The student lists an invented character's assets (a skill, a habit, a language spoken at home, a talent from a hobby), plans where each could help in a new school year, names a strength that may be undervalued there, and judges which match was strongest. Step up from grade 8: from using one strength at a cost to an asset plan across settings. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,9,g9-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g8-sa-tell-truth,g10-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,values; integrity; plan; consequence,,"Unit 3, Identity and assets. The student designs how an invented student could act on a value where honesty is awkward (a group project where one member copied work): what to say and to whom, what the honest path costs, and a judgment of the plan with the look-back step. Step up from grade 8: from choosing the true action at a price to planning how to carry it out. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,9,g9-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g8-sa-snap-judgments,g10-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assumption; first impression; test; revise,,"Unit 3, Identity and assets. The student plans how an invented ninth grader could test a snap judgment about a new lab partner (what to ask, what evidence would change it), runs the case and judges whether the plan caught the error. Step up from grade 8: from checking a judgment a group shares to a plan for testing one's own. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,9,g9-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g8-rel-different-ways,g10-rel-different-ways,,,norm; custom; adapt; common ground,,"Unit 3, Identity and assets. For an invented project team from different backgrounds and schools, the student designs working rules that respect each member's habits (how to disagree, how fast to reply), names what each member adapts, and judges the rules after a scene where they are tested. Step up from grade 8: from finding common ground on a deadline to designing team rules ahead. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
rel,9,g9-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g8-rel-talk-listen,g10-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,check back; main point; tone; channel,ela:e9-sl-discussion (the same discussion norms and evidence moves),"Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The talk turn card adds a last step for 9 to 12: check what landed (the listener says back the main point) and plan the next talk. The student plans a hard school-day talk on an invented case (telling a lab partner the work split is unfair), rehearses it with a partner, and judges from the check-back whether the meaning got across. Step up from grade 8: from choosing channel and words to planning and checking the whole talk."
rel,9,g9-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g8-rel-friendships,g10-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,healthy; give and take; limit; check-in,,"Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The I-statement frame adds a request and a check-back for 9 to 12: when..., I feel..., I would like..., does that work for you? The student sorts signs of healthy and unhealthy friendship in an invented case (a friend who copies homework and gets angry when asked to stop), plans a talk with the frame and a limit, rehearses the talk with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan by the scene's end. Step up from grade 8: from choosing to step back to designing what to say and what limit to hold. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent)."
rel,9,g9-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g8-rel-settle-disagreements,g10-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,interests; options; agreement; follow-up,"health:g9-v-conflict-manage (health applies calm, listen and negotiate steps to preventing violence)","Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. With a partner the student works an invented disagreement over a shared school task: plans the talk, names each side's interests, offers two options, agrees one with the I-statement frame and sets a follow-up check, then judges the agreement after the case's next week. Step up from grade 8: from negotiating one compromise to planning a fix and checking it held. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,9,g9-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g8-rel-say-no,g10-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal plan; exit; ally; values,"health:g9-aod-say-no-across (health applies refusal to parties, rides and jobs)","Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The student designs a refusal plan for an invented school-day pressure (a friend pushing to share test answers in a group chat): the refusal options, an exit, an ally to bring in and the value it rests on, then rehearses it against a card character and judges which part held. Step up from grade 8: from holding one costly no to a plan made before the pressure arrives. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence)."
soc,9,g9-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g8-soc-care-when-struggle,g10-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,support plan; ask first; space; adult,,"Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The student plans support for an invented classmate going through a hard few weeks at school (a lost spot on the team): what to ask first, what kinds of care to offer, when to step back and when to bring in an adult, then judges the plan against how the friend responds. Step up from grade 8: from planning care that costs to a support plan with checkpoints. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
soc,9,g9-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g8-soc-read-feelings,g10-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,cue; context; check in; misread,physical-education:g9-courtesy-aware (being aware of how others feel during activity),"Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The student reads cues in an invented school-day scene (a friend quiet at lunch, short replies after class), plans a check-in with two possible openings, and judges which reading was right once the case reveals more. Step up from grade 8: from reading hidden cues in a group to planning a check-in and testing the reading."
soc,9,g9-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g8-soc-thankful,g10-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; habit; specific; return,,"Unit 4, Relationships that are good for me. The student designs a small gratitude habit for an invented student's school week (who to thank, how and when), names its trade-off (time, feeling awkward), and judges after the case week whether it changed how the people around responded. Step up from grade 8: from thanks for one costly help to a habit of thanks planned and checked."
soc,9,g9-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g8-soc-fair-rules,g10-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; unwritten rule; fair; change plan,,"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student applies the fairness questions to an invented school norm (seniors-only tables in the commons) and to a settled case the courts have ruled on, then designs a plan to change the school norm through school channels, with its trade-offs, and judges which part would work best. Step up from grade 8: from naming what a change cost to designing a change plan. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content."
soc,9,g9-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g8-soc-why-rules,g10-soc-why-rules,,,system; incentive; feed; design,health:g9-phw-factors-and-behavior (health on how factors at many levels shape behavior),"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student traces how an invented video app's feed and an invented school rule each push students' behavior, designs one change to either, and judges the change by who it would help and what it would cost. Step up from grade 8: from naming a system's pressure to redesigning a part of it. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rds,9,g9-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g8-rds-ask-questions,g10-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,open question; assumption; source; change of mind,ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate (judging whether reasoning holds and evidence is relevant),"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. Before deciding on an invented school-wide issue (a new phone rule), the student plans the questions to ask, the sources to check and what would change their mind, then judges after the case whether the questions found what mattered. Step up from grade 8: from staying open under friends' pressure to planning an open inquiry."
rds,9,g9-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g8-rds-check-facts,g10-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,data; distribution; claim; decision,math:alg1-data-displays (comparing distributions with plots before a choice),"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student checks an invented claim spreading at school (""most students sleep six hours"") against a class data set shown in two plots, plans a decision on what the data back, names what the data cannot show, and judges the decision with the look-back step. Step up from grade 8: from weighing evidence under time pressure to a data check designed and judged."
rds,9,g9-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g8-rds-think-ahead,g10-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,short term; long term; trade-off; review,,"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student lays out the short- and long-term results of options in an invented case (posting a clip of a class mishap that would get views), plans the choice with its trade-off, and later judges it against how the story unfolds. Step up from grade 8: from judging a costly choice afterward to planning ahead with a review built in."
rds,9,g9-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g8-rds-careful-thinking,g10-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,process; shortcut; hurry; reflect,,"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose on an invented choice pushed by a trending post, then compares it with the same choice made on a shortcut, and writes where careful thinking changed the result. Step up from grade 8: from thinking against a clock to judging a whole process against a shortcut."
rds,9,g9-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g8-rds-who-is-affected,g10-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakeholder; reach; rule; weigh,,"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student maps everyone an invented school decision reaches (a club's choice to post photos of an event), plans an option that weighs the far side too, and judges the plan against a stakeholder the map first missed. Step up from grade 8: from weighing who is affected against popularity to a designed option checked for gaps."
rds,9,g9-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g8-rds-good-for-us,g10-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; balance; routine; others,,"Unit 5, Norms, media and influence. The student plans a week for an invented student pulled between late gaming streams, sleep and a team counting on them, weighs well-being for self and others, and judges the plan's balance after the case week. Step up from grade 8: from one choice against something wanted to a balanced week planned and checked."
sm,9,g9-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g8-sm-set-goal,g10-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,goal; milestone; partner; revise,health:g9-phw-goal-assess-write (health applies a goal process to a health goal),"Unit 6, Goals with others. The student designs a term goal with a partner on the goal steps card: milestones, what each partner does, the trade-off they accept and a midpoint check, then judges and revises the plan after an invented setback. The goal is checked at the unit's end and carried on as the unit's carry-out cue. Step up from grade 8: from monitoring one term goal to designing a shared one with its review."
sm,9,g9-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g8-sm-step-forward,g10-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; start-up plan; recruit; first step,,"Unit 6, Goals with others. The student designs a start-up plan for something a ninth-grade class lacks (a study group, a welcome table): who to ask, the first step, what could stop it and what it costs, then judges the plan after the first invented week. Step up from grade 8: from starting despite a cost to designing the start."
sm,9,g9-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g8-sm-have-a-say,g10-sm-have-a-say,goal steps,goal steps,proposal; voice; channel; follow through,,"Unit 6, Goals with others. The class plans a proposal to improve one part of ninth-grade school life and the student designs their part on the goal steps card (the audience, the route, the evidence, the trade-offs), carries it out where the school allows and judges what worked. Student voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course. Step up from grade 8: from carrying out one part to designing it."
rel,9,g9-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g8-rel-teamwork,g10-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,team plan; role; norm; look-back,health:g9-meh-teams-negotiate-views (health on teamwork and negotiation in many settings),"Unit 6, Goals with others. The team designs its own plan for a shared project (roles, norms, how to raise a problem), carries it out, and holds a short look-back at the end to judge what to keep and change. Step up from grade 8: from carrying a project under a deadline to designing and judging the team's way of working."
rel,9,g9-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g8-rel-lead-follow,g10-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,agenda; decision method; feedback; follow,,"Unit 6, Goals with others. The student plans how to lead a team session (an agenda, how the group will decide, how to hear every voice), runs it, follows when another member leads, and judges both roles from team feedback. Step up from grade 8: from leading one unpopular decision to planning and judging a whole session."
rel,9,g9-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g8-rel-stand-up,g10-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander plan; safe option; support; report,,"Unit 6, Goals with others. The student designs an upstander plan for an invented case of a student left out of a team in a hurtful way: the safest option, the words with the I-statement frame, support for the person afterward and when to report, rehearses the words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, then judges the plan against the case. Step up from grade 8: from planning one costly speak-up to a plan with follow-up. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
soc,9,g9-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g8-soc-their-view,g10-soc-their-view,,,perspective; summary; check; respect,,"Unit 6, Goals with others. On an invented team split over a project choice, the student writes the other side's view well enough that its holder would agree, plans a question to check it, and judges whether the summary held up when the case gives more. Step up from grade 8: from taking one costly view to planning how to test that one has it right."
soc,9,g9-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g8-soc-notice-good,g10-soc-notice-good,,,credit; specific; feedback; habit,physical-education:g9-encourage-feedback (encouraging others and giving feedback),"Unit 6, Goals with others. The student plans how a team leader could credit each member's work across a project (what to notice, when to say it), and judges after the case which credit landed and why. Step up from grade 8: from crediting a rival once to planning credit across a team."
rel,10,g10-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g9-rel-talk-listen,g11-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,listen to understand; paraphrase; both sides; check back,ela:e10-sl-seminar (the same student-led discussion norms),"Unit 1, Listening to understand. With a partner, the student plans a talk on an invented disagreement where each person thinks the other is not listening, rehearses it with both people using the check-back step, and judges from the partner's account whether each felt understood. Grade 10 brings the other person's side of the talk into the plan."
soc,10,g10-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g9-soc-their-view,g11-soc-their-view,,,viewpoint; reason; strongest case; common ground,social-studies:wh2-contested-questions (naming where informed people agree and disagree),"Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student writes the strongest form of two invented classmates' opposed views on a school question, plans a conversation that finds common ground, and judges whether each person would accept how they were described. New this year: two people's views held at full strength."
soc,10,g10-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g9-soc-read-feelings,g11-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,read; misread; ask; respond,,"Unit 1, Listening to understand. In an invented scene the student reads one person's feelings, then reads the same moment through the second person's eyes, plans a response for each, and judges which reading the case bears out. This year the scene is read from both sides."
sa,10,g10-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g9-sa-feel-think-link,g11-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,interpretation; reaction; each side; loop,,"Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student maps the think-feel-do loops of two invented people in one misunderstanding, plans where either could break the loop, and judges which break the case supports. Grade 10 runs the loop for a second person beside the first. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
soc,10,g10-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g9-soc-why-rules,g11-soc-why-rules,,,system; reward; pushed out; redesign,,"Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student traces how an invented school system (a points reward for attendance) looks to a student it helps and to one it pushes out, designs a change, and judges it from both views. A second person's view now tests the redesign. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rel,10,g10-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g9-rel-settle-disagreements,g11-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,interest; position; proposal; agreement,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. With a partner the student plans and holds an invented hard conversation over a shared commitment, each writing the other's interests before the talk, then agrees a fix with the I-statement frame and checks it later. Grade 10: each side plans from the other's interests. The teacher plays any upset part."
rel,10,g10-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g9-rel-ask-offer-help,g11-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,offer; accept; decline; support map,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student plans how to offer help to an invented friend who may not want it, writes how the offer could sound from the friend's side, uses the support map to plan when an adult must come in, rehearses the offer with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan after the scene. New in grade 10: help planned around the other person's wishes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. A worry about a friend's safety goes to an adult, even when the friend asked for a secret. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
sm,10,g10-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g9-sm-big-feelings,g11-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,heat; pause; response; repair,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student designs a plan for keeping a strong feeling from driving an invented hard talk, adds how the other person's feeling might rise too and what the plan does about it, and judges it in a rehearsed scene. What grade 10 adds: two people's feelings in one plan."
sm,10,g10-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g9-sm-calm-down,g11-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,cool down; shared pause; signal; return,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student builds a coping plan for two people in an invented tense talk: an agreed pause signal, a tool each could use, and how to come back to the talk. The student rehearses it with a partner and judges whether the pause helped both. New this year: the coping plan works for two people at once. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sa,10,g10-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g9-sa-name-feelings,g11-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,own feeling; other's feeling; name aloud; pattern,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student charts the feelings of both people in an invented hard conversation, finds where they set each other off, and plans where naming a feeling aloud could slow the talk, judging the chart against the scene. Grade 10 charts a second person beside the first. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sm,10,g10-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g9-sm-have-a-say,g11-sm-have-a-say,goal steps,goal steps,speak up; audience; listen; revise,,"Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student plans how to raise a class concern with a teacher or school leader (an invented crowded test schedule), writes how the adult may see it, revises the plan to answer that view, and judges the talk after a rehearsal. The other person's view now shapes how voice is used. Student voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course."
rel,10,g10-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g9-rel-different-ways,g11-rel-different-ways,,,custom; respect; adapt; shared norm,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for working across difference in 9 to 12. In an invented mixed team, the student plans working norms with a partner from another background, each writing how the other sees a clash (eye contact, directness, time), and judges the norms after a scene. Grade 10 builds the norms from both people's sides. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
soc,10,g10-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g9-soc-fair-rules,g11-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; fairness; history; dignity,"social-studies:wh2-apartheid (an unfair system and how it was resisted, taught in world history this year)","Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for unfair norms in 9 to 12. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case (school segregation, struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954, or apartheid as world history teaches it), writes how the norm looked to the people it held down and to those who kept it, and plans a fair change to an invented school norm. New this year: the norm seen through the eyes of the people it hurt. Teacher note: settled history, taught with dignity: school segregation was struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954; apartheid's laws were repealed from 1990 to 1991 and it ended with the 1994 election; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (wh2-apartheid)."
sa,10,g10-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g9-sa-who-i-am,g11-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; belonging; seen; shown,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student writes how an invented character sees their own groups and how a newcomer sees them, plans how the character could make room for the newcomer while staying themselves, and judges the plan after a scene. Grade 10 sets one person's sense of self beside how another sees them. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,10,g10-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g9-sa-my-strengths,g11-sa-my-strengths,,,cultural asset; strength; value; share,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student plans how an invented team could use a member's strength from a home language or culture (translating for a visitor, a family tradition of storytelling), writes how the member might want it used or not, and judges the plan from that member's side. This year the strength holder's own wishes shape the plan. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,10,g10-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g9-sa-snap-judgments,g11-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,bias; assumption; other side; correct,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for snap judgments in 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented student could check a snap judgment about a classmate from another school, writes how the judged classmate would experience the guess, and judges whether the plan corrected it. What grade 10 adds: the judged person's view. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
soc,10,g10-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g9-soc-notice-good,g11-soc-notice-good,,,credit; overlooked; specific; land,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student plans credit for an invented teammate whose strength others overlook because it looks different from theirs, writes how the teammate would hear it, and judges the words. The teammate's view now decides whether credit lands."
sa,10,g10-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g9-sa-what-interests-me,g11-sa-what-interests-me,,,interest; expectation; own reason; purpose,,"Unit 3, Culture and respect. Privately the student plans how to weigh an interest against what another person expects of them (a friend, a coach), writes that person's view fairly, and judges after a set time whether the choice kept their own purpose. Grade 10 brings another person's expectations into the plan. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
rel,10,g10-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g9-rel-friendships,g11-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,limit; respect; both sides; healthy,,"Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans how an invented student could set a limit in a friendship (a friend who wants every free period together), writes how the friend may hear it, plans the talk with the I-statement frame and its check-back, rehearses it with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan from both sides after the scene. Grade 10 weighs the friend's side of the limit. The limits are about time, help and plans. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent, with consent and boundaries in health row g9-meh-boundaries-consent)."
rel,10,g10-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g9-rel-say-no,g11-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal; limit; alternative; keep the friend,"health:g9-meh-boundaries-consent (health practices stating limits in friendships, work and online)","Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student designs a refusal for an invented case where a friend presses them to lie to a teacher for the friend, writes how the friend sees the ask, plans an alternative that keeps the friendship, rehearses against a card character and judges what held. New this year: the plan answers the other person's reasons. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence)."
sa,10,g10-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g9-sa-tell-truth,g11-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,honesty; loyalty; two sides; plan,,"Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. In an invented case where telling the truth lets down a friend, the student plans what to say, writes how the friend and the teacher each see it, and judges the plan with the look-back step. Grade 10 holds honesty and a second person's view together. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
soc,10,g10-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g9-soc-read-situation,g11-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakes; signals; other eyes; plan,,"Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student reads an invented weekend plan that turns uneasy, from their own side and from a friend's, plans whether to stay, change it or leave, and judges the plan by both people's results. This year the reading takes in the second person's view."
rds,10,g10-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g9-rds-think-ahead,g11-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,consequence; other people; long run; review,,"Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans a choice in an invented case (saying yes to every favor a friend asks), lays out results for self and for the friend over a month, and judges the plan after the case plays out. Grade 10 adds the friend's results to the plan."
rel,10,g10-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g9-rel-stand-up,g11-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; ask first; support; report,,"Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans how to stand up for an invented classmate mocked online by a school group: first how to ask the classmate what they want, then the safest option with the I-statement frame; the student rehearses both with the teacher, who plays the classmate and then the school group's message, while a partner watches for the steps, and judges the plan from the classmate's side. New in grade 10: the target's wishes lead the plan. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
rel,10,g10-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g9-rel-teamwork,g11-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,role; workload; view; adjust,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The team designs its plan, then each member writes how the split looks from one other member's side; the team adjusts the plan and judges it at the end of the project. Grade 10 tests the team plan against each member's view."
rel,10,g10-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g9-rel-lead-follow,g11-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,lead; follow; feedback; trust,physical-education:g10-challenges-leadership (judging leadership in teams during activity),"Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans and leads a team session, asks a teammate for feedback on the leading, and later follows that teammate's lead, judging both roles from the other person's side. What changes: leading is judged by the one who followed."
sm,10,g10-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g9-sm-stay-on-task,g11-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,focus; partner; agreement; check-in,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The student designs a focus plan with a study partner (shared work blocks, a signal for off-task, a check-in), writes what the partner needs from it, and judges after the case week whether it worked for both. A partner's needs now shape the focus plan."
sm,10,g10-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g9-sm-set-goal,g11-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,shared goal; milestone; commitment; revise,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The team sets a shared goal on the goal steps card, each member writes what they need for it to work, and the plan is revised after a check-in where needs clash. The second person's needs enter the goal plan this year."
sm,10,g10-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g9-sm-plan-and-tidy,g11-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,timeline; handoff; shared calendar; buffer,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The student builds a team timeline where tasks hand off between members, writes what each handoff asks of the next person, plans buffers, and judges the timeline after an invented delay. Grade 10: planning that other people depend on."
sm,10,g10-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g9-sm-step-forward,g11-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; invite; co-own; share,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans how to start a team improvement and invite a quiet member to co-own it, writes how the start looks from that member's side, and judges whether the invitation worked. New this year: starting something with another person."
soc,10,g10-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g9-soc-care-when-struggle,g11-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; ask; respect; space,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. When an invented teammate is struggling and falling behind, the student plans care the teammate would welcome (asked about first), weighs it against the team's deadline, and judges it from the teammate's side. Grade 10 lets the struggling person's view lead the care. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
soc,10,g10-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g9-soc-thankful,g11-soc-thankful,,,thanks; sincere; how it lands; team,,"Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans thanks for each team member at the end of the project, writes how each might want to be thanked (in front of the team or in private), and judges which thanks landed. This year the receiver's wishes shape the thanks."
sa,10,g10-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g9-sa-i-can-try,g11-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,doubt; encourage; plan; support,,"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student helps plan for an invented friend who doubts they can try out for a selective program: two steps, a helper, a fallback, and what the friend would find encouraging, then judges the plan from the friend's side. Grade 10 plans belief alongside another person. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,10,g10-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g9-sa-skills-grow,g11-sa-skills-grow,goal steps,goal steps,feedback; coach; practice plan; growth,,"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student designs a practice plan from a coach's or teacher's invented feedback, writes how the feedback giver sees the gap, and judges whether the plan answers it. Never belief alone. What grade 10 adds: the plan starts from another person's feedback."
rds,10,g10-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g9-rds-ask-questions,g11-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; other view; open mind; revise,ela:e10-ri-argument-evaluate (delineating and evaluating a full argument),"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. Before a class decision on an invented fairness question (who gets first pick of project topics), the student interviews a partner with the other view, plans the questions that test both, and judges whether the plan changed or confirmed their view. Grade 10 puts a second person's questions beside one's own."
rds,10,g10-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g9-rds-check-facts,g11-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,two-way table; evidence; fair; belief,math:geo-two-way-probability (two-way tables of two categories),"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student checks two invented students' claims about the fairness of a school rule against a two-way table, writes why each believed their claim, plans a decision on what the data show, and judges it with the look-back step. The other person's reasons for a belief join the data check."
rds,10,g10-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g9-rds-solve-problems,g11-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,fairness; stakeholder; solution; test,,"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The class takes a fairness problem in school life it can act on (how lab partners are chosen); the student designs a solution with input from a partner who sees the problem differently, pilots it where allowed and judges it from both views. New in grade 10: a second view shapes the solution."
rds,10,g10-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g9-rds-careful-thinking,g11-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,process; partner; disagree; reasons,,"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose with a partner who reaches a different choice on an invented case, compares the steps where they split, and judges whose reasoning held up. Grade 10 tests careful thinking against another person's."
rds,10,g10-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g9-rds-good-for-us,g11-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; fair share; others; balance,,"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student plans a fair split of an invented household's weekly chores among siblings with different schedules, writes each sibling's view, and judges whether the plan is good for everyone. This year the plan weighs a second person's well-being as fully as one's own."
rds,10,g10-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g9-rds-who-is-affected,g11-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakeholder; voice; impact; fair,computer-science:g10-human-choices (human decisions in using and designing a technology),"Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student maps who an invented school decision affects, asks a partner playing one affected group what they would add, plans an option fair to both and judges it. What grade 10 adds: an affected person's own voice in the map."
sa,11,g11-sa-what-interests-me,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g10-sa-what-interests-me,g12-sa-what-interests-me,,,purpose; commitment; cost; priority,,"Unit 1, What matters to me. Privately the student plans how to give time to an interest when something else must give (a paid job, a sport, study hours), writes the cost of each path, and judges after a set time whether the choice held its purpose. Grade 11 puts a real cost on the interest. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,11,g11-sa-tell-truth,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g10-sa-tell-truth,g12-sa-tell-truth,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,integrity; price; plan; stand,,"Unit 1, What matters to me. In an invented case where acting on a value costs a spot (reporting a teammate's rule break before a final game), the student plans what to do and say, names the price, and judges the plan with the look-back step. New this year: honesty planned when it costs. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
sa,11,g11-sa-feel-think-link,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g10-sa-feel-think-link,g12-sa-feel-think-link,feelings faces,feelings faces,pressure; thought trap; values; choice,,"Unit 1, What matters to me. The student maps how pressure (a deadline, a parent's hope, a friend's push) runs through an invented student's thoughts and feelings to a choice against what they value, plans where to break the loop, and judges the plan. Grade 11 maps the loop under pressure. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sa,11,g11-sa-who-i-am,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g10-sa-who-i-am,g12-sa-who-i-am,,,identity; values; pull; consistent,,"Unit 1, What matters to me. The student plans how an invented eleventh grader keeps what matters to them when two groups (a team and a job) each pull hard, names what holding steady costs, and judges the plan after a scene. What grade 11 adds: identity planned under pressure from two sides. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,11,g11-sa-my-strengths,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g10-sa-my-strengths,g12-sa-my-strengths,,,strength; risk; show; opportunity,,"Unit 1, What matters to me. The student plans how an invented character could use a strength where showing it carries a cost (a quiet student leading a debate, a bilingual student always asked to translate for free), weighs the cost, and judges the plan. This year a cost comes with using a strength. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sm,11,g11-sm-set-goal,~CASEL.Self-Management.4,g10-sm-set-goal,g12-sm-set-goal,goal steps,goal steps,long-term goal; setback; cost; revise,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a long-term goal plan for an invented eleventh grader (a skill certificate, a team captaincy) on the goal steps card, plans what the goal costs in time and other wants, meets an invented setback, and revises and judges the plan. Grade 11 tests the plan against a setback and a cost."
sm,11,g11-sm-plan-and-tidy,~CASEL.Self-Management.5,g10-sm-plan-and-tidy,g12-sm-plan-and-tidy,goal steps,goal steps,overload; priority; cut; schedule,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student plans an invented overloaded month (courses, a part-time job, a family duty), decides what to cut, names the cost of each cut, and judges after the case month what the plan protected. New this year: planning when every choice gives something up."
sm,11,g11-sm-stay-on-task,~CASEL.Self-Management.3,g10-sm-stay-on-task,g12-sm-stay-on-task,goal steps,goal steps,motivation; slump; restart; self-check,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a restart plan for an invented student whose focus slumps midway through a long project after a bad grade, names what the restart costs, and judges it from a case log. Grade 11 plans focus after a setback."
sa,11,g11-sa-i-can-try,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g10-sa-i-can-try,g12-sa-i-can-try,goal steps,goal steps,confidence; fallback; risk; try again,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student plans a second try for an invented character who failed a first attempt in public (a driving test, an audition), with two steps, one place to ask for help and a fallback, names the cost of trying again, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: trying again when failing has already cost. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,11,g11-sa-skills-grow,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g10-sa-skills-grow,g12-sa-skills-grow,goal steps,goal steps,plateau; strategy; feedback; rest,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a practice plan for an invented student who has hit a plateau with little time left before an exam, chooses what to change and what to drop, and judges the plan from the case results. Never belief alone. This year growth is planned under time pressure."
sm,11,g11-sm-calm-down,~CASEL.Self-Management.2,g10-sm-calm-down,g12-sm-calm-down,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,stress; coping plan; sleep; recovery,physical-education:g10-rest (building rest and sleep into a schedule),"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student revises an invented student's coping plan for a high-pressure term (exams, a job, a game season): tools for each setting, sleep and rest kept in, what the plan costs in other time, and a review after the case term. Grade 11 tests the coping plan under pressure that lasts. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,11,g11-sm-big-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Management.1,g10-sm-big-feelings,g12-sm-big-feelings,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,frustration; setback; response; recover,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a response plan for a strong feeling after an invented setback (a rejection letter, a lost final), names what reacting would cost, and judges the plan in a second scene. New this year: the feeling comes with a loss."
sa,11,g11-sa-name-feelings,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g10-sa-name-feelings,g12-sa-name-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,disappointment; pressure; shift; early sign,,"Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student charts an invented student's feelings across a term of rising pressure, names exact words as they shift, plans where to catch early signs, and judges the chart. Grade 11 charts feelings under a long pressure. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
rds,11,g11-rds-ask-questions,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g10-rds-ask-questions,g12-rds-ask-questions,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,inquiry; deadline; source; open,social-studies:us1-research-inquiry (the research cycle and its questions),"Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student plans an inquiry on an invented choice with pressure to decide fast (a summer program with a deadline tomorrow): questions, sources and what would change their mind, then judges what the time pressure cost the inquiry. Grade 11 keeps questions open under a deadline."
rds,11,g11-rds-check-facts,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g10-rds-check-facts,g12-rds-check-facts,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,evidence; claim; fee; weigh,,"Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student checks the claims behind an invented costly choice (two programs with different fees and results) against the numbers, plans a decision, names what the evidence cannot settle, and judges the decision with the look-back step. New this year: evidence weighed when the choice has a price."
rds,11,g11-rds-think-ahead,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g10-rds-think-ahead,g12-rds-think-ahead,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,risk; long term; cost; reversible,,"Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student lays out short- and long-term costs of options in an invented choice that is hard to undo (dropping a course for more work hours), plans the pick, and judges it against how the case unfolds. Grade 11 adds what a choice costs and whether it can be undone."
rds,11,g11-rds-careful-thinking,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g10-rds-careful-thinking,g12-rds-careful-thinking,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,pressure; process; skipped step; reflect,,"Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose on an invented choice under pressure from a friend and a deadline, then reflects in writing on which step the pressure tried to skip and what skipping it would have cost. This year careful thinking holds against pressure."
sa,11,g11-sa-snap-judgments,~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g10-sa-snap-judgments,g12-sa-snap-judgments,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assumption; evidence; standing; correct,,"Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student plans how an invented student could check a snap judgment a whole group shares about a new team member when disagreeing costs standing, and judges whether the plan corrected it. What grade 11 adds: checking a judgment with a social price. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,11,g11-rel-talk-listen,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g10-rel-talk-listen,g12-rel-talk-listen,talk turn card,talk turn card,plain speech; respect; goodwill; check back,ela:e11-seminar (initiating and taking part in collaborative discussion),"Unit 4, Working with others. The student plans and rehearses an invented talk where saying the plain truth may cost goodwill (telling a team its plan will not meet the deadline), with the check-back step, and judges whether the meaning got across. Grade 11 plans the talk when speaking plainly has a cost."
rel,11,g11-rel-different-ways,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g10-rel-different-ways,g12-rel-different-ways,,,difference; compromise; give up; norm,,"Unit 4, Working with others. In an invented team where habits clash and the deadline is close, the student plans working norms that respect each member, names what each side gives up, and judges the norms after the deadline passes. New this year: respect kept when it costs time. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
rel,11,g11-rel-teamwork,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g10-rel-teamwork,g12-rel-teamwork,talk turn card,talk turn card,accountability; workload; loss; review,,"Unit 4, Working with others. The team designs a plan for a project with a hard deadline, meets an invented loss (a member drops out), replans without blame, and judges at the end what the plan protected. Grade 11 tests the team plan under a loss."
rel,11,g11-rel-settle-disagreements,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g10-rel-settle-disagreements,g12-rel-settle-disagreements,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,negotiate; trade-off; deal; fairness,,"Unit 4, Working with others. With a partner the student plans and holds a negotiation on an invented team disagreement where a deal costs one member more, agrees a fix with the I-statement frame and judges whether the costs were shared fairly, with a follow-up checking that the uneven cost was made up. What grade 11 adds: a settlement that costs both sides. The teacher plays any upset part."
soc,11,g11-soc-their-view,~CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g10-soc-their-view,g12-soc-their-view,,,perspective; unpopular; disagree; respect,,"Unit 4, Working with others. The student states the view of an invented teammate whose view is unpopular (a junior on a mostly senior team proposing a slower, safer plan), well enough that the teammate would agree, plans how to raise it in the team, and judges the result. This year taking a view comes with a cost."
soc,11,g11-soc-notice-good,~CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g10-soc-notice-good,g12-soc-notice-good,,,credit; rival; pride; generous,,"Unit 4, Working with others. The student plans specific credit for an invented junior teammate whose idea replaced the student's own in a group project, when giving it costs some pride, and judges how it landed. Grade 11 gives credit at a cost: the credit goes to the idea that beat the student's."
soc,11,g11-soc-read-situation,~CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g10-soc-read-situation,g12-soc-read-situation,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakes; pressure; options; leave,,"Unit 4, Working with others. The student reads an invented work-group moment where the stakes rise (a group plan to skip a step that keeps a project safe), plans whether to stay, change it or leave, names each cost, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: reading a moment as pressure builds."
rel,11,g11-rel-ask-offer-help,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g10-rel-ask-offer-help,g12-rel-ask-offer-help,problem size meter,problem size meter,reluctant; trusted adult; cost; follow through,health:g9-meh-help-and-concern (health on asking for and giving help for health),"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans asking for help when it costs (asking a manager to move a shift before finals) and offering help that takes real time, uses the support map for each, rehearses the ask and the offer aloud with a partner playing the coach and then the teammate, and judges the plan. Grade 11 plans help with a cost on both sides. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. A worry about a friend's safety goes to an adult, even when the friend asked for a secret. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
rel,11,g11-rel-friendships,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g10-rel-friendships,g12-rel-friendships,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,healthy; pressure; limit; step back,,"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student weighs an invented friendship where a friend asks for daily help with work the student has no time for, plans a limit with the I-statement frame and its check-back, rehearses the talk with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan from both sides. New this year: a friendship choice with a price, since a yes costs the student's own time and a no may cost the friend's goodwill. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent)."
rel,11,g11-rel-say-no,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g10-rel-say-no,g12-rel-say-no,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal plan; price; exit; ally,,"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student designs a refusal plan for an invented case where saying no carries a price (being dropped from a group trip for refusing to cover a lie), rehearses it against a card character, and judges which part held. Grade 11 plans a no that costs. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence)."
soc,11,g11-soc-care-when-struggle,~CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g10-soc-care-when-struggle,g12-soc-care-when-struggle,feelings faces,feelings faces,concern; ongoing; support; limits,,"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans care for an invented friend whose hard time goes on for weeks, including what the care costs the student and when to bring in an adult, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: care that lasts and has limits. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
soc,11,g11-soc-read-feelings,~CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g10-soc-read-feelings,g12-soc-read-feelings,feelings faces,feelings faces,hidden cue; joke; pressure; respond,,"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student reads the feelings of an invented teammate whose replies grow short over a week of messages after being cut from the starting line-up, plans a check-in that respects the teammate even if the group teases the student for it, and judges the plan. This year reading feelings carries a social cost, read over days rather than in one moment. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
soc,11,g11-soc-thankful,~CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g10-soc-thankful,g12-soc-thankful,,,gratitude; sacrifice; timing; sincere,,"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans thanks for an invented person whose help cost them (a friend who gave up an evening to help study), choosing words and timing, and judges whether the thanks matched the cost. Grade 11 thanks help that had a price."
soc,11,g11-soc-why-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g10-soc-why-rules,g12-soc-why-rules,,,media; pressure; incentive; influence,social-studies:us1-news-reading (telling news from opinion),"Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student traces how an invented media feed and an invented peer norm press students toward a costly habit (buying the newest gear, staying up for streaks), designs one change to resist the pressure, and judges what it would cost. What grade 11 adds: a system's pressure with a price attached. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
sm,11,g11-sm-step-forward,~CASEL.Self-Management.6,g10-sm-step-forward,g12-sm-step-forward,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; risk; recruit; persist,,"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans the start an invented senior could make on a peer tutoring slot that costs the senior a free period, recruits others in the plan, and judges after the first invented weeks whether the start held. New this year: starting when it costs one's own time."
sm,11,g11-sm-have-a-say,~CASEL.Self-Management.7,g10-sm-have-a-say,g12-sm-have-a-say,goal steps,goal steps,advocacy; objection; cost; follow through,,"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The class plans a proposal to change one school routine that staff time limits (a later library hour), weighs its staffing cost and its costs for different groups, plans answers to objections, carries out a part where the school allows, and judges the outcome. Student voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course. Grade 11 weighs the cost of having a say, including the cost to the staff who would make the change work."
rel,11,g11-rel-lead-follow,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g10-rel-lead-follow,g12-rel-lead-follow,talk turn card,talk turn card,unpopular; leadership; trust; follow,physical-education:g11-lead-feedback (leading a small group and giving feedback),"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans how to lead an invented group that must cut one member's part to meet a deadline, rehearses the hard talk with a partner playing the member, then follows a leader they disagree with, judging what each cost. What grade 11 adds: leading when the decision costs a member their part."
rel,11,g11-rel-stand-up,~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g10-rel-stand-up,g12-rel-stand-up,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; risk; safe option; report,,"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student designs an upstander plan for an invented school case where the unkind act comes from a teammate the student depends on for a shared grade, with the safest option, the words and support afterward, rehearses the words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the teammate's unkind act, and judges the plan. This year the plan carries a cost: the shared grade and the working tie. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
soc,11,g11-soc-fair-rules,~CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g10-soc-fair-rules,g12-soc-fair-rules,,,norm; change; price; courage,social-studies:us1-jim-crow (the long-term effects of one Jim Crow practice),"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case the courts have ruled on, names what changing the norm cost the people who did it, and sets beside it an invented school norm whose change would cost the student's own group a privilege (seniors' first pick of lunch tables), plans the change, then judges the plan. New in grade 11: the price of a fair change falls on the student's own group. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (us1-jim-crow)."
rds,11,g11-rds-solve-problems,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g10-rds-solve-problems,g12-rds-solve-problems,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,solution; limit; trade-off; evaluate,,"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The class takes a school problem it can act on with a limited budget of time or money; the student designs a fix within the limit, pilots it where allowed and judges it. Grade 11 solves within a cost limit."
rds,11,g11-rds-good-for-us,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g10-rds-good-for-us,g12-rds-good-for-us,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; promise; want; balance,,"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans an invented choice where well-being for self and others costs something wanted (a weekend job shift against a promise to a younger sibling), and judges the plan after the case week. What grade 11 adds: well-being with a price."
rds,11,g11-rds-who-is-affected,~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g10-rds-who-is-affected,g12-rds-who-is-affected,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakeholder; popular; cost; fairness,computer-science:g11-algorithm-ethics (who a rule-based or data-driven algorithm treats fairly),"Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student maps who an invented senior trip date affects when it suits most seniors but clashes with a large group's work shifts, plans an option fair to all, and judges it. This year weighing everyone affected costs the popular choice."
sa,12,g12-sa-who-i-am,CASEL.Self-Awareness.1,g11-sa-who-i-am,,,,identity; transition; carry; future,,"Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student writes a looking-ahead sketch for an invented senior (or one they make up) moving into work, training or college: which parts of who they are to carry, which groups they will leave and join, the trade-offs, and a judgment of the sketch after a scene set in a first job. Grade 12 plans identity for life past school. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check."
sa,12,g12-sa-my-strengths,CASEL.Self-Awareness.2,g11-sa-my-strengths,,,,asset; application; interview; transfer,,"Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented senior could name strengths for a job interview or application (one from a home language or culture among them), matches each to what the setting asks, and judges which match was strongest. Grade 12 carries strengths into a setting past school. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about."
sa,12,g12-sa-what-interests-me,CASEL.Self-Awareness.9,g11-sa-what-interests-me,,,,purpose; path; trade-off; next step,computer-science:g12-career-goals (connecting what was learned to a personal goal),"Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. Privately the student plans a next step after school that fits an interest (a course, a trade, a job, a year of service), with its trade-offs, and sets a date to judge whether it still fits. The setting is now life after school. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content."
sa,12,g12-sa-i-can-try,CASEL.Self-Awareness.7,g11-sa-i-can-try,,goal steps,goal steps,confidence; first step; support; fallback,,"Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented senior could take on an unfamiliar adult task (applying for a first apartment, starting a training program), with two steps, a place to ask for help and a fallback, and judges the plan after the case. Grade 12 moves the challenge past school. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan."
sa,12,g12-sa-feel-think-link,CASEL.Self-Awareness.5,g11-sa-feel-think-link,,feelings faces,feelings faces,thought; feeling; change; reframe,,"Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student maps how an invented senior's thoughts about leaving school and what the senior cares about shape feelings and choices (a missed application read as ""I will never get in""), plans where to break the loop, and judges the plan in a scene set after graduation. The loop now runs through a change past school. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud."
sa,12,g12-sa-name-feelings,CASEL.Self-Awareness.3,g11-sa-name-feelings,,feelings faces,feelings faces,mixed feelings; transition; early sign; plan,,"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student charts an invented graduate's feelings through a first month at work or college, names mixed feelings exactly, plans early signs to watch for, and judges the chart. Grade 12 charts feelings in a new setting past school. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded."
sm,12,g12-sm-big-feelings,CASEL.Self-Management.1,g11-sm-big-feelings,,feelings faces; calm-down steps,feelings faces; calm-down steps,correction; workplace; recover; repair,physical-education:g12-manage-emotions (chosen ways to manage emotions during activity),"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a response plan for an invented strong-feeling moment at a first job (a sharp correction from a supervisor in front of others), with the early sign, a calm-down step, a response and a repair, and judges it in a rehearsed scene. Grade 12 handles strong feelings at work."
sm,12,g12-sm-calm-down,CASEL.Self-Management.2,g11-sm-calm-down,,calm-down steps,calm-down steps,coping plan; routine; new setting; review,,"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a coping plan for an invented graduate's first term away from school routines (a new schedule, a job, living with others): tools for each setting, rest kept in, a back-up and a review date, and judges it after the case month. The plan now serves life after school, with where to find help after school named in plain words. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor."
sm,12,g12-sm-stay-on-task,CASEL.Self-Management.3,g11-sm-stay-on-task,,goal steps,goal steps,self-start; no one checking; routine; check-in,,"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a focus plan for an invented graduate with no one checking daily (an online course, a job with loose hours), plans self check-ins and judges the plan from a case log. Grade 12: focus kept with no school to push it. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
sm,12,g12-sm-plan-and-tidy,CASEL.Self-Management.5,g11-sm-plan-and-tidy,,goal steps,goal steps,shifts; bill dates; schedule; system,,"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a planning system for an invented graduate's month (work shifts, classes, bill dates, appointments) with a way to keep papers and materials in order, names its trade-offs and judges it after an invented change of shift. Grade 12 plans adult life past school."
sa,12,g12-sa-skills-grow,CASEL.Self-Awareness.8,g11-sa-skills-grow,,goal steps,goal steps,new skill; supervisor; practice; feedback,,"Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a practice plan for a new skill an invented graduate must learn at work or in training, with feedback from a supervisor, and judges it from a case log. Never belief alone. Grade 12 plans growth at work or in training. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
rel,12,g12-rel-lead-follow,CASEL.Relationship Skills.7,g11-rel-lead-follow,,talk turn card,talk turn card,lead; delegate; follow; feedback,physical-education:g12-lead (leading or organizing a small activity event),"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans and leads an invented community event team (a food drive, a youth game day), delegates, follows another leader in a second role, and judges both from team feedback. Grade 12 leads and follows in a community setting."
rel,12,g12-rel-teamwork,CASEL.Relationship Skills.4,g11-rel-teamwork,,talk turn card,talk turn card,team charter; roles; accountability; review,computer-science:g12-team-workflow (a team building one program with a shared plan),"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The team writes a charter for a project that reaches past school (a service project with a community partner): roles, norms and how to raise problems, then carries it out and judges it in a closing review. Grade 12 takes teamwork to a community partner. Also carries AASL.III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL."
rel,12,g12-rel-talk-listen,CASEL.Relationship Skills.1,g11-rel-talk-listen,,talk turn card,talk turn card,professional; plain; check back; channel,ela:e12-seminar (collaborative discussion on grade 12 texts and issues),"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans and rehearses a talk in a setting past school (asking a supervisor to change a schedule, a call with a community partner), chooses the channel and words, uses the check-back step and judges whether the meaning got across. Grade 12 talks to adults at work and in the community."
soc,12,g12-soc-notice-good,CASEL.Social Awareness.2,g11-soc-notice-good,,,,credit; recognition; specific; team,,"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how a team leader in an invented workplace or community group gives specific credit to each member over a project, and judges which credit landed. Grade 12 makes credit part of leading past school."
soc,12,g12-soc-read-situation,CASEL.Social Awareness.7,g11-soc-read-situation,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,workplace; expectation; opportunity; norm,,"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student reads invented settings past school (a first shift, a volunteer meeting, a job interview) for what each asks and what chances it offers, plans how to act in each, and judges the plan against how the case turns out. Grade 12 reads settings beyond school."
sm,12,g12-sm-step-forward,CASEL.Self-Management.6,g11-sm-step-forward,,goal steps,goal steps,initiative; volunteer; start; sustain,,"Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans a start for something a community group or workplace needs (a sign-up sheet, a welcome for new volunteers), with first steps, recruits and costs, and judges it. Grade 12 takes initiative in a setting past school."
soc,12,g12-soc-fair-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.6,g11-soc-fair-rules,,,,norm; law; community; change,social-studies:us2-civil-rights-laws (what the 1964 and 1965 laws achieved),"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case the courts have ruled on and to an invented community norm (a club whose membership rule keeps newcomers out), compares the unwritten rules of two settings, plans a fair change through the group's own process, and judges the plan. Grade 12 turns the questions on community life. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (us2-civil-rights-laws)."
soc,12,g12-soc-why-rules,CASEL.Social Awareness.8,g11-soc-why-rules,,,,system; community; incentive; design,social-studies:gov-media-democracy (how media shape what people know in a democracy),"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student traces how an invented community system (a town's sign-up rules for sports fields, a workplace's shift app) shapes what people do, designs one change, and judges who it would help and what it would cost. Grade 12 studies systems in community and work life. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names."
rds,12,g12-rds-good-for-us,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6,g11-rds-good-for-us,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,well-being; community; balance; long run,physical-education:g12-active-adult (an active week planned around work or study),"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans an invented graduate's week that weighs their own well-being, family and a community commitment, and judges the plan's balance after the case week. Grade 12 balances well-being in adult life."
rds,12,g12-rds-who-is-affected,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7,g11-rds-who-is-affected,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,stakeholder; community; near and far; rule,,"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student maps who an invented community decision affects near and far (moving a bus stop) and which rules or groups it touches, plans an option fair to all and judges it against a group the map first missed. Grade 12 weighs a whole community."
sm,12,g12-sm-have-a-say,CASEL.Self-Management.7,g11-sm-have-a-say,,goal steps,goal steps,voice; process; route; follow through,social-studies:gov-local-action (studying a local policy issue in teams),"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans, for an invented recent graduate, how to have a say in a community or workplace process (a suggestion through a workplace's own channel, a comment to a local service the graduate uses), with the route, evidence and trade-offs, and judges which plan would most likely work. Nothing is carried out in a real public body; real civic action stays in the government course (gov-local-action). No cause picked by the course. Grade 12 uses voice past the school walls through the invented case (Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-sel-1)."
rel,12,g12-rel-stand-up,CASEL.Relationship Skills.9,g11-rel-stand-up,,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,upstander; mentor; safe option; follow up,,"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. As an invented senior mentor who sees a younger student left out in a hurtful way at a school event, the student designs an upstander plan (the safest option, the words, support afterward, when to report), rehearses the words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, then writes how the same steps would carry to a first job, and judges the plan. Grade 12 adds the mentor role and the carry to work. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course."
sa,12,g12-sa-snap-judgments,CASEL.Self-Awareness.6,g11-sa-snap-judgments,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,assumption; first impression; coworker; fair,,"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented new employee could check a snap judgment about a coworker, with what to ask and what evidence would change it, and judges the plan. Grade 12 checks one's own judgments at work. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group."
rel,12,g12-rel-different-ways,CASEL.Relationship Skills.3,g11-rel-different-ways,,,,workplace; generations; respect; norm,computer-science:g12-diverse-teams (how differences on a team shape the work),"Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. In an invented workplace or community team of different cultures, ages and backgrounds, the student plans working norms that respect each member, names what each adapts, and judges them after a scene. Grade 12 works across difference past school. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners."
rds,12,g12-rds-ask-questions,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1,g11-rds-ask-questions,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,question; offer; terms; open,,"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. Before an invented choice past school (accepting a job offer, signing a lease), the student plans the questions to ask, who to ask, and what would change the choice, then judges whether the questions found what mattered. Grade 12 asks questions about adult choices."
rds,12,g12-rds-check-facts,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2,g11-rds-check-facts,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,evidence; numbers; offer; compare,ela:e12-info-multiple-sources (integrating and evaluating sources in different media),"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student compares two invented offers after school (two training programs, two jobs) on the facts and numbers, names what the numbers cannot show, plans a choice and judges it with the look-back step. Grade 12 checks the facts of a choice past school."
rds,12,g12-rds-solve-problems,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3,g11-rds-solve-problems,,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,stop-think-choose; problem size meter,problem; community; plan; evaluate,,"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The class takes a problem in the community it can act on (a park cleanup, a book drive), and beside it a problem in an invented graduate's own life; the student designs the fix with partners, carries it out where allowed, and judges it. Grade 12 solves problems in community life."
rds,12,g12-rds-think-ahead,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4,g11-rds-think-ahead,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,opportunity cost; long term; risk; review,"social-studies:econ-scarcity-choice (scarcity, trade-offs and opportunity cost)","Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student lays out the long-term results of options in an invented choice after school (a loan for a car, working a year before training), plans the pick with its opportunity cost, and judges it against how the case unfolds. Grade 12 thinks ahead about adult choices."
rds,12,g12-rds-careful-thinking,CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5,g11-rds-careful-thinking,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,process; sales pressure; adult choice; reflect,,"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose on an invented adult choice made under pressure from a salesperson or a deadline, and reflects on which step protected the choice. Grade 12 thinks carefully about choices past school."
sa,12,g12-sa-tell-truth,CASEL.Self-Awareness.4,g11-sa-tell-truth,,stop-think-choose,stop-think-choose,integrity; workplace; honesty; cost,,"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans what an invented new employee should do and say when a coworker asks them to cover a mistake, names the cost, and judges the plan with the look-back step. Grade 12 acts on values at work. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct."
rel,12,g12-rel-say-no,CASEL.Relationship Skills.6,g11-rel-say-no,,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,stop-think-choose; I-statement frame,refusal; workplace; exit; values,,"Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a refusal plan for an invented pressure past school (a coworker pushing them to clock in for someone who is late, a crew pushing a risky shortcut on a job), rehearses it against a card character, and judges which part held. Grade 12 says no at work. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence)."
rel,12,g12-rel-ask-offer-help,CASEL.Relationship Skills.8,g11-rel-ask-offer-help,,problem size meter,problem size meter,support map; services; mentor; follow up,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student builds a support map for an invented graduate's first year (a mentor, a campus or workplace office, a community service, family or friends), plans how to ask in each case and what to do if the first helper cannot help, plans an offer of help to a new coworker or roommate, rehearses one ask aloud with a partner playing the mentor or the office, and judges whether the map covers the cases. Grade 12 maps help after school. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
rel,12,g12-rel-friendships,CASEL.Relationship Skills.2,g11-rel-friendships,,I-statement frame,I-statement frame,distance; keep in touch; new friends; limit,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented graduate keeps a friendship across a move while building new ones, with the I-statement frame for a talk about changed time, rehearses that talk with a partner playing the old friend, and judges the plan after the case. Grade 12 keeps friendships through life after school. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent)."
rel,12,g12-rel-settle-disagreements,CASEL.Relationship Skills.5,g11-rel-settle-disagreements,,talk turn card; I-statement frame,talk turn card; I-statement frame,roommate; agreement; interest; follow-up,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. With a partner the student plans and holds an invented disagreement past school (roommates over chores, coworkers over a shift swap), names interests, agrees a fix with the I-statement frame, and judges it at a follow-up. Grade 12 settles disagreements at home and work. The teacher plays any upset part."
soc,12,g12-soc-their-view,CASEL.Social Awareness.1,g11-soc-their-view,,,,perspective; roommate; coworker; respect,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student states the view of an invented roommate or coworker whose habits and values differ, well enough that the person would agree, plans a talk, and judges it. Grade 12 takes the views of people met after school."
soc,12,g12-soc-care-when-struggle,CASEL.Social Awareness.3,g11-soc-care-when-struggle,,feelings faces,feelings faces,care; distance; check in; outside help,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans care for an invented friend struggling after a move away, from a distance (a call, a visit, connecting them with help), with when to bring in someone else, and judges the plan. Grade 12 cares across distance after school. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, ""If a student tells you something""): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help)."
soc,12,g12-soc-read-feelings,CASEL.Social Awareness.4,g11-soc-read-feelings,,feelings faces,feelings faces,cue; supervisor; message; check in,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student reads feelings in an invented workplace or roommate scene with few cues (a short message from a supervisor), plans a check-in, and judges the reading. Grade 12 reads feelings at work and at home."
soc,12,g12-soc-thankful,CASEL.Social Awareness.5,g11-soc-thankful,,,,gratitude; mentor; note; timing,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans thanks to people who helped an invented senior through school (a coach, a mentor, a neighbor), chooses how and when, and judges the plan. Grade 12 thanks people at a turning point in life."
sm,12,g12-sm-set-goal,CASEL.Self-Management.4,g11-sm-set-goal,,goal steps,goal steps,one-year goal; milestone; support; review,,"Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a one-year goal plan for an invented graduate on the goal steps card, with milestones, supports from the support map, trade-offs and a review date, and judges it after an invented setback. Grade 12 sets goals for the first year after school."
"""Stale-note catch (added 2026-11-13): notes that say a piece of work is not done yet go stale the moment it is.

    python -X utf8 scripts/stale.py check [file ...]

A note that waits on other work carries a marker the checker can test:

    Pending(<what>): <the note>

where <what> is one of
- a file path from the repository root: resolved once the file exists;
- path#text: resolved once that file contains the text (Pending(data/standards/x.txt#RST.11-12.8));
- a plan-flow node id (frame:science:5, prog-review:all): resolved once data/plans/flow-status.csv marks it done.

FAIL: a Pending marker whose work has landed (the note is stale: rewrite it to say what is now true and drop the
marker); a Pending marker naming no file, text or node the checker knows the form of; a work-status phrase
("not yet placed", "until it is added", "to come", "TBD", "placeholder" and the like) outside a Pending marker
(mark it so the checker can follow it, or reword it).
Teaching boundaries ("not yet describe", "not yet measured with a ruler") are about the student, not the work, and
are not flagged. With no files, checks the four progressions and the course frames (docs/courses/**/*.md,
data/frames/*.csv when they exist).
"""
import csv
import glob
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
MARK = re.compile(r'Pending\(([^)]*)\)\s*:')
# Work-status wording: says something about our own unfinished work. Kept narrow so a student boundary
# ("not yet describe") never trips it.
WORK = re.compile(
    r"not yet (in the|placed|written|built|added|loaded|in a curio class|named by|decided|chosen)"
    r"|until (it|they|this|that|the [\w ,'-]{1,40}) (is|are) (added|written|built|loaded|placed|decided|chosen)"
    r"|once (it|they|the [\w ,'-]{1,40}) (is|are|exists?) (added|written|built|loaded|placed|decided)"
    r"|\b(?:yet|still|is|are) to come\b|\bTBD\b|\bTODO\b|placeholder|will be (placed|written|added|decided)"
    r"|held here in words", re.I)
_done = None


def done_nodes():
    global _done
    if _done is None:
        _done = set()
        p = os.path.join(ROOT, 'data', 'plans', 'flow-status.csv')
        if os.path.exists(p):
            for r in csv.DictReader(open(p, encoding='utf-8')):
                if r.get('state') == 'done':
                    _done.add(r['node'])
    return _done


def resolved(what):
    """True if landed, False if still waiting, None if the target is not a form the checker knows."""
    what = what.strip()
    path, _, text = what.partition('#')
    if '/' in path or '.' in os.path.basename(path):
        f = os.path.join(ROOT, path)
        if not os.path.exists(f):
            return False
        if not text:
            return True
        return text in open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
    if ':' in what or re.fullmatch(r'P\d+[a-z]?', what):
        return what in done_nodes()
    return None


def check_text(label, text):
    """Fails for one note. Work-status wording inside a Pending clause (up to the next sentence end) is fine."""
    fails = []
    covered = []
    for m in MARK.finditer(text):
        r = resolved(m.group(1))
        if r is None:
            fails.append('%s: Pending(%s) names no file, file#text or plan node' % (label, m.group(1)))
        elif r:
            fails.append('%s: STALE, Pending(%s) has landed; rewrite the note to say what is now true' % (label, m.group(1)))
        end = text.find('. ', m.end())
        covered.append((m.start(), len(text) if end < 0 else end + 1))
    for m in WORK.finditer(text):
        if not any(a <= m.start() < b for a, b in covered):
            fails.append('%s: work-status wording %r outside a Pending(...) marker' % (label, m.group(0)))
    return fails


def check_file(path):
    rel = os.path.relpath(path, ROOT).replace('\\', '/')
    fails = []
    if path.endswith('.csv'):
        rows = list(csv.DictReader(open(path, encoding='utf-8')))
        for i, r in enumerate(rows, 2):
            key = r.get('skill_id') or r.get('id') or r.get('node') or 'line %d' % i
            for col in ('notes', 'note', 'detail'):
                if r.get(col):
                    fails += check_text('%s %s' % (rel, key), r[col])
    else:
        for i, line in enumerate(open(path, encoding='utf-8'), 1):
            fails += check_text('%s:%d' % (rel, i), line)
    return fails


def default_files():
    pats = ['data/progressions/social-studies.csv', 'data/progressions/science.csv', 'data/progressions/ela.csv',
            'data/progressions/math.csv', 'data/frames/*.csv', 'docs/courses/*/frame.md']
    out = []
    for p in pats:
        out += sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ROOT, p)))
    return out


def main(a):
    if not a or a[0] != 'check':
        raise SystemExit(__doc__)
    files = [os.path.join(ROOT, f) for f in a[1:]] or default_files()
    fails = []
    for f in files:
        fails += check_file(f)
    print('stale: %d file(s), %d failure(s)' % (len(files), len(fails)))
    for x in fails:
        print('  FAIL', x)
    sys.exit(1 if fails else 0)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main(sys.argv[1:])
