152: | 8 | 10 | Hard conversations. *How do I hold a hard talk when both of us are upset?* | 10 | g10-rel-ask-offer-help; g10-rel-settle-disagreements; g10-sa-name-feelings; g10-sm-big-feelings; g10-sm-calm-down; g10-sm-have-a-say | None | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e10-rl-complex-characters, characters whose motives conflict and change | Two groups. Group 1, the talk itself: an invented hard conversation over a shared commitment planned and held with a partner, each writing the other's interests first, a fix agreed with the I-statement frame and checked later (RS5); a coping plan for two people in an invented tense talk (an agreed pause signal, a tool each could use, how to come back to the talk), rehearsed and judged on whether the pause helped both (SM2); an offer of help to an invented friend who may not want it, how the offer sounds from the friend's side, the support map used to plan when an adult must come in, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend (RS8). Group 2, feelings on both sides and having a say: the feelings of both people in an invented hard conversation charted, where they set each other off, and where naming a feeling aloud could slow the talk (SA3); a plan for keeping a strong feeling from driving an invented hard talk, with how the other person's feeling might rise too, judged in a rehearsed scene (SM1); a plan to raise a class concern with a teacher or school leader (an invented crowded test schedule), how the adult may see it, the plan revised to answer that view, judged after a rehearsal (SM7). Retrieval only: grade 9 units 1, 2 and 4 rows (the coping plan, the support map, the I-statement frame) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen plan page for a new invented hard talk (both sides' interests and feelings, the pause signal, the fix) and the talk rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: the agreed pause signal in class discussions. Family activity: agree one pause signal at home. Tools: the coping plan page, 