# Curio Class: the foundation (layer 1)

Gathered 2026-11-11 (Cory). This is the one home for what Curio Class is for, who it serves, what it promises and
what it assumes. Every layer above it (the approach to teaching, the progressions, the scopes and sequences, the section plans, the
tools) is built on this page and may not contradict it. Until today these lines were spread over docs/VISION.md,
docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md and docs/agents/decisions/BASELINE.md; those files keep their detail and now
point here. Where one of them disagrees with this page, this page wins and the other file is fixed.

It changes rarely, and only with Cory. The layers and the order of work are in docs/ROADMAP.md ("The layers").

## 1. What it is for

A free, complete, open K-12 classroom toolkit: interactive tools, printables, lessons and units for every standard,
so a teacher can run a full course without paying for curriculum or software (VISION, Mission).

**The end goal.** A teacher can run any course on Curio Class from the first day to the last, free, with no account,
and the course would stand up to a state adoption review. A course is a grade of a subject or a high school course.
Every core subject is planned top down (progression, course frames, scopes and sequences, unit plans) before days
are written (docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md, Cory 2026-11-13); each course is finished as a whole before it is called
finished. The earlier pilot-first bend (2026-11-11) is retired; the probe and guard replace it.

**We own the full course** (Cory, 2026-11-11; base texts added 2026-11-12). Curio Class owns every course: its
shape, tone, format, gates and reads. Where a well-reviewed open program carries a licence as open as ours (CC BY
or public domain), it can be the course's **base text**: adapted into Curio Class days, molded to our needs and
voice, with a credit line that says it was changed (math: Illustrative Mathematics). Programs under non-commercial
or share-alike terms are evidence only, for order, time, models and anchors, never their wording or problems,
so every page stays CC BY 4.0. We do not stitch two programs together: each hangs together through its own routines and pictures, and a
mix is weaker than either. A tool built to a clear job in our unit will also fit the same lesson in a course a
teacher already uses, and that is welcome, but it is not the plan.

## 2. Who it is for

| Who | What that means for the work |
|---|---|
| **A classroom teacher** running a course | The main reader of every scope and sequence, section plan and teacher page. Teacher pages teach the teacher |
| **A parent teaching one child at home** (named 2026-11-11; how far, decided 2026-11-12) | The second audience, served by the same course, never a separate track. Every lesson day carries an "at home" block (a 30 to 40 minute one-child path, what the parent says where the class has partner talk, which items to do, home swaps for materials); a course gives placement from its readiness checks, a numbered calendar ("36-week" in Cory's 2026-11-12 call; the school calendar is 160 days over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) and a record that works as a portfolio; answer keys explain, not only list; sample pieces show what grade-level work looks like. Not planned: separate home courses or multi-age plans |
| **A student alone** | The site is public and a child may open any tool with no adult present. Every tool stands alone: defaults work for a solo user and teacher toggles are extras (BASELINE rule 4) |

## 3. The promises

These hold even where a review, a district system or a convenience would reward breaking them.

1. **Free for good.** No paywall, ever (ROADMAP, Goal).
2. **No accounts, no ads, no tracking, no student analytics** (VISION, "What we will not do"). A tool cannot
   remember a student from one day to the next and there is no live teacher dashboard. We chose that.
3. **Results live with the student and the teacher.** A score code the student shows, and printable record sheets.
   The score code records only what a tool can mark (ROADMAP, "What a finished course holds", item 4).
4. **Every tool works offline as a single file.** Where a tool stores anything, it says on screen that it never
   leaves the device (BASELINE rule 7).
5. **Paper is a first-class product.** Worksheets, units and printables matter as much as screens. Printables read in
   black and white.
6. **Honest words.** We say taught, practiced or shown, per the ledger, never more. Practice tools are not
   diagnoses and score codes are not proof. We never say Curio Class was reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by
   anyone. Public copy names no companies (BASELINE rule 15).
7. **Classroom-safe, and plain about hard facts.** History and science are not softened; they are told with care
   for the people involved, and a fresh agent checks sensitive content before it goes live (BASELINE rules 5, 6, 8).
8. **We do not copy a branded product** in name, look or text, and we respect every licence: read and link, adapt
   only where the licence allows.

## 4. The bar

State adoption is the bar we build to (Cory, 2026-11-10; BASELINE rule 20). A full course should meet the substance
of what state and national curriculum reviews ask for. We do not pursue formal adoption now. The bar applies to
courses and the shelves that feed them; Curiosities and Recess are outside it. The bar never outranks the promises
above. What it asks and what we took from it: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md. The rubrics we read ourselves against are filed
as docs/RUBRIC-*.md.

**Quality over speed, and over saved work** (BASELINE rules 1, 2, 14). Each piece is really good before the next
wave grows. An existing spec or tool carries no weight against what a unit needs: if the unit needs something
different, the old work loses.

## 5. What we assume about the room

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory) unless marked.

1. **Grades 3 to 12: one device per student on a tool day.** The courses are designed that way and say so plainly.
   Every tool day still carries one line for a single projector, because some rooms have nothing else and a reviewer
   will ask.
2. **Kindergarten to grade 2: one shared classroom device**, always with a paper path (Cory, 2026-11-14).
3. **The physical world is written into the lesson plan.** A tool does not replace rolling the marble, using a
   protractor or reading a whole book. Hands-on work, materials and their cost are part of the unit, with a path for
   a teacher who has nothing (docs/reviews/science-4-spine-2026-11-11.md, finding 7).
4. **A teacher judges open work.** A tool marks what has a right answer. For writing, argument and discussion, the
   tool gives the work a structure so it is quick to read, and print carries the rest: a rubric, sample pieces at
   each score level, and a look-for sheet. Our job is to make that judging fast and consistent (BASELINE rule 21
   already says a tool cannot mark writing or talk).
5. **Time.** A scope and sequence states its own year and period length and gives a shorter path where ordinary schools have less
   time than a full program assumes.

## 6. What a tool is for

Decided 2026-11-11 (Cory), from the discussion recorded in docs/agents/decisions/DESK-ANSWERS.md.

1. **A tool is the end piece of instruction, a means to a defined end.** The end is defined in a section plan: what
   each day is for, where a tool is used and for what job. **No tool is built or refined until a section plan names
   its job.**
2. **A tool can be whatever the unit needs.** The section plan asks, for each key moment, what the ideal instrument for
   that idea would be. Sometimes that is a tool nobody has specced; sometimes it is a marble and a ramp. The limits
   are the promises and assumptions on this page and the cost of doing a tool well, not what software can do.
3. **Key tools and light tools.** A key tool is the one a day is planned around: it does the day's main job in a
   way print cannot (feedback on each try, help that fades as the student succeeds, many cases fast), and the paper
   path is the fallback (reworded 2026-11-11; it had read "one the unit cannot run without", which no tool can meet
   while every plan must also run on paper). Key tools are built first and hold the course up. Light tools (called
   practice tools before 2026-11-11) are lighter, built from shared templates over the long term, and still correct
   and still meet the three hooks. Lighter never means lower quality. A course is runnable when its key tools are
   live and print covers the rest. "Key" is a mark given by the section plan; the roles of BASELINE rule 21 (teach,
   see why, practice) still say what a tool does.
4. **Curiosities and Recess** are play and wonder. They claim no standard and are outside the bar (VISION).
5. **Tools and specs made before this page are sorted by demand** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 26). Named by a
   section plan: course work. Live, passing the teaching test, not named: stays on the shelf, free-standing, and
   claims no course coverage. Everything else: frozen. A script makes the list; nobody argues a tool into a course.

## 7. How the work is checked

1. **The curriculum expert reads cold, often** (docs/agents/EXPERT.md). Cory's attention and a teacher's are the
   scarce things, so frequent cold reads by the expert, which teaches itself from outside sources first, are the
   main review at every layer. It is the main read, not the only one (changed 2026-11-11, when Cory accepted the cold strategic review). The
   writer and the expert are the same kind of model, so a cold read removes anchoring but not a shared mistake.
   Three things stand beside it: the sources the expert must open before it judges (the pedagogy and standards
   data, which Cory expects to grow), a script for everything that can be computed (item 2), a Codex read of a
   different model kind, and the agent reads in item 3.
2. **A machine checks what a machine can** (BASELINE rule 27). Standard codes, day counts, coverage of every part
   of a standard, and every answer that can be computed. A key the script cannot reproduce fails the build.
3. **Reads beside the expert** (Cory, 2026-11-11; BASELINE rule 28; changed 2026-11-14). Every row below is
   required before the course it touches is called finished. On 2026-11-14 Cory said there is no outside support
   ("We have no support. Our agents are our best hope for success"): the content rows are agent reads, each by a
   fresh agent that studies its sources first (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md). No outside human reader is
   planned. Pilots are retired (2026-11-13): the probe and guard (docs/pipeline/PROBE-GUARD.md) take their place.

   | Who | What they read or do | When |
   |---|---|---|
   | The expert agent (was a working teacher on the pilot unit) | Reads every plan and the probe and guard days against the subject lens and rubric | Every unit plan; the probe and the guard |
   | The answer-key agent (was a math content reviewer) | Solves every item of one whole course's checks cold and compares with the key, beside the script check | Before a math course is called finished |
   | The safety agent (was a science teacher) | Safety and materials of every investigation, against published school science safety guidance; and every PE unit with striking tools, mats, climbing, resistance training or an outdoor trip, against published school PE safety guidance (Cory, 2026-11-21) | Before a science course is called finished; before such a PE unit goes live |
   | The rights agent (was a second reader on rights) | The licence and source of every text a course teaches from | Before an ELA text is taught |
   | The Spanish agent (was a native Spanish reader) | Family notes, and student materials if they are made | Before Spanish is published in a course |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a health specialist) | The sensitive health topics, against public health guidance | Before those units go live |
   | The sensitivity agent (was a reader from the community concerned) | Native, cultural, disability and hard-history content, after studying the community's own published voice | Before those units go live |

   Rights and Spanish became agent reads on 2026-11-14 (DESK-ANSWERS, the continuity review's four questions); the
   funding rows for a paid Spanish review and teacher pilots stay, marked "only if funded".
4. **Lower layers are solid enough to build on, and expected to be revised** by what is built on them. No layer is
   declared finished in the abstract.
5. **What goes to Cory:** scope, audience, money, accounts, outside people, a live deploy, anything that moves a
   standard between sections, and anything this page cannot settle. Item-level choices inside the standards' limits
   are settled by the expert and reach Cory in a weekly digest (BASELINE, "What goes to Cory").

## Open questions on this page

- Closed 2026-11-14: the device assumption for kindergarten to grade 2 (section 5).
# How a course gets made: the path as it stands, last changed 2026-11-22

The path is judged against the end goal, FOUNDATION 1 (a teacher can run any course from the first day to the last,
free, and it would stand up to a state adoption review), and the bar (quality over speed and over saved work).
Changes to existing work are allowed where they teach better. The decisions behind it are in
docs/agents/decisions/DESK-ANSWERS.md.

## 1. The answer

1. **Plan every core subject from the widest scope down, one layer at a time, before writing days.** ROADMAP's
   layers set the order: foundation, standards, approach, **progressions** (layer 4: what each grade owns, assumes
   and hands on, per subject, across all its grades), each course's frame and scope and sequence (with its map
   against the base text, where there is one), then units. Progressions and course frames are built and reviewed
   for all four core subjects together (math, ELA, science and social studies: 58 courses, 52 of them core, which
   get frames now; the 6 electives wait, see After the core subjects). Social studies then goes first through scope
   and sequence and units; the other three subjects' scopes and sequences are written together, and their units go
   one subject at a time, each reviewed from its progression down. Sections, days, lessons and tools come only after
   every core subject's units are planned and reviewed. This order replaces BASELINE rule 25 ("prove at the bottom
   before planning wider") (settled 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS). How much planning saves at the day level is not
   measured yet; the probe and the guard measure it.
2. **Test the plans against real days twice, with "on" and "off" set beforehand.** Once early, as a **probe**: a
   few days written in each subject once its units are planned, first in social studies at baseline 2, before the
   other three subjects copy the unit template. Once at the end, as the **guard**: a few days written in each
   subject once every unit is planned, before full writing starts. Each measures how far off the plans were
   (section 3, steps 4 and 7; the measures are set in P4).
3. **Learn the process on clean work first; convert old work last.** Social studies goes first because nothing of
   it had been planned, so the process is learned without bending old work into it. Science, ELA and math then move
   forward together; math's old work (a grade 4 scope and sequence, unit plans and 48 written days) is handled by
   writing blind and then reconciling, so it no longer waits for the other subjects (settled 2026-11-15, see
   DESK-ANSWERS).
4. **What crosses days goes into data, with gates, not into prose.** Hand-backs, materials, reserved examples and
   the base-text map are small CSVs that plan.py and lesson.py check against the plans and the day text (built for
   grade 4 on 2026-11-13), with a minutes-and-branches check beside them. Every subject uses them; the progressions
   use the same idea one level up.
5. **Base texts stay, for quality, where a subject has one.** IM for math, OpenSciEd for middle school science, EL
   for ELA K to 5. The gain is real but not automatic. Course order follows what teaches best: a base text's order
   is the default only where the loss check finds it at least as good for learning.
6. **A second kind of reader at each baseline, and agents in place of people.** Every reliability number is the
   model judging itself, so the expert agent was checked and strengthened first (P5), and Codex reads beside it at
   every baseline. No outside human reader is planned (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS): the agent reads
   FOUNDATION 7.3 names (answer key, safety, sensitivity, the expert's text read) take those places.
7. **No spending ceiling.** Codex is used freely; be judicious with tokens, never at the cost of quality (settled
   2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS).

## 2. Why this path

The path rests on the unit 4 test of 2026-11-13 (grade 4 math, days 25 to 28 on the IM base text, against days 7
to 9 of unit 2 as a control). Read safely, the base-text days came out at least as clean as the control (12 residual
problems against 13, none in the source-adapted parts) at about the same writer cost (3.6% over unit 2, inside the
day-to-day spread). Of the 26 problems only the expert caught, planning well can prevent up to about 9 (the
cross-day and stale-plan kinds); the 11 in the day itself are left to the writer and the reads whatever the plans
are. Samples are small (four test days and three control days), all judged by the same kind of model that wrote
them, and COST.md's "tokens" are probably the agent's end-of-run context size rather than billed tokens.

The numbers are in docs/reviews/pipeline-audit-2026-11-13.md, the eight unit 4 reads (cheap-d025 to d028, day-025
to day-028), the two reliability reads (reliability-u04-d025-028, reliability-u02-d007-009),
docs/reviews/plan-s04-replan-2026-11-13.md, docs/pilots/COST.md, docs/pilots/REVIEW-CADENCE.md,
data/plans/defects.csv, docs/sources/INTEGRATION-PLAN.md and docs/COURSES-STATUS.md.

## 3. The path, step by step

Where a subject has work on file it is not thrown away: it is input to the step, read against the new layer above
it (section 4).

### Pre-work (before step 1)

All pre-work is settled before step 1 starts (settled 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS).

- **P1. The social studies standard, made usable for the plan.** Option (d) of 2026-11-12 stands: C3 for skills and
  Massachusetts 2018 as the content anchor in its course order, checked unit by unit against a named few states,
  with Curio Class's own content replacing the anchor's own-state year, under the values rule (settled 2026-11-12
  and 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS; docs/reviews/social-studies-content-standard-2026-11-12.md). All five pieces
  are recorded in docs/courses/social-studies/STANDARD.md. Done 2026-11-13.
  - **P1a. The anchor.** data/standards/ma-hss-2018.txt: 997 Massachusetts 2018 ids, each with an own-words line
    (its terms allow free non-commercial copying with credit, but Curio Class pages are CC BY, so we store ids and
    our own words, as with CCSS), checked by scripts/standards.py.
  - **P1b. The course list.** data/courses.csv is redrawn to Massachusetts's order, with COURSES-STATUS rebuilt
    (Cory's yes on Desk card q-ss-course-list).
  - **P1c. The check states.** California, Tennessee and New York (California and Tennessee by ids and our own
    words only; New York for high school order). The lead names them.
  - **P1d. The replaced state-study year.** What Curio Class teaches in place of Massachusetts grade 3:
    docs/courses/social-studies/grade-3-outline.md.
  - **P1e. Psychology.** An elective, taught after the core.
  No social studies progression starts before P1a to P1d are done. Tasks set aside until a trigger are in
  data/plans/later.csv, shown on the Desk under the plan flow.
- **P2. A social studies base text.** The scout found no openly licensed social studies curriculum that fits a
  whole course, so social studies stays the clean-slate subject and science goes second
  (docs/reviews/social-studies-open-material-2026-11-13.md; the partial bases are settled on Desk card
  q-ss-partial-bases). Done 2026-11-13.
- **P3. The rule change on record.** BASELINE rule 25 is replaced by this order, and HANDOFF and ROADMAP point here,
  so no worker follows the old rule.
- **P4. The probe and guard measures, set before any plan is written.** For each probe (step 4) and the guard (step
  7): the share of day findings traced to the unit plan or above (on: about the unit 4 re-plan's level or lower;
  off: clearly higher); how many unit plans need a change; whether a change is to the template or to one unit. The
  exact thresholds are in docs/pipeline/PROBE-GUARD.md (Desk card q-probe-guard). Done 2026-11-13.
- **P5. The expert reader, made good enough for this work.** docs/agents/EXPERT.md and the briefs in
  docs/pipeline/briefs/ were built for math plans and days, so four pieces came first. Done 2026-11-13.
  - **P5a. Subject lenses** (docs/agents/lenses/): one short file per core subject the expert reads first, saying
    what good teaching of it looks like and the errors reviewers catch, with its sources read once into our own
    words.
  - **P5b. A bar for science and social studies:** docs/RUBRIC-SCIENCE.md (first draft) and the social studies
    section of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md.
  - **P5c. Briefs for the new layers:** progression-read and frame-read.
  - **P5d. Calibration before trust.** A small test set with planted faults of known kinds (a skill owned twice, a
    handoff that skips a grade, a model used before it is taught, a standard taught in part, a values-rule miss, an
    exit that misses the goal), plus past findings whose truth is settled, read cold by the expert and by Codex.
    The line: at least 8 in 10 planted major faults caught, few false alarms, and no kind missed by both. Run 1
    (docs/reviews/calibration-2026-11-13.md): the expert caught 18 of 18 planted majors and Codex passed too; both
    are trusted for progressions, frames, scopes and sequences and unit plans (Desk card q-calibration-1).
    EXPERT.md gained a leniency rule and a severity rule. Re-run after any change to EXPERT.md or a brief; the
    re-run with fresh plantings is later.csv L10.
- **Who decides, at every review and baseline.** The expert settles item-level choices. Cory decides go or no-go
  at each baseline, at the final review and on an "off" result from a probe or the guard, and any change to scope
  (what a course or grade teaches, the course list, a subject's order).

### Step 1. Progressions for the four core subjects, then a review

One progression per subject, all its grades, kept as data with gates: data/progressions/<subject>.csv, one row per
skill, with the columns strand, grade, skill_id, standard, assumes (skill ids from earlier grades), hands_on (skill
ids the next grade assumes), model_in, model_out, words, and cross_subject.

- plan.py checks that a course's readiness items name skill ids an earlier grade owns, that every skill id is owned
  by exactly one grade, and that a carried model (number line, tape diagram, timeline, map scale) is introduced
  before a course leans on it. The gate is scripts/progression.py (inside plan.py check): ids, grades, real codes,
  every standard owned by one grade or excluded, both ends of every handoff, models handed on before use, real
  cross_subject codes, and forward cross-subject links; it notes C3 indicators in two grades, skipped handoffs and
  model gaps. A `~CODE` marks a row that starts or applies a standard another row owns.
- **Across subjects**: the cross_subject column records where one subject leans on another (graphs in grade 6
  science against grade 6 math; reading demands against ELA; timelines and number lines between social studies and
  math). A course may not lean on a skill no subject has taught by that grade. This is why all four are built
  together.
- data/sequence.csv stays as the module order (which topic follows which); the new files sit under it at skill
  level, and sequence.csv's open science errors are fixed in this step. ROADMAP layer 4's home holds the new files.
- Built from the standards and the progressions documents, with each base text's own sequence as evidence (IM K to
  12, OpenSciEd middle school, EL K to 5); the existing grades 3 to 5 math page is folded in.
# The Curio Class approach to teaching

This page says how Curio Class teaches, in every subject, and why. It is layer 3 of a course: it is built on
[FOUNDATION.md](FOUNDATION.md) and on the standards, and every scope and sequence and section plan is built on it. Each subject
adds its own page, which says how the subject carries out each principle. Mathematics and English language arts have pages,
[APPROACH-MATH.md](APPROACH-MATH.md) and [APPROACH-ELA.md](APPROACH-ELA.md); for science and social studies,
the subject lens (docs/agents/lenses/) plus its rubric stands in through the scopes and sequences and unit plans, and
a short approach page per subject is written before days (Cory, 2026-11-14). The subjects after the core have pages
in their course folders: [health](courses/health/APPROACH.md),
[physical education](courses/physical-education/APPROACH.md), [arts](courses/arts/APPROACH.md),
[computer science](courses/computer-science/APPROACH.md), [social and emotional learning](courses/sel/APPROACH.md),
[library and research skills](courses/library/APPROACH.md), [English language development](courses/eld/APPROACH.md),
[career education](courses/career-education/APPROACH.md) and
[world languages](courses/world-languages/APPROACH.md) (shelved).

Two honest notes. Nothing in Curio Class has been tried with children yet, so this page makes no claim about
results; it explains choices, and its rules are rules for section plans, not descriptions of lessons that exist.
And every claim about how students learn rests on a source opened and read for this page. The list at the end
says how much of each was read. Where a federal practice guide rates its own evidence (strong, moderate, or
minimal; the oldest guide says low), that rating is given. The detail behind each claim (who was studied, how
many studies, how big the effect) is in [APPROACH-SOURCES.md](APPROACH-SOURCES.md), "Evidence notes".

Second draft, 2026-11-11, revised after a cold read by the curriculum expert
([reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md)). Cory settled three points the same day
(how a lesson opens, no timers in tools, narrowed on 2026-11-14 to timers with a grounded purpose, BASELINE rule 30; a wording change in SPEC.md). The page as a whole still waits for his
read.

Changed 2026-11-12, Cory's yes on card q-ela-piece-2: principle 3 and the modelling line under "Where the
evidence is thin or contested" now give the federal ratings for reading and writing
([reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md), finding F4).

> **What this means in your room, or at your table**
>
> - We find out what your student already knows before teaching something new.
> - New things are shown clearly, step by step, with examples. Then the help is taken away, on purpose.
> - A lesson may open with a short question or task. The teaching always follows the same day.
> - Old work keeps coming back, from memory, so it is not forgotten.
> - Every day leaves you something you can act on: what was understood, and what to do if it was not.
> - Students talk and write about what they learn. You judge that work; we give you what you need to judge it.
> - A student who is behind works on the grade's content with extra support, not on easier content.
> - A screen is used where it helps, and real things are used where the idea lives in real things.
> - If you are teaching one child, every unit has a note for you.

## The role of the standards

The standards decide **what** a student should know and be able to do by the end of a grade, and in what words.
A Curio Class check uses the standard's own verb, so "explain" is not quietly swapped for "pick".

The standards leave the teaching to us. The mathematics standards say so directly: they "do not dictate
curriculum or teaching methods" and do not define the supports for students who are behind, ahead, or learning
English (Common Core State Standards for Mathematics, introduction). Each subject page says the same for its
own standards from its own standards document. So the order of the year, how each idea is taught, the practice,
the checks and the supports are our choices, and this page and the subject pages are where they are explained.

### Which standards: national, not federal (from the Phase 1 scope, 2026-10-22)

There are no federal K-12 content standards. Federal law (ESSA, and earlier the Department of Education Organization Act) bars the federal government from setting curriculum, and each state adopts its own standards. The highest level that exists is the **national consensus framework** for each subject, written by states, teachers or professional associations. Most state standards are adopted from or modeled on these. That is our alignment target everywhere, which keeps us from tying the site to any one state.

Where a subject has no national framework (Precalculus, Statistics, Calculus), the top level is the College Board AP course framework, which every state recognizes.

| Subject | Framework (version) | Code level in our data | Example | Verified against |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Math | Common Core State Standards for Mathematics (2010) | cluster | `3.NF.A`, `A-REI.B.4` | Cluster list per grade and domain from corestandards.org (from memory, spot-checked) |
| ELA | Common Core ELA (2010) | standard | `RL.4.2`, `RF.K.1` | Strand rules: RL/RI/W 1-10, SL/L 1-6, RF only K-5 (RF.1-2 only K-1), no RL.8 |
