### 7. The historical sensitivity pack lacks the community�?Ts own published voice

**Severity:** Moderate  
**LAYER:** frame  
**REPEAT:** yes, source sufficiency was raised in the K to 2 second-model read  
**Confidence:** firm

**Location:** Base text and Sensitivity reads bullets; units 9 and 16; For the lead 11.

The frame names only the National Archives opinion page for the sensitivity agent�?Ts historical preparation. FOUNDATION section 7 also requires the community�?Ts own published voice.

The [National Archives page](https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/brown-v-board-of-education) supports the constitutional ruling and distinguishes it from later implementation. It does not replace a community account.

**Exact fix:** Name Black American families and plaintiffs as the community concerned, and add [Cheryl Brown Henderson�?Ts account of preserving the Brown history](https://www.brownvboard.org/brown-foundation-story) to the required preparation pack. Require separate sourcing for any specific personal costs taught in unit 16.

Use the precise historical statement:

> In 1954, the Supreme Court held racial segregation in public schools unconstitutional. Implementation followed and met resistance.

I disagree with the combined expert report�?Ts claim that using Brown twice necessarily prevents growth. Unit 9 asks students to consider excluded people�?Ts perspectives; unit 16 asks about the costs of change. Keep Brown as an explicitly identified revisit, with different evidence and questions.

