 succeeded in 116ms:
g10-rel-talk-listen | Unit 1, Listening to understand. With a partner, the student plans a talk on an invented disagreement where each person thinks the other is not listening, rehearses it with both people using the check-back step, and judges from the partner's account whether each felt understood. Grade 10 brings the other person's side of the talk into the plan.
g10-soc-their-view | Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student writes the strongest form of two invented classmates' opposed views on a school question, plans a conversation that finds common ground, and judges whether each person would accept how they were described. New this year: two people's views held at full strength.
g10-soc-read-feelings | Unit 1, Listening to understand. In an invented scene the student reads one person's feelings, then reads the same moment through the second person's eyes, plans a response for each, and judges which reading the case bears out. This year the scene is read from both sides.
g10-sa-feel-think-link | Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student maps the think-feel-do loops of two invented people in one misunderstanding, plans where either could break the loop, and judges which break the case supports. Grade 10 runs the loop for a second person beside the first. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud.
g10-soc-why-rules | Unit 1, Listening to understand. The student traces how an invented school system (a points reward for attendance) looks to a student it helps and to one it pushes out, designs a change, and judges it from both views. A second person's view now tests the redesign. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names.
g10-rel-settle-disagreements | Unit 2, Hard conversations. With a partner the student plans and holds an invented hard conversation over a shared commitment, each writing the other's interests before the talk, then agrees a fix with the I-statement frame and checks it later. Grade 10: each side plans from the other's interests. The teacher plays any upset part.
g10-rel-ask-offer-help | Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student plans how to offer help to an invented friend who may not want it, writes how the offer could sound from the friend's side, uses the support map to plan when an adult must come in, rehearses the offer with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan after the scene. New in grade 10: help planned around the other person's wishes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. A worry about a friend's safety goes to an adult, even when the friend asked for a secret. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g10-sm-big-feelings | Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student designs a plan for keeping a strong feeling from driving an invented hard talk, adds how the other person's feeling might rise too and what the plan does about it, and judges it in a rehearsed scene. What grade 10 adds: two people's feelings in one plan.
g10-sm-calm-down | Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student builds a coping plan for two people in an invented tense talk: an agreed pause signal, a tool each could use, and how to come back to the talk. The student rehearses it with a partner and judges whether the pause helped both. New this year: the coping plan works for two people at once. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g10-sa-name-feelings | Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student charts the feelings of both people in an invented hard conversation, finds where they set each other off, and plans where naming a feeling aloud could slow the talk, judging the chart against the scene. Grade 10 charts a second person beside the first. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
g10-sm-have-a-say | Unit 2, Hard conversations. The student plans how to raise a class concern with a teacher or school leader (an invented crowded test schedule), writes how the adult may see it, revises the plan to answer that view, and judges the talk after a rehearsal. The other person's view now shapes how voice is used. Student voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course.
g10-rel-different-ways | Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for working across difference in 9 to 12. In an invented mixed team, the student plans working norms with a partner from another background, each writing how the other sees a clash (eye contact, directness, time), and judges the norms after a scene. Grade 10 builds the norms from both people's sides. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners.
g10-soc-fair-rules | Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for unfair norms in 9 to 12. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case (school segregation, struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954, or apartheid as world history teaches it), writes how the norm looked to the people it held down and to those who kept it, and plans a fair change to an invented school norm. New this year: the norm seen through the eyes of the people it hurt. Teacher note: settled history, taught with dignity: school segregation was struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954; apartheid's laws were repealed from 1990 to 1991 and it ended with the 1994 election; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (wh2-apartheid).
g10-sa-who-i-am | Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student writes how an invented character sees their own groups and how a newcomer sees them, plans how the character could make room for the newcomer while staying themselves, and judges the plan after a scene. Grade 10 sets one person's sense of self beside how another sees them. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check.
g10-sa-my-strengths | Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student plans how an invented team could use a member's strength from a home language or culture (translating for a visitor, a family tradition of storytelling), writes how the member might want it used or not, and judges the plan from that member's side. This year the strength holder's own wishes shape the plan. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about.
g10-sa-snap-judgments | Unit 3, Culture and respect. Main year for snap judgments in 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented student could check a snap judgment about a classmate from another school, writes how the judged classmate would experience the guess, and judges whether the plan corrected it. What grade 10 adds: the judged person's view. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group.
g10-soc-notice-good | Unit 3, Culture and respect. The student plans credit for an invented teammate whose strength others overlook because it looks different from theirs, writes how the teammate would hear it, and judges the words. The teammate's view now decides whether credit lands.
g10-sa-what-interests-me | Unit 3, Culture and respect. Privately the student plans how to weigh an interest against what another person expects of them (a friend, a coach), writes that person's view fairly, and judges after a set time whether the choice kept their own purpose. Grade 10 brings another person's expectations into the plan. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content.
g10-rel-friendships | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans how an invented student could set a limit in a friendship (a friend who wants every free period together), writes how the friend may hear it, plans the talk with the I-statement frame and its check-back, rehearses it with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan from both sides after the scene. Grade 10 weighs the friend's side of the limit. The limits are about time, help and plans. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent, with consent and boundaries in health row g9-meh-boundaries-consent).
g10-rel-say-no | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student designs a refusal for an invented case where a friend presses them to lie to a teacher for the friend, writes how the friend sees the ask, plans an alternative that keeps the friendship, rehearses against a card character and judges what held. New this year: the plan answers the other person's reasons. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence).
g10-sa-tell-truth | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. In an invented case where telling the truth lets down a friend, the student plans what to say, writes how the friend and the teacher each see it, and judges the plan with the look-back step. Grade 10 holds honesty and a second person's view together. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct.
g10-soc-read-situation | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student reads an invented weekend plan that turns uneasy, from their own side and from a friend's, plans whether to stay, change it or leave, and judges the plan by both people's results. This year the reading takes in the second person's view.
g10-rds-think-ahead | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans a choice in an invented case (saying yes to every favor a friend asks), lays out results for self and for the friend over a month, and judges the plan after the case plays out. Grade 10 adds the friend's results to the plan.
g10-rel-stand-up | Unit 4, Saying no, setting limits. The student plans how to stand up for an invented classmate mocked online by a school group: first how to ask the classmate what they want, then the safest option with the I-statement frame; the student rehearses both with the teacher, who plays the classmate and then the school group's message, while a partner watches for the steps, and judges the plan from the classmate's side. New in grade 10: the target's wishes lead the plan. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.
g10-rel-teamwork | Unit 5, Teams that work. The team designs its plan, then each member writes how the split looks from one other member's side; the team adjusts the plan and judges it at the end of the project. Grade 10 tests the team plan against each member's view.
g10-rel-lead-follow | Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans and leads a team session, asks a teammate for feedback on the leading, and later follows that teammate's lead, judging both roles from the other person's side. What changes: leading is judged by the one who followed.
g10-sm-stay-on-task | Unit 5, Teams that work. The student designs a focus plan with a study partner (shared work blocks, a signal for off-task, a check-in), writes what the partner needs from it, and judges after the case week whether it worked for both. A partner's needs now shape the focus plan.
g10-sm-set-goal | Unit 5, Teams that work. The team sets a shared goal on the goal steps card, each member writes what they need for it to work, and the plan is revised after a check-in where needs clash. The second person's needs enter the goal plan this year.
g10-sm-plan-and-tidy | Unit 5, Teams that work. The student builds a team timeline where tasks hand off between members, writes what each handoff asks of the next person, plans buffers, and judges the timeline after an invented delay. Grade 10: planning that other people depend on.
g10-sm-step-forward | Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans how to start a team improvement and invite a quiet member to co-own it, writes how the start looks from that member's side, and judges whether the invitation worked. New this year: starting something with another person.
g10-soc-care-when-struggle | Unit 5, Teams that work. When an invented teammate is struggling and falling behind, the student plans care the teammate would welcome (asked about first), weighs it against the team's deadline, and judges it from the teammate's side. Grade 10 lets the struggling person's view lead the care. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g10-soc-thankful | Unit 5, Teams that work. The student plans thanks for each team member at the end of the project, writes how each might want to be thanked (in front of the team or in private), and judges which thanks landed. This year the receiver's wishes shape the thanks.
g10-sa-i-can-try | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student helps plan for an invented friend who doubts they can try out for a selective program: two steps, a helper, a fallback, and what the friend would find encouraging, then judges the plan from the friend's side. Grade 10 plans belief alongside another person. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan.
g10-sa-skills-grow | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student designs a practice plan from a coach's or teacher's invented feedback, writes how the feedback giver sees the gap, and judges whether the plan answers it. Never belief alone. What grade 10 adds: the plan starts from another person's feedback.
g10-rds-ask-questions | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. Before a class decision on an invented fairness question (who gets first pick of project topics), the student interviews a partner with the other view, plans the questions that test both, and judges whether the plan changed or confirmed their view. Grade 10 puts a second person's questions beside one's own.
g10-rds-check-facts | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student checks two invented students' claims about the fairness of a school rule against a two-way table, writes why each believed their claim, plans a decision on what the data show, and judges it with the look-back step. The other person's reasons for a belief join the data check.
g10-rds-solve-problems | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The class takes a fairness problem in school life it can act on (how lab partners are chosen); the student designs a solution with input from a partner who sees the problem differently, pilots it where allowed and judges it from both views. New in grade 10: a second view shapes the solution.
g10-rds-careful-thinking | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose with a partner who reaches a different choice on an invented case, compares the steps where they split, and judges whose reasoning held up. Grade 10 tests careful thinking against another person's.
g10-rds-good-for-us | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student plans a fair split of an invented household's weekly chores among siblings with different schedules, writes each sibling's view, and judges whether the plan is good for everyone. This year the plan weighs a second person's well-being as fully as one's own.
g10-rds-who-is-affected | Unit 6, Fair to everyone. The student maps who an invented school decision affects, asks a partner playing one affected group what they would add, plans an option fair to both and judges it. What grade 10 adds: an affected person's own voice in the map.

