 succeeded in 113ms:
g11-sa-what-interests-me | Unit 1, What matters to me. Privately the student plans how to give time to an interest when something else must give (a paid job, a sport, study hours), writes the cost of each path, and judges after a set time whether the choice held its purpose. Grade 11 puts a real cost on the interest. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content.
g11-sa-tell-truth | Unit 1, What matters to me. In an invented case where acting on a value costs a spot (reporting a teammate's rule break before a final game), the student plans what to do and say, names the price, and judges the plan with the look-back step. New this year: honesty planned when it costs. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct.
g11-sa-feel-think-link | Unit 1, What matters to me. The student maps how pressure (a deadline, a parent's hope, a friend's push) runs through an invented student's thoughts and feelings to a choice against what they value, plans where to break the loop, and judges the plan. Grade 11 maps the loop under pressure. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud.
g11-sa-who-i-am | Unit 1, What matters to me. The student plans how an invented eleventh grader keeps what matters to them when two groups (a team and a job) each pull hard, names what holding steady costs, and judges the plan after a scene. What grade 11 adds: identity planned under pressure from two sides. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check.
g11-sa-my-strengths | Unit 1, What matters to me. The student plans how an invented character could use a strength where showing it carries a cost (a quiet student leading a debate, a bilingual student always asked to translate for free), weighs the cost, and judges the plan. This year a cost comes with using a strength. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about.
g11-sm-set-goal | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a long-term goal plan for an invented eleventh grader (a skill certificate, a team captaincy) on the goal steps card, plans what the goal costs in time and other wants, meets an invented setback, and revises and judges the plan. Grade 11 tests the plan against a setback and a cost.
g11-sm-plan-and-tidy | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student plans an invented overloaded month (courses, a part-time job, a family duty), decides what to cut, names the cost of each cut, and judges after the case month what the plan protected. New this year: planning when every choice gives something up.
g11-sm-stay-on-task | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a restart plan for an invented student whose focus slumps midway through a long project after a bad grade, names what the restart costs, and judges it from a case log. Grade 11 plans focus after a setback.
g11-sa-i-can-try | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student plans a second try for an invented character who failed a first attempt in public (a driving test, an audition), with two steps, one place to ask for help and a fallback, names the cost of trying again, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: trying again when failing has already cost. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan.
g11-sa-skills-grow | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a practice plan for an invented student who has hit a plateau with little time left before an exam, chooses what to change and what to drop, and judges the plan from the case results. Never belief alone. This year growth is planned under time pressure.
g11-sm-calm-down | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student revises an invented student's coping plan for a high-pressure term (exams, a job, a game season): tools for each setting, sleep and rest kept in, what the plan costs in other time, and a review after the case term. Grade 11 tests the coping plan under pressure that lasts. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g11-sm-big-feelings | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student designs a response plan for a strong feeling after an invented setback (a rejection letter, a lost final), names what reacting would cost, and judges the plan in a second scene. New this year: the feeling comes with a loss.
g11-sa-name-feelings | Unit 2, Plans and setbacks. The student charts an invented student's feelings across a term of rising pressure, names exact words as they shift, plans where to catch early signs, and judges the chart. Grade 11 charts feelings under a long pressure. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
g11-rds-ask-questions | Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student plans an inquiry on an invented choice with pressure to decide fast (a summer program with a deadline tomorrow): questions, sources and what would change their mind, then judges what the time pressure cost the inquiry. Grade 11 keeps questions open under a deadline.
g11-rds-check-facts | Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student checks the claims behind an invented costly choice (two programs with different fees and results) against the numbers, plans a decision, names what the evidence cannot settle, and judges the decision with the look-back step. New this year: evidence weighed when the choice has a price.
g11-rds-think-ahead | Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student lays out short- and long-term costs of options in an invented choice that is hard to undo (dropping a course for more work hours), plans the pick, and judges it against how the case unfolds. Grade 11 adds what a choice costs and whether it can be undone.
g11-rds-careful-thinking | Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose on an invented choice under pressure from a friend and a deadline, then reflects in writing on which step the pressure tried to skip and what skipping it would have cost. This year careful thinking holds against pressure.
g11-sa-snap-judgments | Unit 3, Weighing evidence for choices. The student plans how an invented student could check a snap judgment a whole group shares about a new team member when disagreeing costs standing, and judges whether the plan corrected it. What grade 11 adds: checking a judgment with a social price. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group.
g11-rel-talk-listen | Unit 4, Working with others. The student plans and rehearses an invented talk where saying the plain truth may cost goodwill (telling a team its plan will not meet the deadline), with the check-back step, and judges whether the meaning got across. Grade 11 plans the talk when speaking plainly has a cost.
g11-rel-different-ways | Unit 4, Working with others. In an invented team where habits clash and the deadline is close, the student plans working norms that respect each member, names what each side gives up, and judges the norms after the deadline passes. New this year: respect kept when it costs time. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners.
g11-rel-teamwork | Unit 4, Working with others. The team designs a plan for a project with a hard deadline, meets an invented loss (a member drops out), replans without blame, and judges at the end what the plan protected. Grade 11 tests the team plan under a loss.
g11-rel-settle-disagreements | Unit 4, Working with others. With a partner the student plans and holds a negotiation on an invented team disagreement where a deal costs one member more, agrees a fix with the I-statement frame and judges whether the costs were shared fairly, with a follow-up checking that the uneven cost was made up. What grade 11 adds: a settlement that costs both sides. The teacher plays any upset part.
g11-soc-their-view | Unit 4, Working with others. The student states the view of an invented teammate whose view is unpopular (a junior on a mostly senior team proposing a slower, safer plan), well enough that the teammate would agree, plans how to raise it in the team, and judges the result. This year taking a view comes with a cost.
g11-soc-notice-good | Unit 4, Working with others. The student plans specific credit for an invented junior teammate whose idea replaced the student's own in a group project, when giving it costs some pride, and judges how it landed. Grade 11 gives credit at a cost: the credit goes to the idea that beat the student's.
g11-soc-read-situation | Unit 4, Working with others. The student reads an invented work-group moment where the stakes rise (a group plan to skip a step that keeps a project safe), plans whether to stay, change it or leave, names each cost, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: reading a moment as pressure builds.
g11-rel-ask-offer-help | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans asking for help when it costs (asking a manager to move a shift before finals) and offering help that takes real time, uses the support map for each, rehearses the ask and the offer aloud with a partner playing the coach and then the teammate, and judges the plan. Grade 11 plans help with a cost on both sides. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. A worry about a friend's safety goes to an adult, even when the friend asked for a secret. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g11-rel-friendships | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student weighs an invented friendship where a friend asks for daily help with work the student has no time for, plans a limit with the I-statement frame and its check-back, rehearses the talk with a partner playing the friend, and judges the plan from both sides. New this year: a friendship choice with a price, since a yes costs the student's own time and a no may cost the friend's goodwill. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent).
g11-rel-say-no | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student designs a refusal plan for an invented case where saying no carries a price (being dropped from a group trip for refusing to cover a lie), rehearses it against a card character, and judges which part held. Grade 11 plans a no that costs. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence).
g11-soc-care-when-struggle | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans care for an invented friend whose hard time goes on for weeks, including what the care costs the student and when to bring in an adult, and judges the plan. What grade 11 adds: care that lasts and has limits. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g11-soc-read-feelings | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student reads the feelings of an invented teammate whose replies grow short over a week of messages after being cut from the starting line-up, plans a check-in that respects the teammate even if the group teases the student for it, and judges the plan. This year reading feelings carries a social cost, read over days rather than in one moment. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g11-soc-thankful | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student plans thanks for an invented person whose help cost them (a friend who gave up an evening to help study), choosing words and timing, and judges whether the thanks matched the cost. Grade 11 thanks help that had a price.
g11-soc-why-rules | Unit 5, Help, support and pressure. The student traces how an invented media feed and an invented peer norm press students toward a costly habit (buying the newest gear, staying up for streaks), designs one change to resist the pressure, and judges what it would cost. What grade 11 adds: a system's pressure with a price attached. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names.
g11-sm-step-forward | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans the start an invented senior could make on a peer tutoring slot that costs the senior a free period, recruits others in the plan, and judges after the first invented weeks whether the start held. New this year: starting when it costs one's own time.
g11-sm-have-a-say | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The class plans a proposal to change one school routine that staff time limits (a later library hour), weighs its staffing cost and its costs for different groups, plans answers to objections, carries out a part where the school allows, and judges the outcome. Student voice in class and school life, with no cause picked by the course. Grade 11 weighs the cost of having a say, including the cost to the staff who would make the change work.
g11-rel-lead-follow | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans how to lead an invented group that must cut one member's part to meet a deadline, rehearses the hard talk with a partner playing the member, then follows a leader they disagree with, judging what each cost. What grade 11 adds: leading when the decision costs a member their part.
g11-rel-stand-up | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student designs an upstander plan for an invented school case where the unkind act comes from a teammate the student depends on for a shared grade, with the safest option, the words and support afterward, rehearses the words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the teammate's unkind act, and judges the plan. This year the plan carries a cost: the shared grade and the working tie. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.
g11-soc-fair-rules | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case the courts have ruled on, names what changing the norm cost the people who did it, and sets beside it an invented school norm whose change would cost the student's own group a privilege (seniors' first pick of lunch tables), plans the change, then judges the plan. New in grade 11: the price of a fair change falls on the student's own group. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (us1-jim-crow).
g11-rds-solve-problems | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The class takes a school problem it can act on with a limited budget of time or money; the student designs a fix within the limit, pilots it where allowed and judges it. Grade 11 solves within a cost limit.
g11-rds-good-for-us | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student plans an invented choice where well-being for self and others costs something wanted (a weekend job shift against a promise to a younger sibling), and judges the plan after the case week. What grade 11 adds: well-being with a price.
g11-rds-who-is-affected | Unit 6, Acting on what matters. The student maps who an invented senior trip date affects when it suits most seniors but clashes with a large group's work shifts, plans an option fair to all, and judges it. This year weighing everyone affected costs the popular choice.

