g12-sa-who-i-am | Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student writes a looking-ahead sketch for an invented senior (or one they make up) moving into work, training or college: which parts of who they are to carry, which groups they will leave and join, the trade-offs, and a judgment of the sketch after a scene set in a first job. Grade 12 plans identity for life past school. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check.
g12-sa-my-strengths | Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented senior could name strengths for a job interview or application (one from a home language or culture among them), matches each to what the setting asks, and judges which match was strongest. Grade 12 carries strengths into a setting past school. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about.
g12-sa-what-interests-me | Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. Privately the student plans a next step after school that fits an interest (a course, a trade, a job, a year of service), with its trade-offs, and sets a date to judge whether it still fits. The setting is now life after school. Teacher note: the list stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content.
g12-sa-i-can-try | Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented senior could take on an unfamiliar adult task (applying for a first apartment, starting a training program), with two steps, a place to ask for help and a fallback, and judges the plan after the case. Grade 12 moves the challenge past school. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan.
g12-sa-feel-think-link | Unit 1, Looking back, looking ahead. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student maps how an invented senior's thoughts about leaving school and what the senior cares about shape feelings and choices (a missed application read as "I will never get in"), plans where to break the loop, and judges the plan in a scene set after graduation. The loop now runs through a change past school. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud.
g12-sa-name-feelings | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student charts an invented graduate's feelings through a first month at work or college, names mixed feelings exactly, plans early signs to watch for, and judges the chart. Grade 12 charts feelings in a new setting past school. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
g12-sm-big-feelings | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a response plan for an invented strong-feeling moment at a first job (a sharp correction from a supervisor in front of others), with the early sign, a calm-down step, a response and a repair, and judges it in a rehearsed scene. Grade 12 handles strong feelings at work.
g12-sm-calm-down | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a coping plan for an invented graduate's first term away from school routines (a new schedule, a job, living with others): tools for each setting, rest kept in, a back-up and a review date, and judges it after the case month. The plan now serves life after school, with where to find help after school named in plain words. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress and mental health topics are health's (health rows g9-meh-influences-judge-plan and g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g12-sm-stay-on-task | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a focus plan for an invented graduate with no one checking daily (an online course, a job with loose hours), plans self check-ins and judges the plan from a case log. Grade 12: focus kept with no school to push it. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL.
g12-sm-plan-and-tidy | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a planning system for an invented graduate's month (work shifts, classes, bill dates, appointments) with a way to keep papers and materials in order, names its trade-offs and judges it after an invented change of shift. Grade 12 plans adult life past school.
g12-sa-skills-grow | Unit 2, Steady under change. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a practice plan for a new skill an invented graduate must learn at work or in training, with feedback from a supervisor, and judges it from a case log. Never belief alone. Grade 12 plans growth at work or in training. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL.
g12-rel-lead-follow | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans and leads an invented community event team (a food drive, a youth game day), delegates, follows another leader in a second role, and judges both from team feedback. Grade 12 leads and follows in a community setting.
g12-rel-teamwork | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The team writes a charter for a project that reaches past school (a service project with a community partner): roles, norms and how to raise problems, then carries it out and judges it in a closing review. Grade 12 takes teamwork to a community partner. Also carries AASL.III.D.2 (the group's learning as one's own job), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL.
g12-rel-talk-listen | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans and rehearses a talk in a setting past school (asking a supervisor to change a schedule, a call with a community partner), chooses the channel and words, uses the check-back step and judges whether the meaning got across. Grade 12 talks to adults at work and in the community.
g12-soc-notice-good | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how a team leader in an invented workplace or community group gives specific credit to each member over a project, and judges which credit landed. Grade 12 makes credit part of leading past school.
g12-soc-read-situation | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student reads invented settings past school (a first shift, a volunteer meeting, a job interview) for what each asks and what chances it offers, plans how to act in each, and judges the plan against how the case turns out. Grade 12 reads settings beyond school.
g12-sm-step-forward | Unit 3, Leading well. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans a start for something a community group or workplace needs (a sign-up sheet, a welcome for new volunteers), with first steps, recruits and costs, and judges it. Grade 12 takes initiative in a setting past school.
g12-soc-fair-rules | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student applies the fairness questions to a settled case the courts have ruled on and to an invented community norm (a club whose membership rule keeps newcomers out), compares the unwritten rules of two settings, plans a fair change through the group's own process, and judges the plan. Grade 12 turns the questions on community life. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; current political disputes are not SEL content. The history itself is social studies' (us2-civil-rights-laws).
g12-soc-why-rules | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student traces how an invented community system (a town's sign-up rules for sports fields, a workplace's shift app) shapes what people do, designs one change, and judges who it would help and what it would cost. Grade 12 studies systems in community and work life. Teacher note: invented apps, accounts and posts only, never the student's own; no product names.
g12-rds-good-for-us | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans an invented graduate's week that weighs their own well-being, family and a community commitment, and judges the plan's balance after the case week. Grade 12 balances well-being in adult life.
g12-rds-who-is-affected | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student maps who an invented community decision affects near and far (moving a bus stop) and which rules or groups it touches, plans an option fair to all and judges it against a group the map first missed. Grade 12 weighs a whole community.
g12-sm-have-a-say | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans, for an invented recent graduate, how to have a say in a community or workplace process (a suggestion through a workplace's own channel, a comment to a local service the graduate uses), with the route, evidence and trade-offs, and judges which plan would most likely work. Nothing is carried out in a real public body; real civic action stays in the government course (gov-local-action). No cause picked by the course. Grade 12 uses voice past the school walls through the invented case (Cory, 2026-11-22, Desk q-prog-sel-1).
g12-rel-stand-up | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. As an invented senior mentor who sees a younger student left out in a hurtful way at a school event, the student designs an upstander plan (the safest option, the words, support afterward, when to report), rehearses the words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, then writes how the same steps would carry to a first job, and judges the plan. Grade 12 adds the mentor role and the carry to work. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.
g12-sa-snap-judgments | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented new employee could check a snap judgment about a coworker, with what to ask and what evidence would change it, and judges the plan. Grade 12 checks one's own judgments at work. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group.
g12-rel-different-ways | Unit 4, Communities and systems. Owner year for 9 to 12. In an invented workplace or community team of different cultures, ages and backgrounds, the student plans working norms that respect each member, names what each adapts, and judges them after a scene. Grade 12 works across difference past school. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners.
g12-rds-ask-questions | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. Before an invented choice past school (accepting a job offer, signing a lease), the student plans the questions to ask, who to ask, and what would change the choice, then judges whether the questions found what mattered. Grade 12 asks questions about adult choices.
g12-rds-check-facts | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student compares two invented offers after school (two training programs, two jobs) on the facts and numbers, names what the numbers cannot show, plans a choice and judges it with the look-back step. Grade 12 checks the facts of a choice past school.
g12-rds-solve-problems | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The class takes a problem in the community it can act on (a park cleanup, a book drive), and beside it a problem in an invented graduate's own life; the student designs the fix with partners, carries it out where allowed, and judges it. Grade 12 solves problems in community life.
g12-rds-think-ahead | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student lays out the long-term results of options in an invented choice after school (a loan for a car, working a year before training), plans the pick with its opportunity cost, and judges it against how the case unfolds. Grade 12 thinks ahead about adult choices.
g12-rds-careful-thinking | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student runs the six-step stop-think-choose on an invented adult choice made under pressure from a salesperson or a deadline, and reflects on which step protected the choice. Grade 12 thinks carefully about choices past school.
g12-sa-tell-truth | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans what an invented new employee should do and say when a coworker asks them to cover a mistake, names the cost, and judges the plan with the look-back step. Grade 12 acts on values at work. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct.
g12-rel-say-no | Unit 5, Hard choices. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a refusal plan for an invented pressure past school (a coworker pushing them to clock in for someone who is late, a crew pushing a risky shortcut on a job), rehearses it against a card character, and judges which part held. Grade 12 says no at work. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes and no real classmate is named; substance, sexual health and dating topics are health's (health rows g9-aod-say-no-across, g9-sh-health-and-consent and g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence).
g12-rel-ask-offer-help | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student builds a support map for an invented graduate's first year (a mentor, a campus or workplace office, a community service, family or friends), plans how to ask in each case and what to do if the first helper cannot help, plans an offer of help to a new coworker or roommate, rehearses one ask aloud with a partner playing the mentor or the office, and judges whether the map covers the cases. Grade 12 maps help after school. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g12-rel-friendships | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans how an invented graduate keeps a friendship across a move while building new ones, with the I-statement frame for a talk about changed time, rehearses that talk with a partner playing the old friend, and judges the plan after the case. Grade 12 keeps friendships through life after school. Teacher note: no real friends are named; dating and relationship health topics are health's (health rows g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence and g9-sh-health-and-consent).
g12-rel-settle-disagreements | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. With a partner the student plans and holds an invented disagreement past school (roommates over chores, coworkers over a shift swap), names interests, agrees a fix with the I-statement frame, and judges it at a follow-up. Grade 12 settles disagreements at home and work. The teacher plays any upset part.
g12-soc-their-view | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student states the view of an invented roommate or coworker whose habits and values differ, well enough that the person would agree, plans a talk, and judges it. Grade 12 takes the views of people met after school.
g12-soc-care-when-struggle | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans care for an invented friend struggling after a move away, from a distance (a call, a visit, connecting them with help), with when to bring in someone else, and judges the plan. Grade 12 cares across distance after school. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Crisis topics are health's (health row g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help).
g12-soc-read-feelings | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student reads feelings in an invented workplace or roommate scene with few cues (a short message from a supervisor), plans a check-in, and judges the reading. Grade 12 reads feelings at work and at home.
g12-soc-thankful | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student plans thanks to people who helped an invented senior through school (a coach, a mentor, a neighbor), chooses how and when, and judges the plan. Grade 12 thanks people at a turning point in life.
g12-sm-set-goal | Unit 6, Support after school. Owner year for 9 to 12. The student designs a one-year goal plan for an invented graduate on the goal steps card, with milestones, supports from the support map, trade-offs and a review date, and judges it after an invented setback. Grade 12 sets goals for the first year after school.

