math m8-two-way-tables IM 8.6, lessons 9 to 10; cluster 8.SP.A, supporting. Students build two-way tables of frequencies and relative frequencies for two categorical variables on the same subjects (curfew and chores), compute row or column relative frequencies, and decide whether the two variables seem associated. Introduces the two-way-table. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 200 (printed 194). Teacher note: survey questions avoid personal or sensitive topics. Relative frequency comes from m7-chance-probability (7.SP.C.6); m7-probability-models adds models from observed frequencies.
math alg1-data-displays Represent one numerical variable with dot plots, histograms and box plots, choose the display that fits the question, and describe the distribution by shape (symmetric, skewed left or right, bimodal, uniform). The course opens with class survey data and published data; students sort statistical from non-statistical questions and numerical from categorical data. Growth from grades 6 and 7: the same three displays, now chosen on purpose and read for shape before any number is computed. Numbers: real data sets of about 10 to 100 values, technology for the larger ones. Limits: one variable at a time; shape words only, no density curves (normal distributions wait for grade 11). IM Alg1.1 lessons 1 to 5 (and 6 to 8 for spreadsheet work). Progressions: shape, centre and spread from 6 to 8 deepened (pdf 283 (p 277)). Modeling: every Statistics and Probability standard is a modeling standard (pdf 282 (p 276)), so data work recurs in grades 10 and 11. Skips grade 10 on purpose: Geometry carries no algebra or functions unit, so Algebra II builds on Algebra I directly (IM course order Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II). Assumes m7-compare-populations (grade 7) and m6-data-displays (grade 6) across grade 8 on purpose: grade 8 statistics is bivariate only (scatter plots and two-way tables), so one-variable displays were last taught in grades 6 and 7, and histograms last in grade 6. Dot plots, histograms and box plots return here after grade 8, whose statistics is bivariate only. Re-entry (revisited): rebuild m6-data-displays, m7-compare-populations briefly before using it, opening with a readiness check; fluent recall after 2 grades without the skill is not assumed. Reactivation (box-plot, unused in grade 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work. Reactivation (dot-plot, unused in grade 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work. Reactivation (histogram, unused in grade 7, 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work.
math geo-two-way-probability Construct and interpret two-way frequency tables when two categories describe each object, use the table as a sample space to estimate conditional probabilities and decide whether events are independent, and explain conditional probability and independence in everyday language (the chance of A given B is not the chance of B given A). Applies the Algebra I relative frequency work (S-ID.B.5). Numbers: counts, relative frequencies as decimals and percents. IM Geo.8 lessons 4, 7 and 9. Progressions: S-CP.4 and S-CP.5 (pdf 294 (p 288)). Limits: two categorical variables, probabilities from relative frequencies in the table.
computer-science g11-algorithm-ethics Unplugged day. A rule-based and a data-driven algorithm for the same job (sorting applications, say) are audited for ethical, societal and bias effects with evidence, giving two views and a dated source for each. Students name who is helped, who is harmed and what the audit could not see, and recommend how each algorithm could be refined. Left out: building either algorithm. Paper path: written outputs for the same ten cases printed side by side. Model carried: benefit-risk-chart.
computer-science g12-team-workflow Device day, several weeks. A team builds one program with a design document, a task list that splits the weeks (plan and design, build in pairs, a review step, a final check) and clear task roles. In the review step a teammate reads the code against the design document and a test-table and either signs off or returns it. Each member's part and contribution is recorded in a log. Depth: a working method for a four-person team, no version-control tool required. Paper path: the design document and the log kept on paper with code printed for review. Model carried: text-program, test-table. Specialty I addition (S1-SWD-PP-08): the design document records whose needs and views shaped each decision, and the review step asks whether the program reflects more than the team's own view. Version control (S1-SWD-PP-08): part met until our editor's offline version history with branch, review and merge exists; then the review step uses it (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-2).
computer-science g12-diverse-teams Unplugged day. A diverse team on the project is studied for how different perspectives improved the design, using a log from g12-team-workflow, which runs earlier in the grade. Students pick two moments in the log where a different view changed a decision and say what the team would have lost without it. Depth: evidence from the log, not opinion about people. Paper path: the log read in pairs. Model carried: benefit-risk-chart. Students also read two supplied published accounts by computing professionals from different backgrounds (sources in the teacher line, no company names) and say how each used computational thinking and met a barrier at work and opened the field to others.
computer-science g12-career-goals Unplugged day. Students connect what they learned to a personal goal and write a plan: the goal, two skills they have shown in the course (evidence from their portfolio), one pathway to check, after researching two computing-related fields from supplied field cards (duties only) and one next step. Depth: a plan, no comparison of schools or pay. Shared at the level of ideas, not personal details; kept private by default. Paper plan. No new model is carried.
