english-language-arts-grade-10 [' 2 ', ' **What is life made of? From Huxley to cloning** (weeks 6 to 9). What did scientists claim they could make, and how did each writer explain it to a general reader? Anchors: T. H. Huxley, "On the Physical Basis of Life" (1868, US public domain); two US government explanations of the same cloning process for different readers: the National Human Genome Research Institute\'s Cloning Fact Sheet (a general account, with Dolly the sheep as its case) and the Food and Drug Administration\'s primer on cloning and its use in livestock operations (reproduction, cloning, risks and uses, for farmers and consumers) (US government works, text only, each page\'s images checked). The unit plan checks both pages\' length and order first; if they do not order comparable material, the fallback pair is Louis Pasteur\'s 1864 Sorbonne lecture on spontaneous generation (a public-domain translation) and John Tyndall\'s essays on the floating matter of the air (1881), two scientists explaining whether life can arise from non-life; an original Curio Class essay of 2,000 to 3,000 words on growing tissues and organs in the lab, and what it means to \'make\' living things, written to the grade 10 text spec. ', ' 14 '] e10-w-narrative
english-language-arts-grade-10 [' 3 ', ' **Under colonial rule: stories from India, the Philippines and South Africa** (weeks 10 to 12). What does each work show about how its people live with power held by others, at home and from abroad, and how can a reader tell from the text? Anchors: Rabindranath Tagore, "The Postmaster" (in Mashi and Other Stories, 1918) and "The Cabuliwallah" (in The Hungry Stones and Other Stories, 1916), and Gitanjali 35, "Where the mind is without fear" (1912); José Rizal, Noli Me Tángere, three or four chapters in Charles Derbyshire\'s translation, The Social Cancer (1912), named by the unit plan; Sol Plaatje, Mhudi (the 1930 Lovedale text), chapters 1 to 4 (the opening and the attack on Kunana, about 32 printed pages) with teacher summaries of the rest, and Native Life in South Africa (1916), chapter 1 in excerpt as background for Mhudi. All US public domain. ', ' 15 '] e10-rl-world-literature
