# The Curio Class approach to teaching

This page says how Curio Class teaches, in every subject, and why. It is layer 3 of a course: it is built on
[FOUNDATION.md](FOUNDATION.md) and on the standards, and every scope and sequence and section plan is built on it. Each subject
adds its own page, which says how the subject carries out each principle. Mathematics and English language arts have pages,
[APPROACH-MATH.md](APPROACH-MATH.md) and [APPROACH-ELA.md](APPROACH-ELA.md); for science and social studies,
the subject lens (docs/agents/lenses/) plus its rubric stands in through the scopes and sequences and unit plans, and
a short approach page per subject is written before days (Cory, 2026-11-14). The subjects after the core have pages
in their course folders: [health](courses/health/APPROACH.md),
[physical education](courses/physical-education/APPROACH.md), [arts](courses/arts/APPROACH.md),
[computer science](courses/computer-science/APPROACH.md), [social and emotional learning](courses/sel/APPROACH.md),
[library and research skills](courses/library/APPROACH.md), [English language development](courses/eld/APPROACH.md),
[career education](courses/career-education/APPROACH.md) and
[world languages](courses/world-languages/APPROACH.md) (shelved).

Two honest notes. Nothing in Curio Class has been tried with children yet, so this page makes no claim about
results; it explains choices, and its rules are rules for section plans, not descriptions of lessons that exist.
And every claim about how students learn rests on a source opened and read for this page. The list at the end
says how much of each was read. Where a federal practice guide rates its own evidence (strong, moderate, or
minimal; the oldest guide says low), that rating is given. The detail behind each claim (who was studied, how
many studies, how big the effect) is in [APPROACH-SOURCES.md](APPROACH-SOURCES.md), "Evidence notes".

Second draft, 2026-11-11, revised after a cold read by the curriculum expert
([reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md)). Cory settled three points the same day
(how a lesson opens, no timers in tools, narrowed on 2026-11-14 to timers with a grounded purpose, BASELINE rule 30; a wording change in SPEC.md). The page as a whole still waits for his
read.

Changed 2026-11-12, Cory's yes on card q-ela-piece-2: principle 3 and the modelling line under "Where the
evidence is thin or contested" now give the federal ratings for reading and writing
([reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md), finding F4).

> **What this means in your room, or at your table**
>
> - We find out what your student already knows before teaching something new.
> - New things are shown clearly, step by step, with examples. Then the help is taken away, on purpose.
> - A lesson may open with a short question or task. The teaching always follows the same day.
> - Old work keeps coming back, from memory, so it is not forgotten.
> - Every day leaves you something you can act on: what was understood, and what to do if it was not.
> - Students talk and write about what they learn. You judge that work; we give you what you need to judge it.
> - A student who is behind works on the grade's content with extra support, not on easier content.
> - A screen is used where it helps, and real things are used where the idea lives in real things.
> - If you are teaching one child, every unit has a note for you.

## The role of the standards

The standards decide **what** a student should know and be able to do by the end of a grade, and in what words.
A Curio Class check uses the standard's own verb, so "explain" is not quietly swapped for "pick".

The standards leave the teaching to us. The mathematics standards say so directly: they "do not dictate
curriculum or teaching methods" and do not define the supports for students who are behind, ahead, or learning
English (Common Core State Standards for Mathematics, introduction). Each subject page says the same for its
own standards from its own standards document. So the order of the year, how each idea is taught, the practice,
the checks and the supports are our choices, and this page and the subject pages are where they are explained.

### Which standards: national, not federal (from the Phase 1 scope, 2026-10-22)

There are no federal K-12 content standards. Federal law (ESSA, and earlier the Department of Education Organization Act) bars the federal government from setting curriculum, and each state adopts its own standards. The highest level that exists is the **national consensus framework** for each subject, written by states, teachers or professional associations. Most state standards are adopted from or modeled on these. That is our alignment target everywhere, which keeps us from tying the site to any one state.

Where a subject has no national framework (Precalculus, Statistics, Calculus), the top level is the College Board AP course framework, which every state recognizes.

| Subject | Framework (version) | Code level in our data | Example | Verified against |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Math | Common Core State Standards for Mathematics (2010) | cluster | `3.NF.A`, `A-REI.B.4` | Cluster list per grade and domain from corestandards.org (from memory, spot-checked) |
| ELA | Common Core ELA (2010) | standard | `RL.4.2`, `RF.K.1` | Strand rules: RL/RI/W 1-10, SL/L 1-6, RF only K-5 (RF.1-2 only K-1), no RL.8 |
| Science | NGSS (2013) | performance expectation | `MS-PS1-4` | Every PE code in the official NGSS All-DCI PDF (exact match) |
| Social Studies | C3 Framework (2013, rev. 2017) | Dimension 2 indicator | `D2.His.14.6-8` | Indicator list from the official C3 PDF |
| Arts | National Core Arts Standards (2014) | discipline + anchor | `VA:Cr2`, `MU:Re7` | 11 anchors x 5 disciplines |
| Health | National Health Education Standards (3rd ed., 2024) | standard 1-8 | `NHES.S3` | SHAPE America 2024 release |
| PE | SHAPE America National PE Standards (2024) | standard 1-4 | `SHAPE.S2` | 2024 release (cut from 5 to 4 standards) |
| Computer Science | **2026 CSTA PK-12 CS Standards** (released July 2026) | standard | `E3-PRO-VD-06`, `MS-SYS-NT-34` | All 196 identifiers extracted from the official 2026 PDF |
| World Languages | ACTFL Proficiency Guidelines; World-Readiness Standards | level / goal area | `ACTFL.Novice Mid`, `WRS.Cultures` | Closed list |
| CTE | Advance CTE Modernized Career Clusters (2024, 14 clusters) | framework | `CTE.Career Clusters` | 2024 framework (replaced 16 clusters) |
| Personal Finance | National Standards for Personal Financial Education (2021) | standard area | `NSPFE.Managing Credit` | 6 areas |
| Psychology | APA National Standards for HS Psychology | pillar | `APA.Cognition` | Pillar names |
| SEL | CASEL framework | competency | `CASEL.Self-Management` | 5 competencies |
| English learners | WIDA ELD Standards Framework (2020) | standard + key language use | `WIDA.ELD-4`, `WIDA.Argue` | 5 standards, 4 key language uses |
| Library / research | AASL National School Library Standards (2018) | shared foundation | `AASL.Curate` | 6 foundations |
| Precalc / Stats / Calc | College Board AP course frameworks | unit | `AP-STAT.U5` | Unit numbers |

`scripts/standards.py` holds every registry, and the build fails on any code that is not real. A check proves a code exists and sits in the right grade band; best fit is a judgment for the subject pages and the reads.

## The principles

They follow the order of a unit: plan, teach, practise, check, and then two that run through all of it.

**1. Start from what students know, and teach in an order where each idea rests on the last.**
New ideas are learned by connecting them to what is already in memory, and what a student already believes can
also mislead (How People Learn II, conclusion 5-1; Deans for Impact). These are summaries of a large body of
research, not a tested recipe for ordering a course. *Rule:* a section plan names the earlier standards it builds
on and checks them first; the scope and sequence gives "builds on" and "where it comes back" for every unit.
One progression per subject, all its grades, kept as data with gates: data/progressions/<subject>.csv, one row per
skill, with the columns strand, grade, skill_id, standard, assumes (skill ids from earlier grades), hands_on (skill
ids the next grade assumes), model_in, model_out, words, and cross_subject.

- plan.py checks that a course's readiness items name skill ids an earlier grade owns, that every skill id is owned
  by exactly one grade, and that a carried model (number line, tape diagram, timeline, map scale) is introduced
  before a course leans on it. The gate is scripts/progression.py (inside plan.py check): ids, grades, real codes,
  every standard owned by one grade or excluded, both ends of every handoff, models handed on before use, real
  cross_subject codes, and forward cross-subject links; it notes C3 indicators in two grades, skipped handoffs and
  model gaps. A `~CODE` marks a row that starts or applies a standard another row owns.
- **Across subjects**: the cross_subject column records where one subject leans on another (graphs in grade 6
  science against grade 6 math; reading demands against ELA; timelines and number lines between social studies and
  math). A course may not lean on a skill no subject has taught by that grade. This is why all four are built
  together.
- data/sequence.csv stays as the module order (which topic follows which); the new files sit under it at skill
  level, and sequence.csv's open science errors are fixed in this step. ROADMAP layer 4's home holds the new files.
- Built from the standards and the progressions documents, with each base text's own sequence as evidence (IM K to
  12, OpenSciEd middle school, EL K to 5); the existing grades 3 to 5 math page is folded in.
- **Write blind, then reconcile** (settled 2026-11-13). Writers (docs/pipeline/briefs/progression-write.md) and the
  first read never see the subject's earlier planning. After stitching, one reconcile step
  (docs/pipeline/briefs/progression-reconcile.md) lists every difference with the old work and settles each by a
  named source, neither side winning by default, and measures the pull (how often the old work won without a
  source). Old work that loses becomes rework for its course's turn. Only the big things go to the Desk: a
  difference no source settles that moves a standard or changes what a grade teaches, or a pull that shows the old
  work steering. Order per subject: band writers, stitch, reconcile, expert and Codex reads (Codex by script,
  scripts/codex_read.py), fix.
- **Review**: the expert reads each subject cold, then one read across all four for the cross-subject column.
  Anything that changes what a grade teaches comes to Cory.

Done 2026-11-13 for all four: social studies 384 rows K to 12 (with data/progressions/social-studies-excluded.csv,
10 codes, and the band drafts in data/progressions/parts/), science 198 rows K to 11 (all 208 NGSS performance
expectations owned; grade 12 has no core course), ELA 374 rows K to 12 (all 455 grade-level codes owned once), math
305 rows K to 11 (53 (+) codes deferred to the electives). No reconcile found the old work steering (science 25
differences, old work won 19, 17 of them by a named source; ELA 18, old won 8, 5 sourced; math 43, old won 28, all
sourced). The cross-subject read's reports end with a "For the
course frames" list that step 2 takes as input (a shared calendar for same-year links, bridges budgeted as days,
the grade 12 timetable).

### Step 2. Course frames for the four core subjects, then a review: baseline 1

A course frame says what the course is: its grades, the standards and progression skills it owns, its days, its
unit list with a reason for each unit, and, where there is a base text, the source map (a section of the frame,
every base lesson placed in one of our units). The day-level data/plans/<course>/source-map.csv comes with the
scope and sequence, scripted from the source's free pages, then judged for unit edges and day counts; grade 4
math's table of 149 IM lessons is already scripted and kept outside the repo in the source store. A course frame is
the course-level summary; its scope and sequence (step 3) then sets every day in order. The 52 core courses of the
four subjects get one each (the 6 electives in these subjects wait, see After the core subjects). Scopes and
sequences already on file are read as input against the progressions, not assumed right.

Then **baseline 1**, a whole-structure review:
- every frame against its progression rows, and the frames of each subject read side by side through the grades;
- where the layers, the gates or the vocabulary need alignment or fixing;
- **the website design check**: whether the site's structure (catalog pages, topics, course and unit pages) still
  fits the hierarchy, and what changes if not. Proposals go to Cory; nothing is rebuilt during planning unless he
  says;
- a Codex read of a sample.

### Step 3. Social studies: scope and sequence for every course, then a review

Each social studies course's frame becomes its scope and sequence (every course day in order, by unit), written to
docs/pipeline/briefs/sequence-write.md: `docs/courses/<course>/scope-and-sequence.md` with one row per day in
`data/plans/<course>/days.csv` (the five day kinds in every subject; course-level check and respond days take
`stretch` = `C`). It places its days on the one school calendar, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md (school days, weeks, the
stagger rule for course-level checks across a grade), settles each "Candidate:" check-set cell and its day cost, and
reserves assessment slots without writing items or section plans. The gate is `scripts/sequence.py` (run by
`plan.py check`). The expert reads each to docs/pipeline/briefs/sequence-read.md against the progression and the
content standard P1 set, Codex reads each too, and one fix pass applies both reads. Cory's call is at the baseline,
not a yes per course.

### Step 4. Social studies: units, then a review, then the probe: baseline 2

- Every social studies unit is planned, with its cross-day files (hand-backs, materials, reserved examples, and the
  source map where a base text is used). The expert reads each.
- **The probe**: a few social studies days (one section, 3 to 5 days) are written from the unit plans and read the
  full way (writer, cheap read, expert read, fix, re-read). Measured against P4: the share of day findings traced
  back to the unit plan or above, how many unit plans need changing, and whether the unit template itself needs a
  change before three more subjects copy it.
- **Baseline 2**: a full review of social studies from the progression down, plus the probe's result. Template
  changes are made here, once, before step 5.

### Step 5. The other three subjects together: the same cycle

Step 3 runs for science, ELA and math together (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS): Codex writes the 37 scopes
and sequences (scripts/codex_scopes.py). Step 4 (units, review, probe) then runs for science, ELA and math together,
after social studies' probe and baseline 2 (settled 2026-11-15, see DESK-ANSWERS), with a review from the progression
down in each subject. The probe runs in every subject: a few days each is cheap, and each subject's units can be off
in their own way.

Science 6 to 8 follows OpenSciEd's order after its loss check. ELA K to 5 and middle school science test the base
text paths that INTEGRATION-PLAN's two small trials were meant to test, so those trials fold into this step.
# SEL progression: expert cold read, 2026-11-21

Reader: the curriculum expert (docs/agents/EXPERT.md), run cold on data/progressions/sel.csv. Nothing was edited except
this report. Brief: docs/pipeline/briefs/progression-read.md, with {subject} = sel.

## What I read

- docs/agents/EXPERT.md; the lens docs/agents/lenses/sel.md; the pass rule, the shared core and part 9 (Social and
  emotional learning) of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md; docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md in full.
- data/progressions/sel.csv: 520 rows, 40 per grade K to 12, 160 owner rows (grades 2, 5, 8, 12) and 360 `~` rows.
  data/progressions/sel-edges.md (130 cross_subject edges; they match the csv one for one).
- data/standards/casel-5.txt (40 sub-skills, our words).
- For leans only: the target rows in data/progressions/health.csv, ela.csv, math.csv, social-studies.csv,
  physical-education.csv and computer-science.csv (ids, grades, standards, and the notes of the health rows named).
  data/progressions/library-excluded.csv and docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4 for the three AASL ids placed in SEL.
- Gate: `python -X utf8 scripts/progression.py check sel`: 520 rows, 0 failures. `stale.py check` on the four SEL
  pages: 0 failures. plan.py not run, as told. No reconcile report exists for SEL.
- My own checks, run in the scratchpad over the csv: each row's unit against STANDARD section 3's grade table (all 520
  match); model first use by grade and by unit; every cross_subject id and every health id named in a note against its
  file and grade; a marker search for each `~` row's year line; rehearsal words in the self-management and
  relationship rows; text similarity between rows of one sub-skill; K to 2 step counts; multi-week tasks; a word search
  for sensitive ground.
- For REPEAT I compared finding kinds with the headings of the health, physical education and computer science
  progression reads of 2026-11-19 and the core reads of 2026-11-13.

## Verdict

The progression is sound in its frame: every grade teaches all 40 sub-skills in the units STANDARD section 3 places
them in, the seven models start in K or grade 1 and run to grade 12 with no gap and no early use, all 130 leans and all
13 health rows named in teacher notes exist at or before their grade, and the within-band step (school day, second
person, cost, past school) is real in almost every row of grades 1, 3, 6, 7, 9, 10, 11 and 12. The biggest risk is bar
item 5: from grade 3 on, 24 of the 30 rows for friendships, asking and offering help, and standing up for others ask
students to choose, plan or write, and name no rehearsal, so a writer building days from the rows would build the
"discussion with no practice" sessions the bar fails. The one change that matters most is a rehearsal clause on those
rows; after it, the year lines missing from kindergarten and grade 4 and the grade 11 rows that reuse grade 8's cases.
Twelve findings; none needs a gate change, and only one goes to Cory.

## Findings, most serious first

### 1. Relationship rows stop rehearsing after grade 2 (serious; firm. LAYER progression. REPEAT new)

Bar part 9 item 5 and its "Fails if" (posters and discussion with no practice), the lens ("Self-management or
relationship skills met only by discussion, with no rehearsal") and STANDARD section 3 ("Role play of refusal and
standing up... the teacher or a card plays the pressure") all expect relationship skills to be rehearsed. Refusal
(RS6) and settling disagreements (RS5) are rehearsed in every grade. Three relationship sub-skills are not:

- RS9 stand up for someone: K to 2 rehearse; **g3, g4, g5, g6, g7, g8, g9, g10, g11, g12** all pick, choose or plan an
  option and none says the words are rehearsed.
- RS2 friendships: g3, g9 and g10 rehearse; **g4, g5, g6, g7, g8, g11, g12** weigh, write or plan only.
- RS8 ask and offer help: g3, g5 and g10 rehearse; **g4, g6, g7, g8, g9, g11, g12** size, plan or map only.
- Also SM1 strong feelings: **g5-sm-big-feelings** and **g6-sm-big-feelings** list and pick a response with no body
  routine or rehearsed response.

Cost: the section plan writer fills "practice by doing" from the row (bar, "Section plan fills: the practice by
doing"). A row that says "chooses the safest option, writing why" becomes a worksheet day. A reviewer marks the bar's
fail condition on every sampled unit that holds RS9.

Fix (row edits; the wording is item-level, I settle it): append one clause to each listed row, before its year line.
- RS9 rows: "The student then rehearses the chosen words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind
  act; no student plays the one who is unkind or the one treated unfairly."
- RS2 rows: "The student rehearses the opening of the talk with a partner, using the I-statement frame."
- RS8 rows: "The student rehearses the ask (or the offer) aloud with a partner playing the helper (or the friend)."
- g5 and g6 sm-big-feelings: "then rehearses the calm-down steps and the chosen response in a short scene."
In grades 9 to 12 the rehearsal sits between the plan and the judgement: plan, rehearse, judge.

### 2. Kindergarten and grade 4 `~` rows lack their year line (moderate; firm. LAYER progression. REPEAT new)

STANDARD section 3 (Ownership): a `~` row "has... a one-line note on how that year differs". The line is present and
varied, not stock, in grades 1, 3, 6, 7, 9, 10 and 11 (for example "Grade 7 makes the other person the judge of
whether they were heard", "New in grade 11: the price of changing a norm"). It is missing from:

- **K: 37 of 40 rows** (all but k-rel-talk-listen, k-rel-ask-offer-help and k-sm-have-a-say, which say "First year: ...").
- **Grade 4: 33 of 40 rows**: g4-sa-tell-truth, g4-soc-read-situation, g4-rds-careful-thinking, g4-sa-i-can-try,
  g4-sm-stay-on-task, g4-sm-plan-and-tidy, g4-sm-step-forward, g4-sa-my-strengths, g4-soc-notice-good,
  g4-sa-snap-judgments, g4-rel-different-ways, g4-sa-name-feelings, g4-sa-feel-think-link, g4-sm-big-feelings,
  g4-sm-calm-down, g4-soc-care-when-struggle, g4-soc-read-feelings, g4-soc-thankful, g4-soc-why-rules,
  g4-rel-friendships, g4-rel-settle-disagreements, g4-rel-ask-offer-help, g4-rel-lead-follow, g4-rel-say-no,
  g4-rel-stand-up, g4-sm-have-a-say, g4-rds-good-for-us, g4-rds-ask-questions, g4-rds-check-facts,
  g4-rds-solve-problems, g4-rds-think-ahead, g4-rds-who-is-affected, g4-sa-skills-grow.
- Grade 1: g1-rel-different-ways and g1-rds-careful-thinking.

The grade 4 cases do carry the second person (the step is real); the line naming it is what is missing. Cost: the
frame writer for grade 4 cannot tell from the row alone what makes the grade 4 day differ from grade 3's and 5's, which
is the lens's first error ("a grade year that repeats another year of its band").

Fix: append one line per row. Kindergarten: "First year: <what the child does first>", for example k-sm-calm-down
"First year: two body steps, done with the teacher." Grade 4: "Grade 4 adds <the second person>", for example
g4-sm-calm-down "Grade 4 adds a partner: the student coaches someone else's plan." g4-rel-say-no "Grade 4 adds the
group: the no is held while the friendship is kept."

### 3. Grade 11 reuses grade 8's cases (moderate; firm. LAYER progression. REPEAT new)

In 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 the third year adds a cost, so grade 8 (owner) and grade 11 both carry "cost". The depth differs
(grade 11 plans and judges), but nine grade 11 rows take grade 8's case almost word for word:

| Sub-skill | Grade 8 case | Grade 11 case | Fix: a new grade 11 case |
|---|---|---|---|
| g11-soc-read-feelings | cues behind jokes, "may cost a laugh" | the same, in a group chat | a teammate's short replies after being cut from a starting line-up, read over a week of messages |
| g11-soc-notice-good | rival team, "costs a little pride" | rival team at a shared event, "a little pride" | credit to a junior teammate whose idea replaced the student's own |
| g11-rds-who-is-affected | popular choice that costs another group | the same | a prom or trip date that suits most seniors but clashes with a large group's work shifts |
| g11-sm-have-a-say | school improvement, costs for groups, objections | the same, plus judging | a proposal to change one school routine that staff time limits (a later library hour), with a staffing cost |
| g11-rel-lead-follow | lead an unpopular decision, follow a leader you disagree with | the same | lead a group that must cut a member's part to meet a deadline |
| g11-soc-fair-rules | settled case plus a school norm someone pays to change | the same | keep the settled case; set beside it an invented school norm whose change would cost the student's own group a privilege |
| g11-sm-step-forward | school improvement that "may cost time and draw teasing" | the same phrase | start a peer tutoring slot that costs the student a free period |
| g11-rel-stand-up | speaking up "costs standing with friends" | the same | speaking up when the unkind act comes from a teammate the student depends on for a shared grade |
# Social and emotional learning pre-work: cold read, 2026-11-19

Read as the curriculum expert (docs/agents/EXPERT.md) with the SEL lens (docs/agents/lenses/sel.md). I did not write these
drafts. Nothing was edited but this file. Confidence is Firm, Likely or Judgment call on each finding. Anything that rests
on my memory is marked "from memory". No outside source was opened (none was asked for), so nothing here rests on the
framework page or the criteria paper themselves.

## What I read

AGENTS.md; docs/agents/EXPERT.md; docs/agents/lenses/sel.md; docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md (pass rule, C1 to C16, part 9,
the SEL row of "Before a scope and sequence", the sources table); docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md;
data/standards/casel-5.txt; docs/APPROACH.md; docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md; docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md (finished page,
for comparison); docs/reviews/prework-computer-science-read-2026-11-19.md (format only). For stale-note and ownership
checks only: scripts/standards.py lines 90 to 140; scripts/progression.py lines 51 to 112 (STD_FILES, code_grades);
docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md lines 118 to 130 (layer 4 rule); data/courses.csv line 41 and data/subjects.csv line 11;
docs/COURSES-STATUS.md SEL rows; one-line id existence checks in data/progressions/ela.csv and social-studies.csv for the
rows STANDARD.md section 7 names.

## Verdict

The standard is sound as a list: all 40 ids are real, unique, in our own words, and the counts match everywhere, and every
one of the 13 grade years teaches each of the 40 exactly once as a unit's own. The biggest risk is that it is not yet
usable for a progression: layer 4 requires every standard to be owned by exactly one grade, the draft gives every
sub-skill thirteen owners and no per-grade depth, and scripts/progression.py has no SEL support at all. The one change that
matters most is a settled ownership and depth rule (finding 1 and 2), plus a full eleven-field block in APPROACH.md
(finding 4), which today fills about half the slots and sends two fields to the wrong place.

## What I counted (so nobody has to again)

